export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","name":"BimCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"bimcraft","title":"What's new in 0.4","summary":"Our own IFC engine, delete and paste any element, hide and isolate, a model tree, several models at once, clash check, find, measure, walk, BCF issues, quantities, compare versions, a 4D schedule, and touch screens.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["whats new","what's new","release notes","changelog","changes","0.4","version","new features","ifc engine","model tree","hide","isolate","clash","bcf","measure","walk","compare","quantities","4d","touch","language","spanish"],"headings":[{"id":"new","text":"New","level":2},{"id":"improved","text":"Improved","level":2},{"id":"fixed","text":"Fixed","level":2},{"id":"not-in-this-version-yet","text":"Not in this version yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThis is what changed in BimCraft 0.4, compared with 0.3, the version Try ran before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new\"\u003ENew\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste any element.\u003C/strong\u003E Pick walls, doors, furniture or anything else and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) picks every element shown. A stair takes its flights and a wall its openings, and deleting a door closes the wall. Each is one step to undo. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-elements\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-elements\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste building elements\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHide, isolate and see through.\u003C/strong\u003E Pick several elements with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click, then use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIsolate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESee-through\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel tree.\u003C/strong\u003E The left panel shows project, site, building, storeys and spaces, with the elements on each grouped by kind. Untick a row to hide it, click to pick, double-click to zoom. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-tree\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-tree\"\u003EFind your way with the model tree\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESeveral models together.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModels\u003C/span\u003E opens more IFC files into the same scene, in the first model&#039;s coordinates. Hide, move or remove each one. You edit and save one model at a time. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/several-models\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/several-models\"\u003ESee several IFC models together\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColor by property.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E tab paints every element by one property, such as class, storey, material or any value in the file, with a legend. Click a value to show only those elements. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/color-by-property\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/color-by-property\"\u003EColor elements by a property\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFind.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel picks every element that matches your rules, by class, name, type, storey, material or any property. Save the rules as named search sets. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClash check.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClash check\u003C/span\u003E finds where two sets of elements run into each other, in one model or across several, with a tolerance. Click a clash to see it, or make an issue of it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/clash-check\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/clash-check\"\u003ECheck for clashes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIssues and BCF.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssues\u003C/span\u003E tab saves the current view as an issue: camera, picked and hidden elements, and the section. Save and open BCF 2.1 files to share issues with other coordination tools. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/issues-bcf\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/issues-bcf\"\u003EIssues and BCF files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeasure in 3D.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): click two points for a distance, with corner and edge snaps, or click a face for its area. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances and face areas in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWalk mode.\u003C/strong\u003E Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWalk\u003C/span\u003E, then a floor, and walk through the model at eye height with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Walls stop you. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/walk\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/walk\"\u003EWalk through a model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESection box and filled cuts.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox\u003C/span\u003E in the section bar keeps only what is inside a box. Cut walls and slabs are now filled, like a drawn section; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill\u003C/span\u003E turns that off. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel checks.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel checks\u003C/span\u003E lists elements missing a property you require, GlobalIds used twice, elements on no storey, and copies stacked on each other. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-checks\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-checks\"\u003ECheck a model for common problems\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EQuantity totals.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity totals\u003C/span\u003E counts elements and adds up lengths, areas and volumes, grouped by class, storey or any property. It can also list one row per element, or every space with its area and volume. Each downloads as a CSV. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/quantity-totals\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/quantity-totals\"\u003EAdd up quantities, list elements and schedule spaces\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECompare versions.\u003C/strong\u003E With two versions of a model open, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare versions\u003C/span\u003E shows what was added, removed, changed or moved, matched by GlobalId, with a CSV of every change. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/compare-versions\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/compare-versions\"\u003ECompare two versions of a model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E4D schedule.\u003C/strong\u003E Open a task list as a CSV, or start from a template, and drag through the dates to see what is built, in progress and not started. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/4d-schedule\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/4d-schedule\"\u003EPlay a construction schedule on the model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpen in DetailCraft.\u003C/strong\u003E An IFC model goes to the detailing app in a new tab, unsaved changes included. Nothing is uploaded. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-in-another-app\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-in-another-app\"\u003EOpen a file in another app\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPick objects in a drawing&#039;s 3D view.\u003C/strong\u003E Click a line, face or block in a DWG or DXF in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows its data. A drawing&#039;s 3D view also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), and shows text, attributes and wide polylines. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\"\u003ESee a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETouch screens.\u003C/strong\u003E Bigger buttons, tap to pick, pinch to zoom, long press to add to the pick, a walk pad, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn 90°\u003C/span\u003E while placing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/touch-screens\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/touch-screens\"\u003EUse BimCraft on a touch screen or tablet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERecover your work.\u003C/strong\u003E If the tab or browser closes before you save an edited model, BimCraft offers it back next time. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover unsaved work\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFind anything.\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top, to run a tool, jump to a setting or open a help topic. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/find-tools\" data-topic=\"basics/find-tools\"\u003EFind tools, settings and help\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERecent files.\u003C/strong\u003E The start screen lists the files you opened lately (Chrome and Edge). Click one to open it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELanguage.\u003C/strong\u003E Every word on screen now comes from a language file. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanguage\u003C/span\u003E offers Español (México). It is a machine-made draft that has not been reviewed yet, and help topics are still in English.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"improved\"\u003EImproved\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBimCraft&#039;s own IFC engine.\u003C/strong\u003E IFC files are read by an engine that is part of the app. Big files are read without freezing the page. Curved surfaces are drawn as curves, IFC4X3 road and rail alignments are drawn, and exports from Archicad open with their zones, layered walls, stairs and property sets. The file panel lists anything the engine could not draw yet under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/file-formats\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/file-formats\"\u003EWhat BimCraft reads from each format\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESmaller offline copy.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E keeps about half as much as in 0.3. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/online-offline\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/online-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESwapping changes the IFC class.\u003C/strong\u003E Swap a table for a sink and the element becomes a sanitary terminal, keeping its GlobalId, place and item number. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/swap-and-update-library-items\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/swap-and-update-library-items\"\u003ESwap a placed item, and update items from the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMore panels windows.\u003C/strong\u003E Give each panel its own window, up to four, and drag panels between windows. A floating panel resizes by touch or pen too. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EArrange the panels and use a second monitor\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrawings read in the background.\u003C/strong\u003E A DWG is read without holding up the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeshes in 2D.\u003C/strong\u003E Polyface and polygon meshes now draw in a drawing&#039;s 2D view. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/view-dwg\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/view-dwg\"\u003EView a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject files from Archicad.\u003C/strong\u003E Opening a \u003Ccode\u003E.pln\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pla\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tpl\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.bpn\u003C/code\u003E file says what it is and what to export instead. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pln-project-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pln-project-files\"\u003EOpen .pln and .pla project files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fixed\"\u003EFixed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E button showed nothing, because spaces were never loaded. It shows a model&#039;s rooms now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EColumns placed on a grid were drawn at the origin. They stand where the file puts them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening a binary DXF gave a general error. It now says what the file is and how to save one BimCraft reads.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E said &quot;not built yet&quot;. They work.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECounts read properly: &quot;1 file&quot;, not &quot;1 files&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-in-this-version-yet\"\u003ENot in this version yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESettings has an \u003Cstrong\u003EAssistants\u003C/strong\u003E switch, but assistants connect through CadCraft Studio, which is not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving and desktop builds are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"This is what changed in BimCraft 0.4, compared with 0.3, the version Try ran before. New Delete, cut, copy and paste any element. Pick walls, doors, furniture or anything else and press Delete, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+C or Ctrl+V. Select all (Ctrl+A) picks every element shown. A stair takes its flights and a wall its openings, and deleting a door closes the wall. Each is one step to undo. See Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements. Hide, isolate and see through. Pick several elements with Ctrl+click or Shift+click, then use the Show menu: Hide (H), Isolate (I), See-through (T) and Show all (Shift+H). See Hide, isolate and see through elements. Model tree. The left panel shows project, site, building, storeys and spaces, with the elements on each grouped by kind. Untick a row to hide it, click to pick, double-click to zoom. See Find your way with the model tree. Several models together. Add a model… under Models opens more IFC files into the same scene, in the first model's coordinates. Hide, move or remove each one. You edit and save one model at a time. See See several IFC models together. Color by property. The Color tab paints every element by one property, such as class, storey, material or any value in the file, with a legend. Click a value to show only those elements. See Color elements by a property. Find. Find in the left panel picks every element that matches your rules, by class, name, type, storey, material or any property. Save the rules as named search sets. See Find elements by class, name or property. Clash check. Clash check finds where two sets of elements run into each other, in one model or across several, with a tolerance. Click a clash to see it, or make an issue of it. See Check for clashes. Issues and BCF. The Issues tab saves the current view as an issue: camera, picked and hidden elements, and the section. Save and open BCF 2.1 files to share issues with other coordination tools. See Issues and BCF files. Measure in 3D. Measure (M): click two points for a distance, with corner and edge snaps, or click a face for its area. See Measure distances and face areas in 3D. Walk mode. Click Walk, then a floor, and walk through the model at eye height with W A S D. Walls stop you. See Walk through a model. Section box and filled cuts. Box in the section bar keeps only what is inside a box. Cut walls and slabs are now filled, like a drawn section; Fill turns that off. See Cut an IFC model with a section plane. Model checks. Model checks lists elements missing a property you require, GlobalIds used twice, elements on no storey, and copies stacked on each other. See Check a model for common problems. Quantity totals. Quantity totals counts elements and adds up lengths, areas and volumes, grouped by class, storey or any property. It can also list one row per element, or every space with its area and volume. Each downloads as a CSV. See Add up quantities, list elements and schedule spaces. Compare versions. With two versions of a model open, Compare versions shows what was added, removed, changed or moved, matched by GlobalId, with a CSV of every change. See Compare two versions of a model. 4D schedule. Open a task list as a CSV, or start from a template, and drag through the dates to see what is built, in progress and not started. See Play a construction schedule on the model. Open in DetailCraft. An IFC model goes to the detailing app in a new tab, unsaved changes included. Nothing is uploaded. See Open a file in another app. Pick objects in a drawing's 3D view. Click a line, face or block in a DWG or DXF in 3D and Properties shows its data. A drawing's 3D view also has Section (X), and shows text, attributes and wide polylines. See See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D. Touch screens. Bigger buttons, tap to pick, pinch to zoom, long press to add to the pick, a walk pad, and Turn 90° while placing. See Use BimCraft on a touch screen or tablet. Recover your work. If the tab or browser closes before you save an edited model, BimCraft offers it back next time. See Recover unsaved work. Find anything. Press Ctrl+K, or click Find in the bar at the top, to run a tool, jump to a setting or open a help topic. See Find tools, settings and help. Recent files. The start screen lists the files you opened lately (Chrome and Edge). Click one to open it again. Language. Every word on screen now comes from a language file. Settings, General, Language offers Español (México). It is a machine-made draft that has not been reviewed yet, and help topics are still in English. Improved BimCraft's own IFC engine. IFC files are read by an engine that is part of the app. Big files are read without freezing the page. Curved surfaces are drawn as curves, IFC4X3 road and rail alignments are drawn, and exports from Archicad open with their zones, layered walls, stairs and property sets. The file panel lists anything the engine could not draw yet under Not drawn yet. See What BimCraft reads from each format. Smaller offline copy. Save for offline keeps about half as much as in 0.3. See Online and offline. Swapping changes the IFC class. Swap a table for a sink and the element becomes a sanitary terminal, keeping its GlobalId, place and item number. See Swap a placed item, and update items from the library. More panels windows. Give each panel its own window, up to four, and drag panels between windows. A floating panel resizes by touch or pen too. See Arrange the panels and use a second monitor. Drawings read in the background. A DWG is read without holding up the page. Meshes in 2D. Polyface and polygon meshes now draw in a drawing's 2D view. See View a DWG or DXF drawing. Project files from Archicad. Opening a .pln, .pla, .tpl or .bpn file says what it is and what to export instead. See Open .pln and .pla project files. Fixed The Spaces button showed nothing, because spaces were never loaded. It shows a model's rooms now. Columns placed on a grid were drawn at the origin. They stand where the file puts them. Opening a binary DXF gave a general error. It now says what the file is and how to save one BimCraft reads. Cut, Delete and Select all said \"not built yet\". They work. Counts read properly: \"1 file\", not \"1 files\". Not in this version yet Settings has an Assistants switch, but assistants connect through CadCraft Studio, which is not available yet. Cloud saving and desktop builds are not available yet. What is not built yet"},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Getting started with BimCraft","summary":"What BimCraft does today, how to open your first model, look at it in 3D, change one value and save a copy.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["start","first run","open","ifc","rvt","dwg","quick start","introduction","viewer","bim"],"headings":[{"id":"open-bimcraft","text":"Open BimCraft","level":2},{"id":"open-your-first-model","text":"Open your first model","level":2},{"id":"look-around","text":"Look around","level":2},{"id":"do-one-real-thing-fix-a-tag","text":"Do one real thing: fix a tag","level":2},{"id":"save-a-copy","text":"Save a copy","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft is the building-model app of the CadCraft family. In this build it \u003Cstrong\u003Eopens, reads and inspects\u003C/strong\u003E building files, and it \u003Cstrong\u003Eedits the data in IFC models\u003C/strong\u003E. It does not draw or model new elements yet. Think of it as a model viewer and checker that also lets you correct names, tags and property values in an IFC file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat it opens:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC (\u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe full model in 3D: orbit, storeys, element properties, and editing of names and property values\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject and family files (\u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA file card: the release it was saved in, project information, the preview picture and linked files. No 3D.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing in 2D: model space and every sheet, layers, linked drawings, print view, and an equipment schedule for tagged foodservice drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA project folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA report of every model in the folder: backups, links, damaged copies, and a suggestion of which file to start with\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything happens in your browser, on your computer. \u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/files\"\u003EFiles BimCraft opens and saves\u003C/a\u003E for the details.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-bimcraft\"\u003EOpen BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen BimCraft in a current browser. Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari all work; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/platforms\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/platforms\"\u003EBrowsers, devices and memory\u003C/a\u003E lists the differences.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for the empty screen that says &quot;Open a building model.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EToday BimCraft runs in the browser only. Desktop builds are not available yet, and neither is cloud saving: you open files from your computer and save copies back to it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-your-first-model\"\u003EOpen your first model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe quickest way to see what BimCraft does is an IFC file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E in the middle of the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick an \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file in \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E and confirm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status line at the bottom of the screen says &quot;Starting the IFC engine…&quot;, then &quot;Building geometry&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see the model in 3D in the middle, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E list on the left, and an empty \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right. The status line ends with &quot;Opened&quot; and the number of elements.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also drag a file from \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E straight onto the page. The screen shows &quot;Drop to open&quot; while you drag.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe IFC engine loads from the site the first time you open an IFC model, unless BimCraft was saved for offline use. DWG and DXF drawings, project files and folders need nothing extra: their readers are part of the app. If the app bar&#039;s button says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, everything opens offline. If not, IFC models will not open until you reconnect unless one already opened in this tab. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/online-offline\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/online-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:online\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOnline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGoing somewhere without a connection? Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar and wait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E. The saved copy (about 11 MB) includes the IFC engine. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-around\"\u003ELook around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with the left mouse button to orbit around the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll the wheel to zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with the right mouse button to pan. On a Mac trackpad, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to see the whole model again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag with one finger to orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPinch with two fingers to zoom, and move two fingers together to pan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to see the whole model again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"do-one-real-thing-fix-a-tag\"\u003EDo one real thing: fix a tag\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a wall, door or any element in the model. It turns blue, and its properties appear on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, click the value next to \u003Cstrong\u003ETag\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new tag and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E section that opened on the left: your change is listed with the old and new value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-a-copy\"\u003ESave a copy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E section, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the new file in your downloads folder. It is named after the original with &quot; (edited)&quot; added, for example \u003Ccode\u003EClinic (edited).ifc\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour original file is never changed. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\"\u003EChange IFC properties and save an edited copy\u003C/a\u003E explains editing in full.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-to-go-next\"\u003EWhere to go next\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe BimCraft screen\u003C/a\u003E: every panel by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other BIM and drawing programs\u003C/a\u003E: what feels different if you are used to Revit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E: point BimCraft at a project folder and let it sort the files out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/view-dwg\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/view-dwg\"\u003EView a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E: sheets, layers and print view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"BimCraft is the building-model app of the CadCraft family. In this build it opens, reads and inspects building files, and it edits the data in IFC models. It does not draw or model new elements yet. Think of it as a model viewer and checker that also lets you correct names, tags and property values in an IFC file. What it opens: File What you get IFC (.ifc) The full model in 3D: orbit, storeys, element properties, and editing of names and property values Project and family files (.rvt, .rfa, .rte, .rft) A file card: the release it was saved in, project information, the preview picture and linked files. No 3D. DWG and DXF The drawing in 2D: model space and every sheet, layers, linked drawings, print view, and an equipment schedule for tagged foodservice drawings A project folder A report of every model in the folder: backups, links, damaged copies, and a suggestion of which file to start with Everything happens in your browser, on your computer. Nothing is uploaded. See Files BimCraft opens and saves for the details. Open BimCraft Open BimCraft in a current browser. Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari all work; Browsers, devices and memory lists the differences. Wait for the empty screen that says \"Open a building model.\" Today BimCraft runs in the browser only. Desktop builds are not available yet, and neither is cloud saving: you open files from your computer and save copies back to it. Open your first model The quickest way to see what BimCraft does is an IFC file. Click Choose a file in the middle of the screen. Pick an .ifc file in Finder / File Explorer / your file manager and confirm. Wait while the status line at the bottom of the screen says \"Starting the IFC engine…\", then \"Building geometry\". You should see the model in 3D in the middle, a File panel and a Storeys list on the left, and an empty Properties panel on the right. The status line ends with \"Opened\" and the number of elements. You can also drag a file from Finder / File Explorer / your file manager straight onto the page. The screen shows \"Drop to open\" while you drag. The IFC engine loads from the site the first time you open an IFC model, unless BimCraft was saved for offline use. DWG and DXF drawings, project files and folders need nothing extra: their readers are part of the app. If the app bar's button says Offline ready, everything opens offline. If not, IFC models will not open until you reconnect unless one already opened in this tab. See Online and offline. Going somewhere without a connection? Click Save for offline in the app bar and wait for Offline ready. The saved copy (about 11 MB) includes the IFC engine. See Working offline. Look around Drag with the left mouse button to orbit around the model. Scroll the wheel to zoom. Drag with the right mouse button to pan. On a Mac trackpad, hold Shift and drag instead. Press F or click Fit to see the whole model again. Drag with one finger to orbit. Pinch with two fingers to zoom, and move two fingers together to pan. Tap Fit to see the whole model again. Do one real thing: fix a tag Click a wall, door or any element in the model. It turns blue, and its properties appear on the right. In the Properties panel, click the value next to Tag. Type the new tag and press Enter. Look at the Changes section that opened on the left: your change is listed with the old and new value. Save a copy Click Save as IFC in the Changes section, or press Ctrl+S. Find the new file in your downloads folder. It is named after the original with \" (edited)\" added, for example Clinic (edited).ifc. Your original file is never changed. Change IFC properties and save an edited copy explains editing in full. Where to go next The BimCraft screen: every panel by name. Coming from other BIM and drawing programs: what feels different if you are used to Revit. Open a whole project folder: point BimCraft at a project folder and let it sort the files out. View a DWG or DXF drawing: sheets, layers and print view."},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"bimcraft","title":"The BimCraft screen","summary":"A tour of every bar, panel and button, by its on-screen name, and how the screen changes for each kind of file.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["screen","layout","panels","toolbar","interface","ui","tour","properties panel","browser","app bar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-app-bar","text":"The app bar","level":2},{"id":"the-left-panel","text":"The left panel","level":2},{"id":"the-stage","text":"The stage","level":2},{"id":"the-properties-panel","text":"The Properties panel","level":2},{"id":"on-a-small-screen","text":"On a small screen","level":2},{"id":"screen-modes","text":"Screen modes","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe BimCraft screen has a bar across the top and three areas under it: a panel on the left, the stage in the middle and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right. What appears in each area depends on what you opened: an IFC model, a project or family file, a drawing or a folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-app-bar\"\u003EThe app bar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe thin bar across the top belongs to the CadCraft site, not to BimCraft itself. From left to right:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe CadCraft mark: goes to the CadCraft home page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe app name and a version menu. In the staff build it shows which build you are running and lets you switch to a released version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAbout BimCraft\u003C/span\u003E: the app&#039;s page on the site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E: BimCraft&#039;s own settings and the ones every app shares. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull screen\u003C/span\u003E: cycles the screen modes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/workspace#screen-modes\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/workspace\" data-hash=\"screen-modes\"\u003EScreen modes\u003C/a\u003E below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E: the light and dark themes. The 3D view and the 2D drawings follow the theme you pick.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-left-panel\"\u003EThe left panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe left panel always starts with \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen a file or folder\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe drop area, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file, or drop a file or folder here\u003C/span\u003E. Click it to choose one file, or drop a file or folder on it (or anywhere on the page).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E: choose a whole project folder. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecent folders\u003C/span\u003E: appears in Chrome and Edge after you open a folder with the folder picker or by dropping it. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReopen\u003C/span\u003E and the folder name to open it again, or the small × to forget it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder it, sections appear as they apply:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESection\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhen it shows\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat is in it\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn IFC model is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour unsaved edits, newest first, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA model or file card is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFacts about the file: size, schema or release, project, author, element count\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA DWG or DXF is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormat, units, entity and layer counts, sheets, linked drawings, equipment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFolder\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA folder is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile and model counts, size, problems found\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the folder\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou opened a file from a folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturns to the folder report\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA drawing is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA checkbox per layer, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll on\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll off\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn IFC model is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA checkbox per storey, top storey first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn IFC model is open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many elements of each kind the model holds (the 30 most common)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-stage\"\u003EThe stage\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe middle of the screen shows the open file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEmpty\u003C/strong\u003E: &quot;Open a building model.&quot; with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E, and a short description of each format.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC model\u003C/strong\u003E: the 3D view with a toolbar above it: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E; the edit commands \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate library items\u003C/a\u003E); then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdates\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECredits\u003C/span\u003E for library items, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E). A view cube sits in the bottom-right corner. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-ifc-model\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-ifc-model\"\u003EOpen an IFC model and look around in 3D\u003C/a\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E chooses icons, labels or both on these buttons and their order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject or family file\u003C/strong\u003E: the file card, with the preview picture, project information and linked files. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/read-rvt-file\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/read-rvt-file\"\u003ERead a project or family file\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG or DXF\u003C/strong\u003E: a row of tabs (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E, then one per sheet, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E when the drawing has tagged equipment, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E), the drawing, and live X and Y coordinates in a corner. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/view-dwg\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/view-dwg\"\u003EView a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\"\u003ESee a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFolder\u003C/strong\u003E: the folder report, with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart here\u003C/span\u003E box and tables of models, links, IFC models and drawings. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003Estatus line\u003C/strong\u003E at the bottom of the stage tells you what BimCraft is doing (&quot;Reading the model…&quot;, &quot;Building geometry: 1200 of 5400 elements&quot;) and what went wrong, in red, when something fails. Messages about success fade after a few seconds.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-properties-panel\"\u003EThe Properties panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe right panel shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor an IFC model: the element you clicked, with its name, type, tag, GlobalId and every property set. Values you can change look like buttons. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/properties-panel\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/properties-panel\"\u003EProperties panel fields\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a drawing: how to move around, the drawing&#039;s linked drawings and images and whether each was found, and a list of what is \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a file card or folder: a short note on what to open next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-small-screen\"\u003EOn a small screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the window is narrower than a laptop screen, BimCraft stacks its areas: the stage on top, then the panels underneath. Scroll the page to reach the panels. On a phone they form one column; on a tablet, two.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"screen-modes\"\u003EScreen modes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull screen\u003C/span\u003E button in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on every platform, cycles three modes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENormal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFull screen, with the bars still showing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFull screen with the app bar hidden. Move the pointer to the top edge, or tap there, to bring it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go back to normal. Browsers without full screen support (such as on an iPhone) switch only between normal and hidden bars.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/files\"\u003EFiles BimCraft opens and saves\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The BimCraft screen has a bar across the top and three areas under it: a panel on the left, the stage in the middle and the Properties panel on the right. What appears in each area depends on what you opened: an IFC model, a project or family file, a drawing or a folder. The app bar The thin bar across the top belongs to the CadCraft site, not to BimCraft itself. From left to right: The CadCraft mark: goes to the CadCraft home page. The app name and a version menu. In the staff build it shows which build you are running and lets you switch to a released version. About BimCraft: the app's page on the site. Settings: BimCraft's own settings and the ones every app shares. See BimCraft settings. Full screen: cycles the screen modes. See Screen modes below. Paper and Model: the light and dark themes. The 3D view and the 2D drawings follow the theme you pick. The left panel The left panel always starts with Open a file or folder: The drop area, Choose a file, or drop a file or folder here. Click it to choose one file, or drop a file or folder on it (or anywhere on the page). Open a folder: choose a whole project folder. See Open a whole project folder. Recent folders: appears in Chrome and Edge after you open a folder with the folder picker or by dropping it. Click Reopen and the folder name to open it again, or the small × to forget it. Under it, sections appear as they apply: Section When it shows What is in it Changes An IFC model is open Your unsaved edits, newest first, with Undo and Save as IFC File A model or file card is open Facts about the file: size, schema or release, project, author, element count Drawing A DWG or DXF is open Format, units, entity and layer counts, sheets, linked drawings, equipment Folder A folder is open File and model counts, size, problems found Back to the folder You opened a file from a folder Returns to the folder report Layers A drawing is open A checkbox per layer, with All on and All off Storeys An IFC model is open A checkbox per storey, top storey first Elements An IFC model is open How many elements of each kind the model holds (the 30 most common) The stage The middle of the screen shows the open file. Empty: \"Open a building model.\" with Choose a file and Open a folder, and a short description of each format. IFC model: the 3D view with a toolbar above it: Fit, Top, Front, Side, then Edges and Spaces; the edit commands Undo, Redo, Copy, Paste and More (see Copy, paste and duplicate library items); then Library, Equipment, Updates and Credits for library items, and Section (see Cut an IFC model with a section plane). A view cube sits in the bottom-right corner. See Open an IFC model and look around in 3D. Settings, General chooses icons, labels or both on these buttons and their order. Project or family file: the file card, with the preview picture, project information and linked files. See Read a project or family file. DWG or DXF: a row of tabs (Model, then one per sheet, then Equipment when the drawing has tagged equipment, 3D, Fit and Print view), the drawing, and live X and Y coordinates in a corner. See View a DWG or DXF drawing and See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D. Folder: the folder report, with the Start here box and tables of models, links, IFC models and drawings. See Open a whole project folder. The status line at the bottom of the stage tells you what BimCraft is doing (\"Reading the model…\", \"Building geometry: 1200 of 5400 elements\") and what went wrong, in red, when something fails. Messages about success fade after a few seconds. The Properties panel The right panel shows: For an IFC model: the element you clicked, with its name, type, tag, GlobalId and every property set. Values you can change look like buttons. See Properties panel fields. For a drawing: how to move around, the drawing's linked drawings and images and whether each was found, and a list of what is Not drawn yet. For a file card or folder: a short note on what to open next. On a small screen When the window is narrower than a laptop screen, BimCraft stacks its areas: the stage on top, then the panels underneath. Scroll the page to reach the panels. On a phone they form one column; on a tablet, two. Screen modes The Full screen button in the app bar, or Ctrl+Shift+F on every platform, cycles three modes: Normal. Full screen, with the bars still showing. Full screen with the app bar hidden. Move the pointer to the top edge, or tap there, to bring it back. Press Esc to go back to normal. Browsers without full screen support (such as on an iPhone) switch only between normal and hidden bars. Related Keys and mouse Files BimCraft opens and saves"},{"slug":"files","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Files BimCraft opens and saves","summary":"Every format BimCraft opens, what it keeps, what it saves, where saved files go, and what changes between browsers and when you are offline.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["formats","open","save","export","download","ifc","rvt","rfa","rte","rft","dwg","dxf","csv","upload","privacy","file size"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-opens","text":"What it opens","level":2},{"id":"not-opened","text":"Not opened","level":3},{"id":"what-it-saves","text":"What it saves","level":2},{"id":"saving-a-second-time","text":"Saving a second time","level":2},{"id":"large-files-and-memory","text":"Large files and memory","level":2},{"id":"online-and-offline","text":"Online and offline","level":2},{"id":"cloud-saving","text":"Cloud saving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft reads files on your computer, in the browser tab. \u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is uploaded\u003C/strong\u003E to CadCraft or anyone else. When you save, BimCraft hands the browser a new file to download; it never writes over a file you opened.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-opens\"\u003EWhat it opens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtensions\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model in 3D, storeys, element counts and every element&#039;s properties. Names, tags and single property values can be changed.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject and family files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA file card: release, build, worksharing, project information, family parameters, linked files and the preview picture. No 3D.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing in 2D: model space, every sheet, layers, linked drawings, images and print view. Model space in 3D too. Formats from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 are the ones checked.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same as DWG.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eany\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA report of every model and drawing in it. Any IFC, model or drawing in the report opens with a click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/file-formats\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/file-formats\"\u003EWhat BimCraft reads from each format\u003C/a\u003E goes into detail.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft decides what a file is by looking at its first bytes as well as its name, so a project file with an unusual extension still opens as a file card.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"not-opened\"\u003ENot opened\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E: BimCraft says &quot;.ifczip is not supported yet. Save or export the model as a plain .ifc file.&quot; Unzip the \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E (it holds a plain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E) or export again as plain IFC.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDGN drawings are listed in a folder report but do not open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPDF, images and office files do not open on their own. Images are used only as pictures inside a DWG.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-saves\"\u003EWhat it saves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou click\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou get\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA new IFC file with your edits, named &quot;\u003Cem\u003Eoriginal name\u003C/em\u003E (edited).ifc&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour browser&#039;s downloads folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E in the equipment schedule\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA CSV of the items in the list, named &quot;\u003Cem\u003Edrawing name\u003C/em\u003E equipment.csv&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour browser&#039;s downloads folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThat is all BimCraft writes. It does not save DWG, DXF or project files, and it never changes the files you opened.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome and Edge save downloads to the folder set in their settings, usually Downloads. To be asked where to save each time, turn on &quot;Ask where to save each file before downloading&quot; in the browser&#039;s download settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFirefox saves to the Downloads folder unless you set it to &quot;Always ask you where to save files&quot; in its settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari saves to Downloads unless you choose another folder under \u003Cstrong\u003ESafari &gt; Settings &gt; General &gt; File download location\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving-a-second-time\"\u003ESaving a second time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach save downloads the whole model with \u003Cstrong\u003Eevery edit since you opened it\u003C/strong\u003E, not only the edits made after the last save. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list starts empty again after each save, so you cannot undo past a save. To start again from the original, open the original file again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"large-files-and-memory\"\u003ELarge files and memory\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC\u003C/strong\u003E files are read completely into the tab&#039;s memory, and the 3D geometry takes more memory on top. A model of a few hundred megabytes can be too much for a laptop. Above 1.5 million triangles, edges are turned off to save memory (&quot;Edges are off for very large models&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProject files\u003C/strong\u003E are not read in full. BimCraft reads only the small parts it needs, about 40 KB to 100 KB however large the file is, so a 500 MB central model on a network drive opens in a moment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG and DXF\u003C/strong\u003E files are read completely. A very large drawing shows &quot;This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFolders\u003C/strong\u003E up to 20,000 files open. Only the few kilobytes each model card needs are read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/platforms\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/platforms\"\u003EBrowsers, devices and memory\u003C/a\u003E for more.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-and-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening files never needs the internet. One reader loads from the site the first time you need it: the IFC engine, the first time you open an IFC model (&quot;Starting the IFC engine…&quot;). The DWG and DXF readers are part of the app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline. If the app bar&#039;s button says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, every format opens as usual. If not, an IFC model cannot open unless one already opened in this tab: reconnect first. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/online-offline\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/online-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo use BimCraft with no connection, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar while you are online and wait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E. The saved copy is about 11 MB because it includes the IFC engine, so IFC models open offline too. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E explains saved copies in general.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cloud-saving\"\u003ECloud saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving is not available yet. Save your edited copies on your own computer or your office&#039;s file server, as you would any other file. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EYour files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"BimCraft reads files on your computer, in the browser tab. Nothing is uploaded to CadCraft or anyone else. When you save, BimCraft hands the browser a new file to download; it never writes over a file you opened. What it opens Format Extensions What you get IFC .ifc The model in 3D, storeys, element counts and every element's properties. Names, tags and single property values can be changed. Project and family files .rvt .rfa .rte .rft A file card: release, build, worksharing, project information, family parameters, linked files and the preview picture. No 3D. DWG .dwg The drawing in 2D: model space, every sheet, layers, linked drawings, images and print view. Model space in 3D too. Formats from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 are the ones checked. DXF .dxf The same as DWG. A folder any A report of every model and drawing in it. Any IFC, model or drawing in the report opens with a click. What BimCraft reads from each format goes into detail. BimCraft decides what a file is by looking at its first bytes as well as its name, so a project file with an unusual extension still opens as a file card. Not opened .ifczip and .ifcxml: BimCraft says \".ifczip is not supported yet. Save or export the model as a plain .ifc file.\" Unzip the .ifczip (it holds a plain .ifc) or export again as plain IFC. DGN drawings are listed in a folder report but do not open. PDF, images and office files do not open on their own. Images are used only as pictures inside a DWG. What it saves You click You get Where Save as IFC A new IFC file with your edits, named \"original name (edited).ifc\" Your browser's downloads folder Download CSV in the equipment schedule A CSV of the items in the list, named \"drawing name equipment.csv\" Your browser's downloads folder That is all BimCraft writes. It does not save DWG, DXF or project files, and it never changes the files you opened. Chrome and Edge save downloads to the folder set in their settings, usually Downloads. To be asked where to save each time, turn on \"Ask where to save each file before downloading\" in the browser's download settings. Firefox saves to the Downloads folder unless you set it to \"Always ask you where to save files\" in its settings. Safari saves to Downloads unless you choose another folder under Safari \u003E Settings \u003E General \u003E File download location. Saving a second time Each save downloads the whole model with every edit since you opened it, not only the edits made after the last save. The Changes list starts empty again after each save, so you cannot undo past a save. To start again from the original, open the original file again. Large files and memory IFC files are read completely into the tab's memory, and the 3D geometry takes more memory on top. A model of a few hundred megabytes can be too much for a laptop. Above 1.5 million triangles, edges are turned off to save memory (\"Edges are off for very large models\"). Project files are not read in full. BimCraft reads only the small parts it needs, about 40 KB to 100 KB however large the file is, so a 500 MB central model on a network drive opens in a moment. DWG and DXF files are read completely. A very large drawing shows \"This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.\" Folders up to 20,000 files open. Only the few kilobytes each model card needs are read. See Browsers, devices and memory for more. Online and offline Opening files never needs the internet. One reader loads from the site the first time you need it: the IFC engine, the first time you open an IFC model (\"Starting the IFC engine…\"). The DWG and DXF readers are part of the app. You are offline. If the app bar's button says Offline ready, every format opens as usual. If not, an IFC model cannot open unless one already opened in this tab: reconnect first. See Online and offline. To use BimCraft with no connection, click Save for offline in the app bar while you are online and wait for Offline ready. The saved copy is about 11 MB because it includes the IFC engine, so IFC models open offline too. Working offline explains saved copies in general. Cloud saving Cloud saving is not available yet. Save your edited copies on your own computer or your office's file server, as you would any other file. See Your files."},{"slug":"open-ifc-model","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Open an IFC model and look around in 3D","summary":"Open an IFC file, orbit, pan and zoom, and use Fit, Top, Front and Side to get your bearings.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["ifc","3d","orbit","pan","zoom","fit","top view","front view","side view","navigate","viewcube","camera"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"moving-around","text":"Moving around","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen an IFC model, move around it in 3D and jump to the standard views. About five minutes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou need an \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file. Most BIM programs export one; in Revit it is \u003Cstrong\u003EFile &gt; Export &gt; IFC\u003C/strong\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\"\u003EGet a project file into 3D through IFC\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E (or the drop area at the top left).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose your \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file and confirm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for the status line to finish. It shows &quot;Starting the IFC engine…&quot; the first time, then &quot;Reading the model…&quot; and &quot;Building geometry: … of … elements&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the model: it opens in a three-quarter view, fitted to the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar above the model to look straight down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E to look at the front, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E to look from the side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, to go back to the three-quarter view of the whole model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe model in the middle, with thin dark edges for a drawing-like look.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel on the left: the file name, size, IFC schema (for example IFC2X3 or IFC4), project name, phase, the program it was exported from, author, export date, and element and triangle counts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E lists under it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe status line: &quot;Opened \u003Cem\u003Efile name\u003C/em\u003E: \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E elements in \u003Cem\u003Es\u003C/em\u003E s&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-around\"\u003EMoving around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button, or hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and drag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel, or drag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee everything\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac;input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac, Mouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad: drag with one finger pressed to orbit; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag to pan; scroll with two fingers or pinch to zoom. A two-finger click is a right-click, and you can drag with it to pan, but \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag is easier.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with one finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch with two fingers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove two fingers together\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee everything\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOrbiting always turns around a point in the middle of the model. Panning moves that point, so after a long pan the orbit may feel off-center: click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to reset it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting named views.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft has four fixed views (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E), the view cube in the bottom-right corner and free orbit. It does not save views.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELooking for plans and sections.\u003C/strong\u003E There are no 2D plans or section boxes in this build. Cut the model with one \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E plane (\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E), or turn storeys off: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\"\u003EShow and hide storeys, spaces and edges\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a project file and expecting 3D.\u003C/strong\u003E A project file opens as a file card. Export IFC first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening an \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Unzip it, or export plain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA very large model makes the tab slow or crash.\u003C/strong\u003E See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/platforms\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/platforms\"\u003EBrowsers, devices and memory\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/inspect-element\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/inspect-element\"\u003EPick an element and read its properties\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Open an IFC model, move around it in 3D and jump to the standard views. About five minutes. You need an .ifc file. Most BIM programs export one; in Revit it is File \u003E Export \u003E IFC. See Get a project file into 3D through IFC. Steps Click Choose a file (or the drop area at the top left). Choose your .ifc file and confirm. Wait for the status line to finish. It shows \"Starting the IFC engine…\" the first time, then \"Reading the model…\" and \"Building geometry: … of … elements\". Look at the model: it opens in a three-quarter view, fitted to the screen. Click Top in the toolbar above the model to look straight down. Click Front to look at the front, then Side to look from the side. Click Fit, or press F, to go back to the three-quarter view of the whole model. What you should see The model in the middle, with thin dark edges for a drawing-like look. The File panel on the left: the file name, size, IFC schema (for example IFC2X3 or IFC4), project name, phase, the program it was exported from, author, export date, and element and triangle counts. The Storeys and Elements lists under it. The status line: \"Opened file name: n elements in s s\". Moving around To Do this Orbit Drag with the left button Pan Drag with the right button, or hold Shift (or Ctrl) and drag with the left button Zoom Scroll the wheel, or drag with the middle button See everything Press F or click Fit On a trackpad: drag with one finger pressed to orbit; hold Shift or Ctrl and drag to pan; scroll with two fingers or pinch to zoom. A two-finger click is a right-click, and you can drag with it to pan, but Shift and drag is easier. To Do this Orbit Drag with one finger Zoom Pinch with two fingers Pan Move two fingers together See everything Tap Fit Orbiting always turns around a point in the middle of the model. Panning moves that point, so after a long pan the orbit may feel off-center: click Fit to reset it. Common mistakes Expecting named views. BimCraft has four fixed views (Fit, Top, Front, Side), the view cube in the bottom-right corner and free orbit. It does not save views. Looking for plans and sections. There are no 2D plans or section boxes in this build. Cut the model with one Section plane (Cut an IFC model with a section plane), or turn storeys off: Show and hide storeys, spaces and edges. Opening a project file and expecting 3D. A project file opens as a file card. Export IFC first. Opening an .ifczip. Unzip it, or export plain .ifc. A very large model makes the tab slow or crash. See Browsers, devices and memory. Next Pick an element and read its properties."},{"slug":"storeys-and-spaces","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Show and hide storeys, spaces and edges","summary":"Turn storeys off to see inside a building, show rooms and spaces, and switch the edge lines on and off.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["storeys","levels","floors","hide","isolate","rooms","spaces","ifcspace","edges","visibility","section"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps-look-at-one-floor","text":"Steps: look at one floor","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"steps-show-rooms-and-spaces","text":"Steps: show rooms and spaces","level":2},{"id":"steps-edges","text":"Steps: edges","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELook inside an IFC model by turning off the storeys above the one you care about, then show the rooms and spaces, and switch the edge lines off for a cleaner look.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-look-at-one-floor\"\u003ESteps: look at one floor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-ifc-model\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-ifc-model\"\u003EOpen an IFC model and look around in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E list on the left. The top storey is first, like reading a section from the roof down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick the roof and every storey above the floor you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E to look down onto that floor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick the storeys again to put them back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach storey you untick disappears at once, with everything on it. The list only shows storeys that hold something visible. Elements that are not assigned to any storey are grouped as \u003Cstrong\u003ENot on a storey\u003C/strong\u003E at the bottom; site elements and some furniture often land there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-show-rooms-and-spaces\"\u003ESteps: show rooms and spaces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar above the model. The button stays pressed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the model: rooms and spaces appear as pale, see-through volumes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a space to read its properties, such as its name, number and area, on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E again to hide them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESpaces are hidden when a model opens because they would otherwise cover the walls and floors.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-edges\"\u003ESteps: edges\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges\u003C/span\u003E to turn the thin edge lines off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick it again to turn them back on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn very large models (over 1.5 million triangles) the button is greyed out with the tip &quot;Edges are off for very large models&quot;. This saves memory.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnticking a storey and still seeing parts of it.\u003C/strong\u003E An element belongs to the storey the exporting program assigned it to. A tall wall or a stair often belongs only to its base storey, so it stays when the storey above is hidden, or vanishes with the storey below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting a section box.\u003C/strong\u003E It is not built yet. Cut the model with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E), hide storeys, or untick rows in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel tree\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-tree\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-tree\"\u003EFind your way with the model tree\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClicking a space through a wall.\u003C/strong\u003E Clicking picks the nearest visible surface. Hide the storey above, or switch to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, then click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStorey names look odd.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft shows the storey names saved in the file. If they say &quot;Level 1&quot; or a number, that is what the exporter wrote.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Look inside an IFC model by turning off the storeys above the one you care about, then show the rooms and spaces, and switch the edge lines off for a cleaner look. Steps: look at one floor Open an IFC model. See Open an IFC model and look around in 3D. Find the Storeys list on the left. The top storey is first, like reading a section from the roof down. Untick the roof and every storey above the floor you want. Click Top to look down onto that floor. Tick the storeys again to put them back. What you should see Each storey you untick disappears at once, with everything on it. The list only shows storeys that hold something visible. Elements that are not assigned to any storey are grouped as Not on a storey at the bottom; site elements and some furniture often land there. Steps: show rooms and spaces Click Spaces in the toolbar above the model. The button stays pressed. Look at the model: rooms and spaces appear as pale, see-through volumes. Click a space to read its properties, such as its name, number and area, on the right. Click Spaces again to hide them. Spaces are hidden when a model opens because they would otherwise cover the walls and floors. Steps: edges Click Edges to turn the thin edge lines off. Click it again to turn them back on. On very large models (over 1.5 million triangles) the button is greyed out with the tip \"Edges are off for very large models\". This saves memory. Common mistakes Unticking a storey and still seeing parts of it. An element belongs to the storey the exporting program assigned it to. A tall wall or a stair often belongs only to its base storey, so it stays when the storey above is hidden, or vanishes with the storey below. Expecting a section box. It is not built yet. Cut the model with Section (see Cut an IFC model with a section plane), hide storeys, or untick rows in the Model tree (see Find your way with the model tree). Clicking a space through a wall. Clicking picks the nearest visible surface. Hide the storey above, or switch to Top, then click. Storey names look odd. BimCraft shows the storey names saved in the file. If they say \"Level 1\" or a number, that is what the exporter wrote."},{"slug":"find","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Find elements by class, name or property","summary":"Find every element that matches one or more rules — its class, name, type, storey, material or any property value — pick them all, and keep the rules as a named search set.","section":"tasks","order":22,"keywords":["find","search","filter","query","rule","search set","selection set","saved search","property","value","class","material","pick all"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick every element of one kind or with one value in a single step: every door, every wall on Level 2, every element whose fire rating is EI60, every wall that costs more than a set amount.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the first rule, choose a property (the element&#039;s own ones first, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClass\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStorey\u003C/span\u003E, then the model&#039;s property sets), how to compare, and a value. The value box suggests the values the model has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo narrow it down, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a rule\u003C/span\u003E. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery rule\u003C/span\u003E to need all of them, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAny rule\u003C/span\u003E for one of them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in a value box. What matches is picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo keep the rules, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as a set\u003C/span\u003E, type a name and click it again. Click a set&#039;s name later to find with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe matches picked in the 3D view and the model tree, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E saying how many by class. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to show only them, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to hide them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWords match without caring about capitals. Numbers compare as numbers: &quot;is more than 1000&quot; means the value, not the text.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ehas a value\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ehas no value\u003C/span\u003E need no value; they find what is filled in, or missing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaved sets stay in this browser for every model you open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is found.\u003C/strong\u003E A rule needs a value it can match: look at the suggestions in the value box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA property is missing from the list.\u003C/strong\u003E No element with a shape in this model has it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/color-by-property\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/color-by-property\"\u003EColor elements by a property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Pick every element of one kind or with one value in a single step: every door, every wall on Level 2, every element whose fire rating is EI60, every wall that costs more than a set amount. Steps Open an IFC model. Open Find in the left panel. In the first rule, choose a property (the element's own ones first, such as Class and Storey, then the model's property sets), how to compare, and a value. The value box suggests the values the model has. To narrow it down, click Add a rule. Choose Every rule to need all of them, or Any rule for one of them. Click Find, or press Enter in a value box. What matches is picked. To keep the rules, click Save as a set, type a name and click it again. Click a set's name later to find with it. What you should see The matches picked in the 3D view and the model tree, and Properties saying how many by class. Press I to show only them, H to hide them. Words match without caring about capitals. Numbers compare as numbers: \"is more than 1000\" means the value, not the text. has a value and has no value need no value; they find what is filled in, or missing. Saved sets stay in this browser for every model you open. Common mistakes Nothing is found. A rule needs a value it can match: look at the suggestions in the value box. A property is missing from the list. No element with a shape in this model has it. Next Color elements by a property Hide, isolate and see through elements"},{"slug":"model-tree","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Find your way with the model tree","summary":"Browse an IFC model by project, site, building, storey and space, with its elements grouped by kind; tick boxes to show and hide, click a row to pick, double-click to zoom.","section":"tasks","order":23,"keywords":["model tree","tree","structure","spatial structure","browser","outline","navigator","storey","level","floor","space","room","group","class","hide","show","pick","select","zoom"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"keys","text":"Keys","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee how a model is put together and get to any element without hunting for it in 3D: the building, its storeys, the spaces on each storey, and the elements in them, grouped by kind (walls, doors, slabs…).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel tree\u003C/span\u003E on the left opens down to the storeys.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the arrow beside a row to open or close it. A storey holds its spaces first, then its elements by kind with how many there are. An element made of parts, such as a stair, holds its parts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick a row&#039;s box to hide everything in it; tick it to show it again. A box with a dash means only some of what is in it is shown.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a row&#039;s name to pick what it stands for: one element, or every element in a group, storey or space. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click adds them to the pick, or takes them out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003Ei\u003C/span\u003E button beside a storey, space or building to see its own properties.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click a row to zoom to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you pick an element in the 3D view, the tree opens down to it and marks it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHidden elements are struck through. The tree&#039;s boxes and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) work together: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements the model does not place in any storey are in a group at the end, \u003Cstrong\u003ENot in the spatial structure\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA long group shows 200 rows at a time; click \u003Cstrong\u003EShow more\u003C/strong\u003E under it for the rest.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys\"\u003EKeys\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a row&#039;s name focused: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E move, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes or goes up, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ticks the box, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E picks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA storey&#039;s box is ticked but nothing shows.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E list below the tree turns whole storeys off on its own: tick it there too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA row picks nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E Some rows, such as an empty space, have no elements that can be drawn; the status line says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-elements\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-elements\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste building elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\"\u003EShow and hide storeys, spaces and edges\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See how a model is put together and get to any element without hunting for it in 3D: the building, its storeys, the spaces on each storey, and the elements in them, grouped by kind (walls, doors, slabs…). Steps Open an IFC model. The Model tree on the left opens down to the storeys. Click the arrow beside a row to open or close it. A storey holds its spaces first, then its elements by kind with how many there are. An element made of parts, such as a stair, holds its parts. Untick a row's box to hide everything in it; tick it to show it again. A box with a dash means only some of what is in it is shown. Click a row's name to pick what it stands for: one element, or every element in a group, storey or space. Ctrl+click adds them to the pick, or takes them out. Click the i button beside a storey, space or building to see its own properties. Double-click a row to zoom to it. What you should see When you pick an element in the 3D view, the tree opens down to it and marks it. Hidden elements are struck through. The tree's boxes and Show (H, I, Shift+H) work together: see Hide, isolate and see through elements. Elements the model does not place in any storey are in a group at the end, Not in the spatial structure. A long group shows 200 rows at a time; click Show more under it for the rest. Keys With a row's name focused: ↑ and ↓ move, → opens, ← closes or goes up, Space ticks the box, Enter picks. Common mistakes A storey's box is ticked but nothing shows. The Storeys list below the tree turns whole storeys off on its own: tick it there too. A row picks nothing. Some rows, such as an empty space, have no elements that can be drawn; the status line says so. Next Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements Show and hide storeys, spaces and edges"},{"slug":"hide-and-isolate","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Hide, isolate and see through elements","summary":"Pick several elements, hide them, show only them, or show them with everything else see-through, then bring everything back.","section":"tasks","order":24,"keywords":["hide","isolate","see-through","transparent","ghost","x-ray","show all","unhide","select several","multiple selection","ctrl click","shift click","visibility"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClear the view down to what you are looking at: hide elements that are in the way, show only the ones you picked, or keep everything else on screen as a faint outline so you still know where you are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-ifc-model\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-ifc-model\"\u003EOpen an IFC model and look around in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an element to pick it. To pick more, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click each one. Clicking a picked element that way takes it out again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar and choose what to do, or press its key:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) hides the picked elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIsolate\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows only the picked elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESee-through\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows the picked elements as they are and everything else see-through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, to show every element again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith several elements picked, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E says how many and lists them by class. Click one on its own to see its properties.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHidden elements cannot be picked: a click lands on whatever is behind them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESee-through elements cannot be picked either, so you can click straight through walls to what you isolated.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEdges hide with their element, and turn faint with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHiding, isolating and see-through stay as they are while you edit, place library items or cut the model with a section plane. Opening another model shows everything again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe menu items are greyed out.\u003C/strong\u003E Nothing is picked. Click an element first; point at an item to read why it cannot act.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIsolate showed a wall I had hidden.\u003C/strong\u003E Isolating shows exactly what you picked, hidden or not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA whole storey is missing.\u003C/strong\u003E That is the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E list, not \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E: tick the storey again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\"\u003EShow and hide storeys, spaces and edges\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Clear the view down to what you are looking at: hide elements that are in the way, show only the ones you picked, or keep everything else on screen as a faint outline so you still know where you are. Steps Open an IFC model. See Open an IFC model and look around in 3D. Click an element to pick it. To pick more, hold Ctrl or Shift and click each one. Clicking a picked element that way takes it out again. Open Show in the toolbar and choose what to do, or press its key: Hide (H) hides the picked elements. Isolate (I) shows only the picked elements. See-through (T) shows the picked elements as they are and everything else see-through. Click Show all in the same menu, or press Shift+H, to show every element again. What you should see With several elements picked, Properties says how many and lists them by class. Click one on its own to see its properties. Hidden elements cannot be picked: a click lands on whatever is behind them. See-through elements cannot be picked either, so you can click straight through walls to what you isolated. Edges hide with their element, and turn faint with it. Hiding, isolating and see-through stay as they are while you edit, place library items or cut the model with a section plane. Opening another model shows everything again. Common mistakes The menu items are greyed out. Nothing is picked. Click an element first; point at an item to read why it cannot act. Isolate showed a wall I had hidden. Isolating shows exactly what you picked, hidden or not. A whole storey is missing. That is the Storeys list, not Show all: tick the storey again. Next Show and hide storeys, spaces and edges Cut an IFC model with a section plane"},{"slug":"section-plane","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Cut an IFC model with a section plane","summary":"Slice the 3D model from the top, front or side, or keep only what is inside a section box, to see inside; move the cut by dragging or typing, flip it, and pick what the cut shows.","section":"tasks","order":25,"keywords":["section","section plane","section box","clip box","cut","clipping","slice","plan cut","floor plan","elevation","inside","interior","flip","offset"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"a-section-box","text":"A section box","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECut through an IFC model with one plane to see inside it: from the top for a plan, or from the front or side for a section through the building.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-ifc-model\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-ifc-model\"\u003EOpen an IFC model and look around in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The model is cut halfway up, and a bar appears under the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose where to cut from: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E. Front and Side also turn the view to look at the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the cut: drag the \u003Cstrong\u003EOffset\u003C/strong\u003E slider, or type a distance in metres in the box and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. You can type millimetres too, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1200 mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip\u003C/span\u003E to keep the other side of the cut.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an element on the cut to see its properties.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOff\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E again, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take the cut away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-section-box\"\u003EA section box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox\u003C/span\u003E in the same bar to keep only what is inside a box. Each of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E has two sliders: where the box starts and where it ends, in metres from the model&#039;s edge. The values show beside them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBox round the pick\u003C/span\u003E fits the box round the elements you picked, with 0.3 m to spare, and zooms to them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhole model\u003C/span\u003E opens the box to the whole model again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E go back to one plane.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETop\u003C/strong\u003E keeps what is below the cut, so you look down into the rooms. The offset is measured up from the bottom of the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFront\u003C/strong\u003E keeps what is behind the cut, seen from the front; \u003Cstrong\u003ESide\u003C/strong\u003E keeps what is beyond it, seen from the side. The offset is measured from the front or the side of the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA thin colored outline shows where the plane is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere the plane cuts through a wall, slab or other solid, the cut face is filled in a dark color, like a drawn section. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill\u003C/span\u003E in the bar turns this off and on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe view cube in the bottom-right corner turns the model to look at the cut from any side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClicking picks what you can see: anything cut away cannot be picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing changed.\u003C/strong\u003E The cut may be above the roof or outside the model. Drag the slider, or type a smaller offset.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe offset jumped back.\u003C/strong\u003E A distance beyond the model is moved to its end, and the status line says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe cut is hollow.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill\u003C/span\u003E in the bar is off: click it to fill the cut faces of walls, slabs and other solids.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA filled cut looks speckled or misses a piece.\u003C/strong\u003E Filling needs closed solids; an element whose shape in the file is not closed cannot be filled cleanly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/storeys-and-spaces\"\u003EShow and hide storeys, spaces and edges\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\"\u003ESee a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D\u003C/a\u003E — drawings in 3D have the same section plane\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Cut through an IFC model with one plane to see inside it: from the top for a plan, or from the front or side for a section through the building. Steps Open an IFC model. See Open an IFC model and look around in 3D. Click Section in the toolbar, or press X. The model is cut halfway up, and a bar appears under the toolbar. Choose where to cut from: Top, Front or Side. Front and Side also turn the view to look at the cut. Move the cut: drag the Offset slider, or type a distance in metres in the box and press Enter. You can type millimetres too, for example 1200 mm. Click Flip to keep the other side of the cut. Click an element on the cut to see its properties. Click Off, click Section again, or press X to take the cut away. A section box Click Box in the same bar to keep only what is inside a box. Each of X, Y and Z has two sliders: where the box starts and where it ends, in metres from the model's edge. The values show beside them. Box round the pick fits the box round the elements you picked, with 0.3 m to spare, and zooms to them. Whole model opens the box to the whole model again. Top, Front or Side go back to one plane. What you should see Top keeps what is below the cut, so you look down into the rooms. The offset is measured up from the bottom of the model. Front keeps what is behind the cut, seen from the front; Side keeps what is beyond it, seen from the side. The offset is measured from the front or the side of the model. A thin colored outline shows where the plane is. Where the plane cuts through a wall, slab or other solid, the cut face is filled in a dark color, like a drawn section. Fill in the bar turns this off and on. The view cube in the bottom-right corner turns the model to look at the cut from any side. Clicking picks what you can see: anything cut away cannot be picked. Common mistakes Nothing changed. The cut may be above the roof or outside the model. Drag the slider, or type a smaller offset. The offset jumped back. A distance beyond the model is moved to its end, and the status line says so. The cut is hollow. Fill in the bar is off: click it to fill the cut faces of walls, slabs and other solids. A filled cut looks speckled or misses a piece. Filling needs closed solids; an element whose shape in the file is not closed cannot be filled cleanly. Next Show and hide storeys, spaces and edges See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D — drawings in 3D have the same section plane"},{"slug":"color-by-property","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Color elements by a property","summary":"Paint every element by the value of one property, such as its class, type, storey, material, fire rating or cost, read the legend, and click a value to show only those elements.","section":"tasks","order":26,"keywords":["color","colour","color by","legend","smart view","appearance","property","type","material","fire rating","filter","isolate","theme","values"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee at a glance how a property is spread over the model: which walls are external, which materials are used where, which elements have no fire rating. Every element takes the color of its value, and the legend says what each color means and how many elements have it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E tab on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a property in the list. The first ones belong to every element (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClass\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStorey\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E…); the rest come from the model&#039;s property sets and quantity sets, under the set&#039;s name. The number after each one is how many elements have it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the legend: a color, the value and how many elements have it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a value to show only those elements. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click to add more values. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to show everything again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOff\u003C/span\u003E to go back to the model&#039;s own colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUp to seven values each get their own color, the most common value first. More values than that share one gray, listed as \u003Cstrong\u003Eother values\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA number with many different values is split into six equal ranges, from light (low) to dark (high).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements without the property are a pale gray, listed as \u003Cstrong\u003ENo value\u003C/strong\u003E, so the colored ones stand out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe colors change with Paper and Model, and stay while you edit the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA type&#039;s property counts for every element of that type; when an element has its own value for the same property, its own value wins.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA property is missing from the list.\u003C/strong\u003E No element with a shape in the model has it. Properties of storeys and spaces themselves are not listed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything is the same color.\u003C/strong\u003E Every element has the same value, or none: look at the legend&#039;s counts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-tree\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-tree\"\u003EFind your way with the model tree\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See at a glance how a property is spread over the model: which walls are external, which materials are used where, which elements have no fire rating. Every element takes the color of its value, and the legend says what each color means and how many elements have it. Steps Open an IFC model. Click the Color tab on the right. Choose a property in the list. The first ones belong to every element (Class, Type, Storey, Material, Name…); the rest come from the model's property sets and quantity sets, under the set's name. The number after each one is how many elements have it. Read the legend: a color, the value and how many elements have it. Click a value to show only those elements. Hold Ctrl and click to add more values. Press Shift+H to show everything again. Click Off to go back to the model's own colors. What you should see Up to seven values each get their own color, the most common value first. More values than that share one gray, listed as other values. A number with many different values is split into six equal ranges, from light (low) to dark (high). Elements without the property are a pale gray, listed as No value, so the colored ones stand out. The colors change with Paper and Model, and stay while you edit the model. A type's property counts for every element of that type; when an element has its own value for the same property, its own value wins. Common mistakes A property is missing from the list. No element with a shape in the model has it. Properties of storeys and spaces themselves are not listed. Everything is the same color. Every element has the same value, or none: look at the legend's counts. Next Hide, isolate and see through elements Find your way with the model tree"},{"slug":"several-models","scope":"bimcraft","title":"See several IFC models together","summary":"Add more IFC files to the scene, such as architecture, structure and services, see them in the same coordinates, move one, hide one, and choose which one you edit.","section":"tasks","order":27,"keywords":["federate","federation","federated model","several models","multiple files","combine","overlay","coordination","discipline","structure","mep","services","offset","move model","active model","add a model"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELook at models from different disciplines in one place: the architect&#039;s model with the structural and services models in it. Each stays its own file; BimCraft only shows them together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the first IFC model as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModels\u003C/span\u003E on the left, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model…\u003C/span\u003E and choose one or more IFC files. Each appears in the list and in the 3D view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick a model to hide it; tick it to show it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a model comes in at the wrong place (a different survey point), click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E under it, type how far to move it along X, Y and Z in metres, and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe model with the dot is the one you edit. Click another model&#039;s dot to edit that one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick × beside a model to take it out of the scene.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery model in the first model&#039;s coordinates, as its file places it. Models exported from the same project line up without moving anything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPicking an element of a model you are not editing shows its properties read only, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit that model\u003C/span\u003E to switch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel tree\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list, placing library items and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E all work on the model you edit. Hide, isolate, see-through and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E work across every model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E downloads the model you edit, with its changes only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA model is far away or missing.\u003C/strong\u003E Its file uses other coordinates. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to find it, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E it into place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete or Copy is greyed out on a picked element.\u003C/strong\u003E It belongs to another model: switch to it first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a file with Choose a file replaced everything.\u003C/strong\u003E That starts a new scene. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model…\u003C/span\u003E to add files to the one you have.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/color-by-property\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/color-by-property\"\u003EColor elements by a property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Look at models from different disciplines in one place: the architect's model with the structural and services models in it. Each stays its own file; BimCraft only shows them together. Steps Open the first IFC model as usual. In Models on the left, click Add a model… and choose one or more IFC files. Each appears in the list and in the 3D view. Untick a model to hide it; tick it to show it again. If a model comes in at the wrong place (a different survey point), click Move under it, type how far to move it along X, Y and Z in metres, and click Apply. The model with the dot is the one you edit. Click another model's dot to edit that one. Click × beside a model to take it out of the scene. What you should see Every model in the first model's coordinates, as its file places it. Models exported from the same project line up without moving anything. Picking an element of a model you are not editing shows its properties read only, with Edit that model to switch. The Model tree, Storeys, Color, the Changes list, placing library items and Save as IFC all work on the model you edit. Hide, isolate, see-through and Select all work across every model. Save as IFC downloads the model you edit, with its changes only. Common mistakes A model is far away or missing. Its file uses other coordinates. Use Fit to find it, then Move it into place. Delete or Copy is greyed out on a picked element. It belongs to another model: switch to it first. Opening a file with Choose a file replaced everything. That starts a new scene. Use Add a model… to add files to the one you have. Next Hide, isolate and see through elements Color elements by a property"},{"slug":"measure","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Measure distances and face areas in 3D","summary":"Measure between two points of an IFC model with snapping to corners and edges, read the difference along X, Y and Z, and measure the area of a flat face.","section":"tasks","order":28,"keywords":["measure","measurement","distance","length","dimension","area","face area","snap","corner","edge","tape","ruler","clearance"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECheck a clearance, a wall length or the size of a face straight from the model, without opening the authoring program.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A bar opens at the bottom of the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA distance:\u003C/strong\u003E with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDistance\u003C/span\u003E chosen, click the first point, then the second. Near a corner the pointer snaps to it, near an edge onto the edge; the small label at the pointer says which. Each pair of clicks makes one measurement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA face&#039;s area:\u003C/strong\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFace area\u003C/span\u003E and click a face. The whole flat face lights up and its area shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E to remove the measurements, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) to stop measuring.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA line between the two points with its length on it, and in the bar the length with how far it goes along X, Y and Z (in the model&#039;s axes, Z up), in metres.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA face&#039;s area in square metres, on the face and in the bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELabels stay on their measurements while you orbit, pan and zoom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you measure, clicks do not pick elements. A section plane or box still cuts the model, and you can measure what it shows, cut faces included.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe point lands on the face, not the corner.\u003C/strong\u003E Snapping looks at the element under the pointer. Move closer to the corner, or zoom in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe area is smaller than expected.\u003C/strong\u003E Openings are left out, and a face that bends is several faces: click each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stopped measuring.\u003C/strong\u003E The first \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drops a half-made distance; the second stops.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Check a clearance, a wall length or the size of a face straight from the model, without opening the authoring program. Steps Open an IFC model. Click Measure in the toolbar, or press M. A bar opens at the bottom of the view. A distance: with Distance chosen, click the first point, then the second. Near a corner the pointer snaps to it, near an edge onto the edge; the small label at the pointer says which. Each pair of clicks makes one measurement. A face's area: choose Face area and click a face. The whole flat face lights up and its area shows. Click Clear to remove the measurements, or Done (or Esc) to stop measuring. What you should see A line between the two points with its length on it, and in the bar the length with how far it goes along X, Y and Z (in the model's axes, Z up), in metres. A face's area in square metres, on the face and in the bar. Labels stay on their measurements while you orbit, pan and zoom. While you measure, clicks do not pick elements. A section plane or box still cuts the model, and you can measure what it shows, cut faces included. Common mistakes The point lands on the face, not the corner. Snapping looks at the element under the pointer. Move closer to the corner, or zoom in. The area is smaller than expected. Openings are left out, and a face that bends is several faces: click each. Esc stopped measuring. The first Esc drops a half-made distance; the second stops. Next Cut an IFC model with a section plane Hide, isolate and see through elements"},{"slug":"issues-bcf","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Issues and BCF files","summary":"Save a view of the model as an issue — camera, picked elements, hidden ones, section — share it as a BCF file with other coordination tools, and open their BCF files to see their issues.","section":"tasks","order":29,"keywords":["bcf","bcfzip","issue","issues","topic","viewpoint","clash report","coordination","comment","snapshot","open bcf","save bcf","export","import"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoint at a problem in the model and keep the view that shows it, then hand it on in a BCF file, the format other coordination tools read and write.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and set up the view: orbit to it, pick the elements involved, hide what is in the way, cut it with a section plane or box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssues\u003C/span\u003E tab on the right, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew issue from this view\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a title, and a comment if you like; choose a status; give your name or e-mail. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECreate issue\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E on an issue to put its view back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave BCF\u003C/span\u003E to download every issue as one \u003Ccode\u003E.bcf\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo see issues someone else made, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen BCF…\u003C/span\u003E and choose their \u003Ccode\u003E.bcf\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.bcfzip\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach issue in the list with a picture of its view, its title, status, author and date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E puts back the camera, the picked elements, what was hidden (or the only things shown) and the section. Elements are found by their GlobalId in every open model; the status line says if some are not there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA section plane or box from another tool is shown when it lines up with the model&#039;s axes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file is BCF 2.1 and keeps the view in the model&#039;s own coordinates, so other tools put the camera in the same place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIssues stay in this tab until you save them. Opening another model keeps the list; open the model the issues are about before you show them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA parallel (orthogonal) camera from another tool is shown with this viewer&#039;s perspective camera, backed off so the same part of the model fills the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShow moved the camera somewhere odd.\u003C/strong\u003E The issue was made on another model or with other coordinates. Open the right model first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe file will not open.\u003C/strong\u003E It is not a BCF zip, or it is damaged; the status line says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Point at a problem in the model and keep the view that shows it, then hand it on in a BCF file, the format other coordination tools read and write. Steps Open an IFC model and set up the view: orbit to it, pick the elements involved, hide what is in the way, cut it with a section plane or box. Click the Issues tab on the right, then New issue from this view. Type a title, and a comment if you like; choose a status; give your name or e-mail. Click Create issue. Click Show on an issue to put its view back. Click Save BCF to download every issue as one .bcf file. To see issues someone else made, click Open BCF… and choose their .bcf or .bcfzip file. What you should see Each issue in the list with a picture of its view, its title, status, author and date. Show puts back the camera, the picked elements, what was hidden (or the only things shown) and the section. Elements are found by their GlobalId in every open model; the status line says if some are not there. A section plane or box from another tool is shown when it lines up with the model's axes. Good to know The file is BCF 2.1 and keeps the view in the model's own coordinates, so other tools put the camera in the same place. Issues stay in this tab until you save them. Opening another model keeps the list; open the model the issues are about before you show them. A parallel (orthogonal) camera from another tool is shown with this viewer's perspective camera, backed off so the same part of the model fills the view. Common mistakes Show moved the camera somewhere odd. The issue was made on another model or with other coordinates. Open the right model first. The file will not open. It is not a BCF zip, or it is damaged; the status line says why. Next Hide, isolate and see through elements Cut an IFC model with a section plane"},{"slug":"clash-check","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Check for clashes","summary":"Find where elements of one set run into elements of another — ducts through beams, walls into slabs — in one model or across several, with a tolerance, and turn a clash into an issue.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["clash","clash detection","clash check","hard clash","interference","collision","coordination","overlap","tolerance","mep","structure"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind the places where two sets of elements run into each other, for example everything in the services model against the structure, then look at each one and keep the ones that matter as issues.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model, and add the others with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model…\u003C/span\u003E if you are checking across models. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/several-models\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/several-models\"\u003ESee several IFC models together\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClash check\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAgainst\u003C/span\u003E, choose two sets: the elements you picked, a class (over every open model), a saved search set (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E), or everything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet how much overlap to ignore, in metres. 0.01 ignores elements that only just meet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The check runs in the background; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E stops it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a result to see it: the two elements are picked and shown, everything else see-through, and the view zooms to them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake an issue of it\u003C/span\u003E under a result to write it up in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIssues\u003C/span\u003E tab. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/issues-bcf\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/issues-bcf\"\u003EIssues and BCF files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe clashes listed with both elements&#039; names (and their files, when several models are open) and how deep they overlap, the deepest first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFaces that only touch, such as a wall standing on a slab or a door in its opening, are not clashes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis is a basic hard-clash check: elements whose surfaces cross. An element hidden wholly inside another (a pipe buried in a wall) is not found yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe depth is how far the two elements&#039; bounds overlap at the least: a guide, not an exact measurement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances and face areas in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Find the places where two sets of elements run into each other, for example everything in the services model against the structure, then look at each one and keep the ones that matter as issues. Steps Open an IFC model, and add the others with Add a model… if you are checking across models. See See several IFC models together. Open Clash check in the left panel. In Check and Against, choose two sets: the elements you picked, a class (over every open model), a saved search set (see Find elements by class, name or property), or everything. Set how much overlap to ignore, in metres. 0.01 ignores elements that only just meet. Click Run. The check runs in the background; Cancel stops it. Click a result to see it: the two elements are picked and shown, everything else see-through, and the view zooms to them. Click Make an issue of it under a result to write it up in the Issues tab. See Issues and BCF files. What you should see The clashes listed with both elements' names (and their files, when several models are open) and how deep they overlap, the deepest first. Faces that only touch, such as a wall standing on a slab or a door in its opening, are not clashes. Good to know This is a basic hard-clash check: elements whose surfaces cross. An element hidden wholly inside another (a pipe buried in a wall) is not found yet. The depth is how far the two elements' bounds overlap at the least: a guide, not an exact measurement. Next Measure distances and face areas in 3D Hide, isolate and see through elements"},{"slug":"inspect-element","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Pick an element and read its properties","summary":"Click any element in the 3D model to highlight it and read its name, type, tag, GlobalId, property sets and quantities.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["properties","pick","select","click","element","parameters","property sets","pset","quantities","globalid","guid","type","inspect"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"getting-at-hidden-elements","text":"Getting at hidden elements","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick elements in an IFC model and read everything the file says about them: attributes, property sets, type properties and quantities.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an element, for example a door. Click without dragging: a drag of more than a few pixels orbits instead of picking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the element: it is highlighted in blue.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right. At the top are the element&#039;s kind (such as &quot;Door&quot; or &quot;WallStandardCase&quot;) and its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the first block: \u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EObject type\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETag\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDescription\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EGlobalId\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ELine\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll down to the property sets. The first three are open; click the name of any other set to open it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick another element to switch to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click empty background, to clear the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe highlighted element and its data on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProperty sets in alphabetical order. Sets that come from the element&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Etype\u003C/strong\u003E have &quot; (type)&quot; after their name and the note &quot;Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EQuantity sets (such as lengths, areas and volumes) listed with the other sets. Quantities are read-only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EValues you can change look like buttons. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\"\u003EChange IFC properties and save an edited copy\u003C/a\u003E shows how.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/properties-panel\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/properties-panel\"\u003EProperties panel fields\u003C/a\u003E explains each field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"getting-at-hidden-elements\"\u003EGetting at hidden elements\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo reach something inside the building, untick the storeys above it in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo reach a wall behind a room volume, turn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the element.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDragging a little while clicking.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft treats that as an orbit and does not pick. Hold the mouse still.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting units next to numbers.\u003C/strong\u003E Values are shown as stored in the file, rounded to four decimals, without a unit symbol. Lengths in a metric export are usually millimeters or meters; check the exporter&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELooking for a parameter by its familiar name.\u003C/strong\u003E Exporters rename and regroup parameters into IFC property sets such as \u003Ccode\u003EPset_WallCommon\u003C/code\u003E. Look through each set; custom parameters often sit in a set named after the exporting program or your company.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting a search box.\u003C/strong\u003E Click to pick an element; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click picks more, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E picks every element shown. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E list on the left only counts elements by kind; it is not clickable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPicking on a project file card.\u003C/strong\u003E A file card has no elements to pick: &quot;Element properties need the 3D model. Open an IFC export of this file to see them.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Pick elements in an IFC model and read everything the file says about them: attributes, property sets, type properties and quantities. Steps Open an IFC model. Click an element, for example a door. Click without dragging: a drag of more than a few pixels orbits instead of picking. Look at the element: it is highlighted in blue. Look at the Properties panel on the right. At the top are the element's kind (such as \"Door\" or \"WallStandardCase\") and its name. Read the first block: Name, Type, Object type, Tag, Description, GlobalId and Line. Scroll down to the property sets. The first three are open; click the name of any other set to open it. Click another element to switch to it. Press Esc, or click empty background, to clear the selection. What you should see The highlighted element and its data on the right. Property sets in alphabetical order. Sets that come from the element's type have \" (type)\" after their name and the note \"Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.\" Quantity sets (such as lengths, areas and volumes) listed with the other sets. Quantities are read-only. Values you can change look like buttons. Change IFC properties and save an edited copy shows how. Properties panel fields explains each field. Getting at hidden elements To reach something inside the building, untick the storeys above it in the Storeys list. To reach a wall behind a room volume, turn Spaces off. Click the element. Common mistakes Dragging a little while clicking. BimCraft treats that as an orbit and does not pick. Hold the mouse still. Expecting units next to numbers. Values are shown as stored in the file, rounded to four decimals, without a unit symbol. Lengths in a metric export are usually millimeters or meters; check the exporter's settings. Looking for a parameter by its familiar name. Exporters rename and regroup parameters into IFC property sets such as Pset_WallCommon. Look through each set; custom parameters often sit in a set named after the exporting program or your company. Expecting a search box. Click to pick an element; Ctrl+click or Shift+click picks more, and Ctrl+A picks every element shown. The Elements list on the left only counts elements by kind; it is not clickable. Picking on a project file card. A file card has no elements to pick: \"Element properties need the 3D model. Open an IFC export of this file to see them.\""},{"slug":"walk","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Walk through a model","summary":"Walk through an IFC model at eye height with the keyboard, with gravity keeping you on the floor and walls stopping you.","section":"tasks","order":31,"keywords":["walk","walkthrough","first person","fly","navigate","eye height","gravity","collision","wasd","inside","tour"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee a model as someone standing in it: walk down a corridor, into a room, up a stair.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWalk\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the floor where you want to stand. You stand there with your eyes at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEye height\u003C/span\u003E (1.6 m to start), looking the way the view looked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the arrow keys. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to go faster. Drag in the view to look round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E to stop. The view stays where you were, ready to orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGravity\u003C/strong\u003E keeps you on the floor below you and takes you up steps and stairs. Turn it off to float: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rises, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sinks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStop at walls\u003C/strong\u003E stops you just short of walls, doors and anything else in the way, and lets you slide along them. Turn it off to walk through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHidden and see-through elements, and what a section cuts away, do not stop you and are not stood on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing happens when I press W.\u003C/strong\u003E The keys go to a box you are typing in: click in the view first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI fell a long way.\u003C/strong\u003E There was no floor under the spot (an opening, or the floor is hidden). Turn gravity off, or start on a floor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See a model as someone standing in it: walk down a corridor, into a room, up a stair. Steps Open an IFC model and click Walk in the toolbar. Click the floor where you want to stand. You stand there with your eyes at Eye height (1.6 m to start), looking the way the view looked. Move with W A S D or the arrow keys. Hold Shift to go faster. Drag in the view to look round. Press Esc or click Done to stop. The view stays where you were, ready to orbit. What you should see Gravity keeps you on the floor below you and takes you up steps and stairs. Turn it off to float: E or Page Up rises, Q or Page Down sinks. Stop at walls stops you just short of walls, doors and anything else in the way, and lets you slide along them. Turn it off to walk through. Hidden and see-through elements, and what a section cuts away, do not stop you and are not stood on. Common mistakes Nothing happens when I press W. The keys go to a box you are typing in: click in the view first. I fell a long way. There was no floor under the spot (an opening, or the floor is hidden). Turn gravity off, or start on a floor. Next Hide, isolate and see through elements Cut an IFC model with a section plane"},{"slug":"model-checks","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Check a model for common problems","summary":"Find elements missing a property you need, GlobalIds used twice, elements on no storey and copies stacked on top of each other.","section":"tasks","order":32,"keywords":["check","model check","validation","qa","quality","missing property","required","duplicate","globalid","guid","storey","stacked","overlap"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELook over a model before you hand it on: the problems that break schedules, quantities and other people&#039;s software.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel checks\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERequired properties\u003C/span\u003E, choose a class (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAny class\u003C/span\u003E) and a property every element of it must have. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a property\u003C/span\u003E for more. They stay in this browser for every model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun checks\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a result to pick those elements and zoom to them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMissing a required property\u003C/strong\u003E: each rule with the elements that have no value for it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGlobalId used twice\u003C/strong\u003E: elements sharing one GlobalId. Other software treats them as one element.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn no storey\u003C/strong\u003E: elements with a shape that no storey contains.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopies on top of each other\u003C/strong\u003E: elements of the same class and type with the same shape in the same place, which count twice in quantities.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChecks look at the active model when several are open. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/several-models\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/several-models\"\u003ESee several IFC models together\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter an edit, run the checks again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Look over a model before you hand it on: the problems that break schedules, quantities and other people's software. Steps Open an IFC model. Open Model checks in the left panel. Under Required properties, choose a class (or Any class) and a property every element of it must have. Click Add a property for more. They stay in this browser for every model. Click Run checks. Click a result to pick those elements and zoom to them. What you should see Missing a required property: each rule with the elements that have no value for it. GlobalId used twice: elements sharing one GlobalId. Other software treats them as one element. On no storey: elements with a shape that no storey contains. Copies on top of each other: elements of the same class and type with the same shape in the same place, which count twice in quantities. Good to know Checks look at the active model when several are open. See See several IFC models together. After an edit, run the checks again. Next Find elements by class, name or property Delete, cut, copy and paste elements"},{"slug":"quantity-totals","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Add up quantities, list elements and schedule spaces","summary":"Count elements and add up their lengths, areas, volumes or any number property, grouped by any property; list one row per element; or schedule the spaces with their areas and volumes. Each downloads as a CSV.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["quantity","quantities","takeoff","totals","sum","schedule","bill of quantities","boq","qto","csv","group by","count","length","area","volume","spaces","rooms","room schedule","area schedule","element list"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"one-row-per-element-or-the-spaces","text":"One row per element, or the spaces","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGet totals out of a model: wall lengths per storey, door counts per type, slab volume per material, the cost of every zone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity totals\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery element\u003C/span\u003E or only the picked ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup by\u003C/span\u003E, choose a property, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClass\u003C/span\u003E. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a group\u003C/span\u003E to split further, up to three, for example by \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStorey\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd up\u003C/span\u003E, tick the numbers to total. The model&#039;s quantity sets are ticked to start with.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E to open the table in a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA row per group with how many elements it has and each total, and a total row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements without a value are counted but add nothing. When that happens, the CSV adds a &quot;left out&quot; column next to the total so you can see how many.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENumbers are in the file&#039;s own units, as the file stores them, often millimetres for lengths.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"one-row-per-element-or-the-spaces\"\u003EOne row per element, or the spaces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne row per element\u003C/strong\u003E: each element on its own row, with the properties under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumns\u003C/span\u003E and the numbers under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd up\u003C/span\u003E. The CSV starts with each element&#039;s GlobalId. Click the first value in a row to pick that element.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpaces\u003C/strong\u003E: each room with its number, name, storey, area and volume, and their totals. Areas and volumes come from the space&#039;s quantities as the file states them. When the file has none, they are measured from the space&#039;s shape in m² and m³ and shown in italics; the CSV says which is which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/color-by-property\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/color-by-property\"\u003EColor elements by a property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Get totals out of a model: wall lengths per storey, door counts per type, slab volume per material, the cost of every zone. Steps Open an IFC model. Open Quantity totals in the left panel. In Count, choose Every element or only the picked ones. Under Group by, choose a property, such as Class. Click Add a group to split further, up to three, for example by Storey. Under Add up, tick the numbers to total. The model's quantity sets are ticked to start with. Click Download CSV to open the table in a spreadsheet. What you should see A row per group with how many elements it has and each total, and a total row. Elements without a value are counted but add nothing. When that happens, the CSV adds a \"left out\" column next to the total so you can see how many. Numbers are in the file's own units, as the file stores them, often millimetres for lengths. One row per element, or the spaces Change Show: One row per element: each element on its own row, with the properties under Columns and the numbers under Add up. The CSV starts with each element's GlobalId. Click the first value in a row to pick that element. Spaces: each room with its number, name, storey, area and volume, and their totals. Areas and volumes come from the space's quantities as the file states them. When the file has none, they are measured from the space's shape in m² and m³ and shown in italics; the CSV says which is which. Next Find elements by class, name or property Color elements by a property"},{"slug":"compare-versions","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Compare two versions of a model","summary":"Open two versions of the same IFC model and see which elements were added, removed and changed, matched by their GlobalIds, with a CSV of every change.","section":"tasks","order":34,"keywords":["compare","diff","difference","versions","revision","changes","changed","added","removed","moved","globalid","guid","what changed"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee what changed between the model you had and the one you were sent: which elements are new, which are gone, which moved or got new property values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the newer version, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a model…\u003C/span\u003E and choose the older one. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/several-models\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/several-models\"\u003ESee several IFC models together\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModels\u003C/span\u003E, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare versions\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOlder version\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENewer version\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an element in a list to pick it and see its properties. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E for every change, property by property.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E to go back to the normal view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdded\u003C/strong\u003E elements in blue, from the newer version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemoved\u003C/strong\u003E elements in red, from the older version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChanged\u003C/strong\u003E elements in ochre, from the newer version, each saying what changed: properties, a move with how far, or a new shape.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything that is the same, see-through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements are matched by their GlobalId. A program that gives elements new GlobalIds on every export makes every element look removed and added again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPositions are compared where the models sit in the scene. If one version was moved on purpose, line them up first with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModels\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA GlobalId used twice in one model is left out of the comparison. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-checks\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-checks\"\u003ECheck a model for common problems\u003C/a\u003E finds those.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/issues-bcf\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/issues-bcf\"\u003EIssues and BCF files\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/quantity-totals\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/quantity-totals\"\u003EAdd up quantities by property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See what changed between the model you had and the one you were sent: which elements are new, which are gone, which moved or got new property values. Steps Open the newer version, then click Add a model… and choose the older one. See See several IFC models together. In Models, open Compare versions. Choose the Older version and the Newer version. Click Compare. Click an element in a list to pick it and see its properties. Click Download CSV for every change, property by property. Click Clear to go back to the normal view. What you should see Added elements in blue, from the newer version. Removed elements in red, from the older version. Changed elements in ochre, from the newer version, each saying what changed: properties, a move with how far, or a new shape. Everything that is the same, see-through. Good to know Elements are matched by their GlobalId. A program that gives elements new GlobalIds on every export makes every element look removed and added again. Positions are compared where the models sit in the scene. If one version was moved on purpose, line them up first with Move in Models. A GlobalId used twice in one model is left out of the comparison. Check a model for common problems finds those. Next Issues and BCF files Add up quantities by property"},{"slug":"open-in-another-app","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Open a file in another app","summary":"Hand the drawing or model you have open to another app in the lineup in a new tab — a drawing to the drafting app, a model to the detailing app — without uploading anything.","section":"tasks","order":35,"keywords":["handoff","hand off","open in","edit in","another app","new tab","send to","detailing","drafting","cadcraft","detailcraft"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECarry on with a file in the app made for the next job, without saving it and opening it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing to edit it in the drafting app, or an IFC model to detail it in the detailing app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E on the 2D view of a drawing, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen in DetailCraft\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar of a model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe other app opens in a new tab with the file already open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file in the new tab. BimCraft keeps it open too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA model with unsaved changes goes over with those changes in it. Your own copy is not saved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA drawing goes over without its linked drawings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file passes between the two tabs inside your browser. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA button only shows when the other app is open where you are and can take a file this way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf nothing opens, the browser blocked the new tab: allow pop-ups for this site and click again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/view-dwg\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/view-dwg\"\u003EView a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/files\"\u003EFiles BimCraft opens and saves\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Carry on with a file in the app made for the next job, without saving it and opening it again. Steps Open a DWG or DXF drawing to edit it in the drafting app, or an IFC model to detail it in the detailing app. Click Edit in CadCraft on the 2D view of a drawing, or Open in DetailCraft in the toolbar of a model. The other app opens in a new tab with the file already open. What you should see The file in the new tab. BimCraft keeps it open too. A model with unsaved changes goes over with those changes in it. Your own copy is not saved. A drawing goes over without its linked drawings. Good to know The file passes between the two tabs inside your browser. Nothing is uploaded. A button only shows when the other app is open where you are and can take a file this way. If nothing opens, the browser blocked the new tab: allow pop-ups for this site and click again. Next View a DWG or DXF drawing Files BimCraft opens and saves"},{"slug":"4d-schedule","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Play a construction schedule on the model","summary":"Open a task list as a CSV, link each task to elements by a property, and move through the dates to see what is built, in progress and not started yet.","section":"tasks","order":36,"keywords":["4d","schedule","construction sequence","timeline","phasing","programme","gantt","tasks","dates","simulation","progress","built","csv"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"the-task-list","text":"The task list","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWatch the building go up: each task in your schedule linked to the elements it builds, shown day by day.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E4D schedule\u003C/span\u003E in the left panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload a template\u003C/span\u003E for a task list with a week per storey, and change it in a spreadsheet. Or write your own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a task list…\u003C/span\u003E and choose the CSV.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the date slider, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-task-list\"\u003EThe task list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA CSV with a header row and the columns \u003Cstrong\u003ETask\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EStart\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFinish\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EField\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EValue\u003C/strong\u003E. Commas or semicolons both work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETask\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStart\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFinish\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EValue\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELevel 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-02\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-13\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStorey\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELevel 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoof\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-16\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClass\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIfcRoof\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERoof\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-16\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2026-11-20\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPset_WallCommon.IsExternal\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWrite dates as YYYY-MM-DD.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA row links the task to every element whose \u003Cstrong\u003EField\u003C/strong\u003E has exactly that \u003Cstrong\u003EValue\u003C/strong\u003E. The field is a property such as Storey, Class, Name, Type, Material, Tag or GlobalId, or any property written as its set and name: \u003Ccode\u003EPset_WallCommon.FireRating\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERepeat a task on more rows, with the same dates, to link more elements to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBuilt\u003C/strong\u003E: elements whose tasks are all finished, in their own colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn progress\u003C/strong\u003E: elements with a task under way that day, in ochre. A task is under way from its start date through its finish date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot started\u003C/strong\u003E: see-through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EElements in no task stay as they are. The list shows how many elements each task found. Rows that could not be read, or found nothing, are listed with their line numbers.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/color-by-property\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/color-by-property\"\u003EColor elements by a property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Watch the building go up: each task in your schedule linked to the elements it builds, shown day by day. Steps Open an IFC model and open 4D schedule in the left panel. Click Download a template for a task list with a week per storey, and change it in a spreadsheet. Or write your own. Click Open a task list… and choose the CSV. Drag the date slider, or click Play. The task list A CSV with a header row and the columns Task, Start, Finish, Field and Value. Commas or semicolons both work. Task Start Finish Field Value Level 1 2026-11-02 2026-11-13 Storey Level 1 Roof 2026-11-16 2026-11-20 Class IfcRoof Roof 2026-11-16 2026-11-20 Pset_WallCommon.IsExternal Yes Write dates as YYYY-MM-DD. A row links the task to every element whose Field has exactly that Value. The field is a property such as Storey, Class, Name, Type, Material, Tag or GlobalId, or any property written as its set and name: Pset_WallCommon.FireRating. Repeat a task on more rows, with the same dates, to link more elements to it. What you should see Built: elements whose tasks are all finished, in their own colors. In progress: elements with a task under way that day, in ochre. A task is under way from its start date through its finish date. Not started: see-through. Elements in no task stay as they are. The list shows how many elements each task found. Rows that could not be read, or found nothing, are listed with their line numbers. Next Find elements by class, name or property Color elements by a property"},{"slug":"edit-ifc-properties","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Change IFC properties and save an edited copy","summary":"Change an element's name, tag, description or a property value, undo, and download a new IFC file while the original stays as it was.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["edit","change","properties","parameters","tag","name","rename","mark","save as","download","undo","copy","ifc","write"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"what-you-can-change","text":"What you can change","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"kinds-of-values","text":"Kinds of values","level":2},{"id":"undo","text":"Undo","level":2},{"id":"why-your-edit-changes-only-one-element","text":"Why your edit changes only one element","level":2},{"id":"warnings-before-you-lose-work","text":"Warnings before you lose work","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECorrect data in an IFC model: rename an element, fix its tag, change a property value. Then save a new IFC file with your changes. The file you opened is never touched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-can-change\"\u003EWhat you can change\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEditable\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName, Object type, Tag, Description\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESingle property values (text, numbers, Yes/No)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType properties (sets marked &quot;(type)&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, and the change applies to every element of that type\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType, GlobalId, Line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuantities (lengths, areas, volumes, counts)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELists, enumerations and other multi-part values\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGeometry, position, adding or deleting elements\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and click the element you want to change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, click the value you want to change. It turns into a text box. An empty value shows as \u003Cem\u003Eempty\u003C/em\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click somewhere else, to keep it. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to cancel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the value: it is now bold with a blue bar beside it, which marks an edited value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E section on the left: the change is listed as \u003Cem\u003Eelement\u003C/em\u003E: \u003Cem\u003Efield\u003C/em\u003E, old value to new value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERepeat for other values or other elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E (it shows how many changes are waiting, for example &quot;Save as IFC (3)&quot;), or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind &quot;\u003Cem\u003Eoriginal name\u003C/em\u003E (edited).ifc&quot; in your downloads folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status line says &quot;Saved \u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E (edited).ifc with \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E changes (… bytes). Your original file is unchanged.&quot; The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list empties, ready for the next round.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"kinds-of-values\"\u003EKinds of values\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText\u003C/strong\u003E: anything you type is kept as is. Clearing Name, Object type, Tag or Description removes the value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENumbers\u003C/strong\u003E: type a plain number, such as \u003Ccode\u003E2.4\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1200\u003C/code\u003E, in the file&#039;s own units. No unit symbols. Anything else shows &quot;Use a number.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhole numbers\u003C/strong\u003E (counts, integers): &quot;Use a whole number.&quot; if you type a decimal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYes/No\u003C/strong\u003E: the value becomes a menu with Yes and No.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach value keeps its IFC type, so a number stays a number and other programs read it correctly.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo\"\u003EUndo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E section, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you are not typing in a field.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERepeat to undo more. Each undo removes the newest change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou cannot undo past a save: after saving, the list starts again. The saved file keeps everything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"why-your-edit-changes-only-one-element\"\u003EWhy your edit changes only one element\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExporters save space by sharing data. One property set may be shared by twenty doors, and one value (&quot;Fire rating: 60 min&quot;) may be listed in ninety sets. If BimCraft simply changed the shared value, every element that shares it would change too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESo BimCraft copies before it writes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the property set you edit is shared with other elements, your element gets its own copy of the set, and only the copy changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the value is shared with other sets, your set gets its own copy of the value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe neighbors keep their old data. This is called \u003Cem\u003Ecopy-on-write\u003C/em\u003E. It adds a few small entries to the file, which every IFC program reads normally.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EType properties are the exception on purpose.\u003C/strong\u003E A set marked &quot;(type)&quot; belongs to the element&#039;s type, and in IFC every element of that type reads it from there. Changing it changes the type, so every element of the type shows the new value. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list marks such edits with &quot;(type)&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/type-vs-instance\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/type-vs-instance\"\u003EChange a type property for every element of a type\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"warnings-before-you-lose-work\"\u003EWarnings before you lose work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening another file while you have unsaved changes asks &quot;You have \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E unsaved changes to \u003Cem\u003Efile\u003C/em\u003E. Discard them?&quot; Click Cancel to keep working, then save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClosing or reloading the tab with unsaved changes makes the browser ask whether to leave.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyping units.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E2400 mm\u003C/code\u003E is refused. Type \u003Ccode\u003E2400\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChanging a type property when you meant one element.\u003C/strong\u003E Look for the &quot;(type)&quot; note before you type. If you want one element to differ, change an instance property or its Tag or Name instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting the original file to change.\u003C/strong\u003E Saving always downloads a new file. Replace the original yourself if that is what you want, and keep a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with no changes.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft only saves when there is something to save; otherwise the browser&#039;s own &quot;Save page&quot; may open. Close it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving twice and expecting two small files.\u003C/strong\u003E Each save holds every edit since you opened the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting edits to go back into a project file.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft writes IFC only. Bring the edited IFC into your authoring program the way you import any IFC.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Correct data in an IFC model: rename an element, fix its tag, change a property value. Then save a new IFC file with your changes. The file you opened is never touched. What you can change Field Editable Name, Object type, Tag, Description Yes Single property values (text, numbers, Yes/No) Yes Type properties (sets marked \"(type)\") Yes, and the change applies to every element of that type Type, GlobalId, Line No Quantities (lengths, areas, volumes, counts) No Lists, enumerations and other multi-part values No Geometry, position, adding or deleting elements No Steps Open an IFC model and click the element you want to change. In the Properties panel, click the value you want to change. It turns into a text box. An empty value shows as empty. Type the new value. Press Enter, or click somewhere else, to keep it. Press Esc to cancel. Look at the value: it is now bold with a blue bar beside it, which marks an edited value. Look at the Changes section on the left: the change is listed as element: field, old value to new value. Repeat for other values or other elements. Click Save as IFC (it shows how many changes are waiting, for example \"Save as IFC (3)\"), or press Ctrl+S. Find \"original name (edited).ifc\" in your downloads folder. What you should see The status line says \"Saved name (edited).ifc with n changes (… bytes). Your original file is unchanged.\" The Changes list empties, ready for the next round. Kinds of values Text: anything you type is kept as is. Clearing Name, Object type, Tag or Description removes the value. Numbers: type a plain number, such as 2.4 or 1200, in the file's own units. No unit symbols. Anything else shows \"Use a number.\" Whole numbers (counts, integers): \"Use a whole number.\" if you type a decimal. Yes/No: the value becomes a menu with Yes and No. Each value keeps its IFC type, so a number stays a number and other programs read it correctly. Undo Click Undo in the Changes section, or press Ctrl+Z while you are not typing in a field. Repeat to undo more. Each undo removes the newest change. You cannot undo past a save: after saving, the list starts again. The saved file keeps everything. Why your edit changes only one element Exporters save space by sharing data. One property set may be shared by twenty doors, and one value (\"Fire rating: 60 min\") may be listed in ninety sets. If BimCraft simply changed the shared value, every element that shares it would change too. So BimCraft copies before it writes: If the property set you edit is shared with other elements, your element gets its own copy of the set, and only the copy changes. If the value is shared with other sets, your set gets its own copy of the value. The neighbors keep their old data. This is called copy-on-write. It adds a few small entries to the file, which every IFC program reads normally. Type properties are the exception on purpose. A set marked \"(type)\" belongs to the element's type, and in IFC every element of that type reads it from there. Changing it changes the type, so every element of the type shows the new value. The Changes list marks such edits with \"(type)\". See Change a type property for every element of a type. Warnings before you lose work Opening another file while you have unsaved changes asks \"You have n unsaved changes to file. Discard them?\" Click Cancel to keep working, then save. Closing or reloading the tab with unsaved changes makes the browser ask whether to leave. Common mistakes Typing units. 2400 mm is refused. Type 2400. Changing a type property when you meant one element. Look for the \"(type)\" note before you type. If you want one element to differ, change an instance property or its Tag or Name instead. Expecting the original file to change. Saving always downloads a new file. Replace the original yourself if that is what you want, and keep a copy. Pressing Ctrl+S with no changes. BimCraft only saves when there is something to save; otherwise the browser's own \"Save page\" may open. Close it. Saving twice and expecting two small files. Each save holds every edit since you opened the model. Expecting edits to go back into a project file. BimCraft writes IFC only. Bring the edited IFC into your authoring program the way you import any IFC."},{"slug":"type-vs-instance","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Change a type property for every element of a type","summary":"Tell type properties from instance properties, change a value on the type so every element follows, and check that one element's own edit stays its own.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["type","instance","type properties","family type","shared","copy on write","all elements","bulk edit","manufacturer","model"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"type-or-instance","text":"Type or instance?","level":2},{"id":"steps-change-the-type","text":"Steps: change the type","level":2},{"id":"steps-change-one-element-only","text":"Steps: change one element only","level":2},{"id":"save","text":"Save","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet the manufacturer on a door type so every door of that type shows it, then give one door its own fire rating without touching the others. This shows the difference between type and instance properties in BimCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-or-instance\"\u003EType or instance?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn \u003Cstrong\u003Einstance\u003C/strong\u003E property belongs to one element: its tag, its mark, its room, its own fire rating.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Etype\u003C/strong\u003E property belongs to the type that many elements share: manufacturer, model, width of a door type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, sets that come from the type have &quot; (type)&quot; after their name and the line &quot;Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.&quot; Every other set belongs to the element itself. \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E in the first block shows the type&#039;s name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-change-the-type\"\u003ESteps: change the type\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model and click a door.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENote its \u003Cstrong\u003EType\u003C/strong\u003E in the first block of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel, for example &quot;Single-Flush 900 x 2100&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll to a set whose name ends in &quot;(type)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the value you want to change, for example \u003Cstrong\u003EManufacturer\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list: the entry ends with &quot;(type)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick another door of the same type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the same value: it shows the new value too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-change-one-element-only\"\u003ESteps: change one element only\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one door.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a set without &quot;(type)&quot; in its name, for example \u003Ccode\u003EPset_DoorCommon\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a value, such as \u003Cstrong\u003EFireRating\u003C/strong\u003E, type a new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the door next to it, of the same type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the same value: it still shows the old value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEven if the exporter shared that property set between both doors, BimCraft gave the first door its own copy before changing it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties#why-your-edit-changes-only-one-element\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\" data-hash=\"why-your-edit-changes-only-one-element\"\u003EWhy your edit changes only one element\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save\"\u003ESave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the saved copy in BimCraft, or in any IFC viewer, to check both changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot every model has types.\u003C/strong\u003E Some exporters write no type objects, or put everything on the instance. Then there are no &quot;(type)&quot; sets, and each element must be changed on its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe same value appears in a type set and an instance set.\u003C/strong\u003E Some exporters write a parameter in both places. Other programs may read either one; change both if you need them to agree.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting to pick &quot;all doors of this type&quot; and edit them together.\u003C/strong\u003E There is no multi-select in this build. A type property is the way to change many elements at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Set the manufacturer on a door type so every door of that type shows it, then give one door its own fire rating without touching the others. This shows the difference between type and instance properties in BimCraft. Type or instance? An instance property belongs to one element: its tag, its mark, its room, its own fire rating. A type property belongs to the type that many elements share: manufacturer, model, width of a door type. In the Properties panel, sets that come from the type have \" (type)\" after their name and the line \"Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.\" Every other set belongs to the element itself. Type in the first block shows the type's name. Steps: change the type Open an IFC model and click a door. Note its Type in the first block of the Properties panel, for example \"Single-Flush 900 x 2100\". Scroll to a set whose name ends in \"(type)\". Click the value you want to change, for example Manufacturer. Type the new value and press Enter. Look at the Changes list: the entry ends with \"(type)\". Click another door of the same type. Check the same value: it shows the new value too. Steps: change one element only Click one door. Open a set without \"(type)\" in its name, for example Pset_DoorCommon. Click a value, such as FireRating, type a new value and press Enter. Click the door next to it, of the same type. Check the same value: it still shows the old value. Even if the exporter shared that property set between both doors, BimCraft gave the first door its own copy before changing it. See Why your edit changes only one element. Save Click Save as IFC or press Ctrl+S. Open the saved copy in BimCraft, or in any IFC viewer, to check both changes. Common mistakes Not every model has types. Some exporters write no type objects, or put everything on the instance. Then there are no \"(type)\" sets, and each element must be changed on its own. The same value appears in a type set and an instance set. Some exporters write a parameter in both places. Other programs may read either one; change both if you need them to agree. Expecting to pick \"all doors of this type\" and edit them together. There is no multi-select in this build. A type property is the way to change many elements at once."},{"slug":"assistants","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Let an AI assistant work with your model","summary":"Turn on assistants in Settings so an assistant on your computer can find elements, read their properties and isolate them in the view, through CadCraft Studio. Off until you turn it on.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["ai","assistant","claude","mcp","script","automation","studio","find elements","isolate","properties","permissions","disconnect","activity"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-do-in-bimcraft","text":"What an assistant can do in BimCraft","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"while-an-assistant-is-connected","text":"While an assistant is connected","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-off","text":"Turn it off","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (Claude, or another assistant that speaks MCP) look into the IFC model you have open, what it can and cannot do, and how to stop it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-do-in-bimcraft\"\u003EWhat an assistant can do in BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt asks to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat happens\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENeeds\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFind elements\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt gets a list of elements by class, storey, name or property value, as \u003Cstrong\u003EFind\u003C/strong\u003E does\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead properties\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt gets one element&#039;s attributes, type, property sets and quantities, as \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E shows them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIsolate, see-through, hide or show\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe 3D view changes as if you had used the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow\u003C/span\u003E menu\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange the view\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn assistant never changes the model itself: it cannot delete, move, add or save anything in BimCraft, and it cannot open files. You open the model; it works with the one that is open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants reach BimCraft through CadCraft Studio on your computer; the plain browser has no way in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E and choose the \u003Cstrong\u003EBimCraft\u003C/strong\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EAssistants\u003C/strong\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked for you. Tick \u003Cstrong\u003EChange the view\u003C/strong\u003E too if the assistant may isolate and hide elements.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EConnect the assistant in Studio (Studio shows the address and the settings to paste into it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"while-an-assistant-is-connected\"\u003EWhile an assistant is connected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA note at the top of the right panel says which assistant is connected and what it can do. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDisconnect\u003C/span\u003E to end its session at once; it has to connect again to continue.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity\u003C/strong\u003E, under \u003Cstrong\u003EAssistants\u003C/strong\u003E in Settings, lists every call the assistant made, newest first, with whether it worked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAsk in plain words, for example &quot;isolate the external walls on Level 1&quot; or &quot;which doors have no fire rating?&quot;. The assistant finds the elements and shows them in your view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-off\"\u003ETurn it off\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUntick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. Every assistant is disconnected straight away, and the setting is kept for next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-also\"\u003ESee also\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (Claude, or another assistant that speaks MCP) look into the IFC model you have open, what it can and cannot do, and how to stop it. What an assistant can do in BimCraft It asks to What happens Needs Find elements It gets a list of elements by class, storey, name or property value, as Find does Read Read properties It gets one element's attributes, type, property sets and quantities, as Properties shows them Read Isolate, see-through, hide or show The 3D view changes as if you had used the Show menu Change the view An assistant never changes the model itself: it cannot delete, move, add or save anything in BimCraft, and it cannot open files. You open the model; it works with the one that is open. Turn it on Assistants reach BimCraft through CadCraft Studio on your computer; the plain browser has no way in. Open Settings and choose the BimCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft. Read is ticked for you. Tick Change the view too if the assistant may isolate and hide elements. Connect the assistant in Studio (Studio shows the address and the settings to paste into it). While an assistant is connected A note at the top of the right panel says which assistant is connected and what it can do. Click Disconnect to end its session at once; it has to connect again to continue. Activity, under Assistants in Settings, lists every call the assistant made, newest first, with whether it worked. Ask in plain words, for example \"isolate the external walls on Level 1\" or \"which doors have no fire rating?\". The assistant finds the elements and shows them in your view. Turn it off Untick Let AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft. Every assistant is disconnected straight away, and the setting is kept for next time. See also Find elements by class, name or property Hide, isolate and see through elements BimCraft settings"},{"slug":"read-rvt-file","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Read a project or family file","summary":"Open an .rvt, .rfa, .rte or .rft file and read its release, worksharing, project information, family parameters, links and preview picture, without the program that made it.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["rvt","rfa","rte","rft","project file","family","release","version","which version","build","worksharing","central","project information","preview","thumbnail","links"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"why-there-is-no-3d","text":"Why there is no 3D","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind out what is in a project or family file without opening it in Revit: which release saved it, whether it is workshared, the project information, and what it links to. Useful when a file arrives from someone else and you need to know what release to open it in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose an \u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait a moment. The status line says &quot;Reading the file…&quot; and then &quot;Opened \u003Cem\u003Efile name\u003C/em\u003E&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the top of the card: the preview picture saved in the file, the kind of file and the release it was saved in, and the project name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll down to read the sections of the card.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe card in the middle, with these sections when the file holds them:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESection\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it tells you\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile name, size, kind (project, project template, family or family template), the release and build it was saved in, worksharing, the central model&#039;s path, where it was last saved, who saved it, locale and document GUID\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject information\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s own fields, such as name, number, client, address and status. Fields still at their new-project defaults are left out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamily, Family: …\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFor a family: its name, category, release, last update and its parameters, group by group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinked and referenced files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery linked model, CAD link, image and keynote table the file points to, with its saved path\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the left, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel repeats the key facts and adds \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E: how much of the file BimCraft actually read, usually about 40 KB of a file many megabytes in size. On the right, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel says element properties need the 3D model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"why-there-is-no-3d\"\u003EWhy there is no 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D model and the elements inside a project file are stored in an undocumented format. BimCraft reads only the parts of the file that are documented or plainly readable: the file information, project information, family data, the link list and the preview picture. It does not guess at the rest. To see the model in 3D and read element properties, export IFC from the program that made the file. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\"\u003EGet a project file into 3D through IFC\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting 3D.\u003C/strong\u003E See above.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinks show only their saved path.\u003C/strong\u003E A file opened on its own cannot check whether its links exist. Open the folder it lives in instead, and BimCraft marks each link as found or not: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This file is incomplete…&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is still &quot;online only&quot; does this too. BimCraft still opens it and shows only what was recovered: an amber notice at the top of the card, a \u003Cstrong\u003ERecovered from this file\u003C/strong\u003E section listing the parts whose every byte is in the file, and \u003Cstrong\u003ENot in the file\u003C/strong\u003E for the rest. Nothing is filled in for the missing part, so the release, preview picture or project information can be missing when they were stored past the cut. If even the file&#039;s list of contents was cut off, nothing can be opened. Copy the original again and reopen it for the full file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No preview picture in this file&quot;.\u003C/strong\u003E Some files are saved without one. That is normal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a backup by mistake.\u003C/strong\u003E Files named like \u003Ccode\u003ETower.0004.rvt\u003C/code\u003E are numbered backups. Opening the folder groups them with their model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Find out what is in a project or family file without opening it in Revit: which release saved it, whether it is workshared, the project information, and what it links to. Useful when a file arrives from someone else and you need to know what release to open it in. Steps Click Choose a file. Choose an .rvt, .rfa, .rte or .rft file. Wait a moment. The status line says \"Reading the file…\" and then \"Opened file name\". Look at the top of the card: the preview picture saved in the file, the kind of file and the release it was saved in, and the project name. Scroll down to read the sections of the card. What you should see The card in the middle, with these sections when the file holds them: Section What it tells you File File name, size, kind (project, project template, family or family template), the release and build it was saved in, worksharing, the central model's path, where it was last saved, who saved it, locale and document GUID Project information The project's own fields, such as name, number, client, address and status. Fields still at their new-project defaults are left out. Family, Family: … For a family: its name, category, release, last update and its parameters, group by group Linked and referenced files Every linked model, CAD link, image and keynote table the file points to, with its saved path On the left, the File panel repeats the key facts and adds Read: how much of the file BimCraft actually read, usually about 40 KB of a file many megabytes in size. On the right, the Properties panel says element properties need the 3D model. Why there is no 3D The 3D model and the elements inside a project file are stored in an undocumented format. BimCraft reads only the parts of the file that are documented or plainly readable: the file information, project information, family data, the link list and the preview picture. It does not guess at the rest. To see the model in 3D and read element properties, export IFC from the program that made the file. See Get a project file into 3D through IFC. Common mistakes Expecting 3D. See above. Links show only their saved path. A file opened on its own cannot check whether its links exist. Open the folder it lives in instead, and BimCraft marks each link as found or not: Open a whole project folder. \"This file is incomplete…\" The file was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is still \"online only\" does this too. BimCraft still opens it and shows only what was recovered: an amber notice at the top of the card, a Recovered from this file section listing the parts whose every byte is in the file, and Not in the file for the rest. Nothing is filled in for the missing part, so the release, preview picture or project information can be missing when they were stored past the cut. If even the file's list of contents was cut off, nothing can be opened. Copy the original again and reopen it for the full file. \"No preview picture in this file\". Some files are saved without one. That is normal. Opening a backup by mistake. Files named like Tower.0004.rvt are numbered backups. Opening the folder groups them with their model."},{"slug":"rvt-to-ifc","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Get a project file into 3D through IFC","summary":"Export IFC from the program that made a project file, open the IFC in BimCraft, and know what the export keeps.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["export","ifc export","rvt to ifc","convert","3d","geometry","ifc2x3","ifc4","coordination view","reference view"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"what-the-export-keeps-and-what-it-does-not","text":"What the export keeps, and what it does not","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETurn a project file into an IFC file you can orbit, inspect and edit in BimCraft. The export happens in the program that made the file; BimCraft opens the result.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the project in the program that made it. In Revit, that is the release shown on BimCraft&#039;s card (&quot;saved in Revit …&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a 3D view that shows what you want to export. Many exporters export only what that view shows when asked to.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EFile &gt; Export &gt; IFC\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the IFC version. IFC2X3 Coordination View 2.0 and IFC4 Reference View both open in BimCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a folder and file name, and export.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn BimCraft, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E and choose the new \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe model in 3D, with storeys taken from the project&#039;s levels and the project information in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel. \u003Cstrong\u003EExported from\u003C/strong\u003E names the exporter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-export-keeps-and-what-it-does-not\"\u003EWhat the export keeps, and what it does not\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKept\u003C/strong\u003E: geometry, storeys, element kinds, names, types, most parameters (as IFC property sets), quantities if you ask the exporter for them, and rooms as spaces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot kept\u003C/strong\u003E: views, sheets, annotation, families as editable families, worksets and design options. An IFC file is a snapshot of the model&#039;s elements, not the project.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParameters missing in BimCraft.\u003C/strong\u003E The exporter decides which parameters go into IFC. Look for options such as &quot;Export Revit property sets&quot;, &quot;Export IFC common property sets&quot; and &quot;Export base quantities&quot;, and export again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERooms missing.\u003C/strong\u003E Turn on the exporter&#039;s option to export rooms or spaces, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E in BimCraft to see them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving as \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft opens plain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E only. Choose plain IFC, or unzip the \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModel far from the origin.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft centers the model when it opens, so a model placed far away still appears in the middle of the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting edits to flow back.\u003C/strong\u003E Edits you save in BimCraft go into a new IFC file, not into the project file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Turn a project file into an IFC file you can orbit, inspect and edit in BimCraft. The export happens in the program that made the file; BimCraft opens the result. Steps Open the project in the program that made it. In Revit, that is the release shown on BimCraft's card (\"saved in Revit …\"). Open a 3D view that shows what you want to export. Many exporters export only what that view shows when asked to. Choose File \u003E Export \u003E IFC. Pick the IFC version. IFC2X3 Coordination View 2.0 and IFC4 Reference View both open in BimCraft. Choose a folder and file name, and export. In BimCraft, click Choose a file and choose the new .ifc file. What you should see The model in 3D, with storeys taken from the project's levels and the project information in the File panel. Exported from names the exporter. What the export keeps, and what it does not Kept: geometry, storeys, element kinds, names, types, most parameters (as IFC property sets), quantities if you ask the exporter for them, and rooms as spaces. Not kept: views, sheets, annotation, families as editable families, worksets and design options. An IFC file is a snapshot of the model's elements, not the project. Common mistakes Parameters missing in BimCraft. The exporter decides which parameters go into IFC. Look for options such as \"Export Revit property sets\", \"Export IFC common property sets\" and \"Export base quantities\", and export again. Rooms missing. Turn on the exporter's option to export rooms or spaces, then click Spaces in BimCraft to see them. Saving as .ifczip. BimCraft opens plain .ifc only. Choose plain IFC, or unzip the .ifczip. Model far from the origin. BimCraft centers the model when it opens, so a model placed far away still appears in the middle of the screen. Expecting edits to flow back. Edits you save in BimCraft go into a new IFC file, not into the project file."},{"slug":"pln-project-files","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Open .pln and .pla project files","summary":"What BimCraft does with .pln, .pla, .tpl and .bpn files today, and how to export IFC and DWG from them so the model and drawings open here.","section":"tasks","order":72,"keywords":["pln","pla","tpl","bpn","project file","archive","template","backup","ifc export","dwg export","translator","zones","layouts"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"what-opens-today","text":"What opens today","level":2},{"id":"export-ifc-the-model","text":"Export IFC (the model)","level":2},{"id":"export-dwg-the-drawings","text":"Export DWG (the drawings)","level":2},{"id":"troubleshooting","text":"Troubleshooting","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGet a project saved as \u003Ccode\u003E.pln\u003C/code\u003E (project), \u003Ccode\u003E.pla\u003C/code\u003E (archive with its library), \u003Ccode\u003E.tpl\u003C/code\u003E (template) or \u003Ccode\u003E.bpn\u003C/code\u003E (backup) into BimCraft. These are Archicad&#039;s own files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-opens-today\"\u003EWhat opens today\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe project files themselves do not open yet.\u003C/strong\u003E Their format is not published, and BimCraft does not read it yet. When you open one, BimCraft says so and points here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn IFC export opens fully\u003C/strong\u003E: the model in 3D, storeys, zones as spaces, element data, property sets and classifications, with every BimCraft tool.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA DWG export opens\u003C/strong\u003E in the 2D view, with its layers and layouts, and in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E. To edit it, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-ifc-the-model\"\u003EExport IFC (the model)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the project in Archicad.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EFile &gt; Save As\u003C/strong\u003E, and pick \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC\u003C/strong\u003E as the file type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick an IFC translator (Archicad&#039;s name for a set of IFC export settings). One for \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC4 Reference View\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC2X3 Coordination View 2.0\u003C/strong\u003E suits BimCraft. IFC4X3 opens too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what to export: the entire project, or only the elements visible on the stories you are looking at.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E in BimCraft and choose the \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECheck these in the translator&#039;s settings before you export:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZones\u003C/strong\u003E: export them, so rooms arrive as spaces. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E in BimCraft to see them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProperties and classifications\u003C/strong\u003E: export them, so they show in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E and can be used in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuantity totals\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, not \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E. BimCraft opens plain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-dwg-the-drawings\"\u003EExport DWG (the drawings)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the plan, section or layout you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cstrong\u003EFile &gt; Save As\u003C/strong\u003E, and pick \u003Cstrong\u003EDWG\u003C/strong\u003E as the file type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a DXF-DWG translator, and save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a whole set of sheets, save the layouts from the Layout Book, or publish them as DWG.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E in BimCraft. Drawings linked by the export open with it when they are in the same folder; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\"\u003ELinked drawings and pictures\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"troubleshooting\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… is a project file BimCraft does not read yet.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Export IFC or DWG as above, and open that file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe model opens without rooms.\u003C/strong\u003E Zones were not exported. Turn them on in the IFC translator and export again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpenings are not cut.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft says so under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel. Export again; if it stays, open the model in the program that made it and check the openings there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText on the DWG looks different.\u003C/strong\u003E The export may keep fonts BimCraft does not have; BimCraft draws text in a plain sans-serif font.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\"\u003EGet a project file into 3D through IFC\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/file-formats\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/file-formats\"\u003EWhat BimCraft reads from each format\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Get a project saved as .pln (project), .pla (archive with its library), .tpl (template) or .bpn (backup) into BimCraft. These are Archicad's own files. What opens today The project files themselves do not open yet. Their format is not published, and BimCraft does not read it yet. When you open one, BimCraft says so and points here. An IFC export opens fully: the model in 3D, storeys, zones as spaces, element data, property sets and classifications, with every BimCraft tool. A DWG export opens in the 2D view, with its layers and layouts, and in 3D. To edit it, use Edit in CadCraft. Export IFC (the model) Open the project in Archicad. Choose File \u003E Save As, and pick IFC as the file type. Pick an IFC translator (Archicad's name for a set of IFC export settings). One for IFC4 Reference View or IFC2X3 Coordination View 2.0 suits BimCraft. IFC4X3 opens too. Choose what to export: the entire project, or only the elements visible on the stories you are looking at. Save, then click Choose a file in BimCraft and choose the .ifc file. Check these in the translator's settings before you export: Zones: export them, so rooms arrive as spaces. Click Spaces in BimCraft to see them. Properties and classifications: export them, so they show in Properties and can be used in Find, Color and Quantity totals. Plain .ifc, not .ifczip or .ifcxml. BimCraft opens plain .ifc only. Export DWG (the drawings) Open the plan, section or layout you want. Choose File \u003E Save As, and pick DWG as the file type. Pick a DXF-DWG translator, and save. For a whole set of sheets, save the layouts from the Layout Book, or publish them as DWG. Open the .dwg in BimCraft. Drawings linked by the export open with it when they are in the same folder; see Linked drawings and pictures. Troubleshooting \"… is a project file BimCraft does not read yet.\" Export IFC or DWG as above, and open that file. The model opens without rooms. Zones were not exported. Turn them on in the IFC translator and export again. Openings are not cut. BimCraft says so under Not drawn yet in the File panel. Export again; if it stays, open the model in the program that made it and check the openings there. Text on the DWG looks different. The export may keep fonts BimCraft does not have; BimCraft draws text in a plain sans-serif font. See also Get a project file into 3D through IFC and What BimCraft reads from each format."},{"slug":"open-project-folder","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Open a whole project folder","summary":"Point BimCraft at a project folder to see every model, its backups and links at once, and let the Start here box suggest which file to work on.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["folder","directory","project folder","network drive","server","start here","which file","backups","links","xrefs","recent folders","drag folder"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"use-the-start-here-box","text":"Use the Start here box","level":2},{"id":"open-files-from-the-report","text":"Open files from the report","level":2},{"id":"reopen-a-folder-later","text":"Reopen a folder later","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a whole project folder, such as the one you were handed for a job, and let BimCraft sort it out: which models are there, which copies are backups, what links to what, which files are damaged, and which file you most likely want to open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the few kilobytes each model card needs are read, so a folder on a network drive or a synced cloud folder is fine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the project folder in the window that opens and click \u003Cstrong\u003ESelect\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Cstrong\u003EUpload\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EOpen\u003C/strong\u003E, depending on your system).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EView files\u003C/strong\u003E when the browser asks &quot;Let site view files?&quot;. This only lets the tab read the folder; nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status line counts: &quot;Listing the folder: … files&quot;, then &quot;Checking Revit files: … of …&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the project folder in the window that opens and confirm. Firefox may ask you to confirm that the page can read the folder&#039;s files; they still stay on your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status line says &quot;Reading the folder: … files&quot;, then &quot;Checking Revit files: … of …&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the project folder in the window that opens and click \u003Cstrong\u003EUpload\u003C/strong\u003E. Despite the word, nothing leaves your Mac: the files are only handed to the tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status line says &quot;Reading the folder: … files&quot;, then &quot;Checking Revit files: … of …&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also \u003Cstrong\u003Edrag the folder\u003C/strong\u003E from \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E onto the page. A dragged folder opens without any permission prompt in every browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe folder report fills the middle:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe folder name, counts of models, CAD drawings, IFC models and files, and the total size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERed or amber notes if any files are incomplete or any linked files are missing from the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart here\u003C/span\u003E box: the file BimCraft suggests you open, with the reasons.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EModels\u003C/strong\u003E: one row per model, with its backups grouped under it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinked and referenced files\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC models\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ECAD drawings\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ESet aside\u003C/strong\u003E, when the folder has any.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the left, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFolder\u003C/span\u003E panel sums it up; \u003Cstrong\u003EProblems\u003C/strong\u003E says &quot;None&quot; when every copy is sound.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-the-start-here-box\"\u003EUse the Start here box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe box names one file and says why, for example:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Newest saved work in this folder (Mar 3, 2025).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003EConsultant.rvt\u003C/em\u003E is linked into other models, so it is background, not the file to edit.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003ETower.rvt\u003C/em\u003E looks like the obvious choice, but it is damaged; its last good copy is from …&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;This is a central model: in Revit, open it with &quot;Create New Local&quot; and work in the local copy.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the reasons.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen its card\u003C/span\u003E to read that file&#039;s card, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow it in the list\u003C/span\u003E to find its row in \u003Cstrong\u003EModels\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck with whoever last worked on the project before you rely on it. The box says so too: it is a best guess from names, dates and links.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-files-from-the-report\"\u003EOpen files from the report\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a model&#039;s name to open its card. The card checks each link against the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to\u003C/span\u003E and the folder name at the top of the card (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the folder\u003C/span\u003E on the left) to return.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick any IFC file under \u003Cstrong\u003EIFC models\u003C/strong\u003E to open it in 3D.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick any DWG or DXF under \u003Cstrong\u003ECAD drawings\u003C/strong\u003E to open it in 2D. Its linked drawings and pictures are found in the folder automatically.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reopen-a-folder-later\"\u003EReopen a folder later\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFolders you opened with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E or by dragging are listed under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecent folders\u003C/span\u003E on the left. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReopen\u003C/span\u003E and the folder name to open it again without choosing it. After a browser restart, Chrome or Edge asks once more; choose &quot;Allow on every visit&quot; to stop it asking. The list is kept in this browser only, never sent anywhere. Click × to forget a folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis browser cannot remember folders between visits. Choose the folder again with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E, or drag it in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPicking a folder that is too big.\u003C/strong\u003E Over 20,000 files: &quot;This folder has more than 20,000 files. Pick a smaller folder, such as one project.&quot; Pick the project folder, not the whole drive.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPicking the parent of several projects.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart here\u003C/span\u003E box then chooses across projects. Pick one project.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERevit&#039;s own folders look ignored.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E*_backup\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*_log\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ERevit_temp\u003C/code\u003E folders are housekeeping and are listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ESet aside\u003C/strong\u003E, not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting a model to open in 3D from its card.\u003C/strong\u003E A project file is a card only. Open its IFC export.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPhones and tablets.\u003C/strong\u003E Many mobile browsers cannot choose folders at all. Use a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/check-folder-health\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/check-folder-health\"\u003ECheck a folder for damaged copies, missing links and leftovers\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/folder-roles\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/folder-roles\"\u003EFolder report: roles, statuses and links\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Open a whole project folder, such as the one you were handed for a job, and let BimCraft sort it out: which models are there, which copies are backups, what links to what, which files are damaged, and which file you most likely want to open. Only the few kilobytes each model card needs are read, so a folder on a network drive or a synced cloud folder is fine. Steps Click Open a folder on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen). Choose the project folder in the window that opens and click Select (or Upload or Open, depending on your system). Click View files when the browser asks \"Let site view files?\". This only lets the tab read the folder; nothing is uploaded. Wait while the status line counts: \"Listing the folder: … files\", then \"Checking Revit files: … of …\". Click Open a folder on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen). Choose the project folder in the window that opens and confirm. Firefox may ask you to confirm that the page can read the folder's files; they still stay on your computer. Wait while the status line says \"Reading the folder: … files\", then \"Checking Revit files: … of …\". Click Open a folder on the left (or in the middle of the empty screen). Choose the project folder in the window that opens and click Upload. Despite the word, nothing leaves your Mac: the files are only handed to the tab. Wait while the status line says \"Reading the folder: … files\", then \"Checking Revit files: … of …\". You can also drag the folder from Finder / File Explorer / your file manager onto the page. A dragged folder opens without any permission prompt in every browser. What you should see The folder report fills the middle: The folder name, counts of models, CAD drawings, IFC models and files, and the total size. Red or amber notes if any files are incomplete or any linked files are missing from the folder. The Start here box: the file BimCraft suggests you open, with the reasons. Models: one row per model, with its backups grouped under it. Linked and referenced files, IFC models, CAD drawings and Set aside, when the folder has any. On the left, the Folder panel sums it up; Problems says \"None\" when every copy is sound. Use the Start here box The box names one file and says why, for example: \"Newest saved work in this folder (Mar 3, 2025).\" \"Consultant.rvt is linked into other models, so it is background, not the file to edit.\" \"Tower.rvt looks like the obvious choice, but it is damaged; its last good copy is from …\" \"This is a central model: in Revit, open it with \"Create New Local\" and work in the local copy.\" Read the reasons. Click Open its card to read that file's card, or Show it in the list to find its row in Models. Check with whoever last worked on the project before you rely on it. The box says so too: it is a best guess from names, dates and links. Open files from the report Click a model's name to open its card. The card checks each link against the folder. Click Back to and the folder name at the top of the card (or Back to the folder on the left) to return. Click any IFC file under IFC models to open it in 3D. Click any DWG or DXF under CAD drawings to open it in 2D. Its linked drawings and pictures are found in the folder automatically. Reopen a folder later Folders you opened with Open a folder or by dragging are listed under Recent folders on the left. Click Reopen and the folder name to open it again without choosing it. After a browser restart, Chrome or Edge asks once more; choose \"Allow on every visit\" to stop it asking. The list is kept in this browser only, never sent anywhere. Click × to forget a folder. This browser cannot remember folders between visits. Choose the folder again with Open a folder, or drag it in. Common mistakes Picking a folder that is too big. Over 20,000 files: \"This folder has more than 20,000 files. Pick a smaller folder, such as one project.\" Pick the project folder, not the whole drive. Picking the parent of several projects. The Start here box then chooses across projects. Pick one project. Revit's own folders look ignored. *_backup, *_log and Revit_temp folders are housekeeping and are listed under Set aside, not read. Expecting a model to open in 3D from its card. A project file is a card only. Open its IFC export. Phones and tablets. Many mobile browsers cannot choose folders at all. Use a computer. See Check a folder for damaged copies, missing links and leftovers and Folder report: roles, statuses and links."},{"slug":"check-folder-health","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Check a folder for damaged copies, missing links and leftovers","summary":"Use the folder report to find cut-off model files, pick the newest complete backup, see which links are missing, and spot lock and temp files.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["health check","damaged","corrupt","incomplete","truncated","backup","missing links","xref missing","audit","leftovers","lock files","dwl","bak","handover"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps-damaged-copies","text":"Steps: damaged copies","level":2},{"id":"what-the-health-check-does","text":"What the health check does","level":2},{"id":"steps-missing-links","text":"Steps: missing links","level":2},{"id":"steps-leftovers","text":"Steps: leftovers","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECheck a project folder before you hand it over or start work on it: is every model complete, which backup is the last good one, and are all the linked files there?\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-damaged-copies\"\u003ESteps: damaged copies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the project folder. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the notes under the folder name. &quot;2 files are incomplete or unreadable. See the models below.&quot; means there is something to look at.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cstrong\u003EModels\u003C/strong\u003E, look at the \u003Cstrong\u003EStatus\u003C/strong\u003E column. \u003Cstrong\u003EComplete\u003C/strong\u003E is fine. An amber &quot;1 incomplete&quot; means one copy is bad but another is sound. A red \u003Cstrong\u003EIncomplete\u003C/strong\u003E means no sound copy was found.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003EAll … copies\u003C/strong\u003E under a model with a problem. It lists the main file and every numbered backup, newest first, with size, date and status.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the note above the list, for example &quot;Tower.rvt is incomplete (183.2 MB, should be at least 214.0 MB). The card shows the newest complete copy, Tower.0012.rvt (Mar 3, 2025).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the copy you want to open. Only complete copies can be clicked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-health-check-does\"\u003EWhat the health check does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery copy of every model gets a quick check of its header, 512 bytes each, so even a folder of large models is checked in seconds. A file whose own index points past its end was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is still &quot;online only&quot; looks the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe card for each model is read from the main file when it is sound, and from the newest sound backup when it is not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-missing-links\"\u003ESteps: missing links\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for the note &quot;\u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E linked files are not in this folder.&quot; under the folder name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll to \u003Cstrong\u003ELinked and referenced files\u003C/strong\u003E. Missing links are listed first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the \u003Cstrong\u003EWhere\u003C/strong\u003E column for each link:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn this folder\u003C/strong\u003E: found at the path the model saved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESame name found\u003C/strong\u003E: a file with the same name is elsewhere in the folder. Check it is the right one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary file\u003C/strong\u003E: a keynote or assembly code table. These come with the program on each computer, so they are not expected in the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot in this folder\u003C/strong\u003E: not found anywhere in the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead \u003Cstrong\u003EUsed by\u003C/strong\u003E to see which models link each file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft looks for each link first by its relative path, then by the end of its saved path (so \u003Ccode\u003E\\\\server\\jobs\\Tower\\Cad\\X-Refs\\site.dwg\u003C/code\u003E is found at \u003Ccode\u003EX-Refs/site.dwg\u003C/code\u003E when you picked the \u003Ccode\u003ECad\u003C/code\u003E folder), then by file name anywhere in the folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-leftovers\"\u003ESteps: leftovers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScroll to \u003Cstrong\u003ESet aside\u003C/strong\u003E at the end of the report.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the three lines that may appear:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHousekeeping folders (\u003Ccode\u003E*_backup\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*_log\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERevit_temp\u003C/code\u003E): not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELock, log and backup leftovers: files such as \u003Ccode\u003E.dwl\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dwl2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.bak\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.err\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.log\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tmp\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.sv$\u003C/code\u003E. These are left by programs that crashed or were open when the folder was copied. They are usually safe to delete once nobody has the files open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOther files: everything else BimCraft does not read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDeleting the damaged main file before saving a good backup.\u003C/strong\u003E Copy the newest complete backup first, then deal with the damaged file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrusting &quot;Same name found&quot; blindly.\u003C/strong\u003E Two different drawings can share a name. Open both if in doubt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThinking a missing library file is a problem.\u003C/strong\u003E Keynote and assembly code tables live with the program on each computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe file count differs between browsers.\u003C/strong\u003E In Firefox and Safari, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E hands over every file, including those inside housekeeping folders, so the count (and the 20,000-file limit) includes them. Dragging the folder in skips them, as Chrome and Edge do. The report is otherwise the same.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Check a project folder before you hand it over or start work on it: is every model complete, which backup is the last good one, and are all the linked files there? Steps: damaged copies Open the project folder. See Open a whole project folder. Read the notes under the folder name. \"2 files are incomplete or unreadable. See the models below.\" means there is something to look at. In Models, look at the Status column. Complete is fine. An amber \"1 incomplete\" means one copy is bad but another is sound. A red Incomplete means no sound copy was found. Open All … copies under a model with a problem. It lists the main file and every numbered backup, newest first, with size, date and status. Read the note above the list, for example \"Tower.rvt is incomplete (183.2 MB, should be at least 214.0 MB). The card shows the newest complete copy, Tower.0012.rvt (Mar 3, 2025).\" Click the copy you want to open. Only complete copies can be clicked. What the health check does Every copy of every model gets a quick check of its header, 512 bytes each, so even a folder of large models is checked in seconds. A file whose own index points past its end was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is still \"online only\" looks the same. The card for each model is read from the main file when it is sound, and from the newest sound backup when it is not. Steps: missing links Look for the note \"n linked files are not in this folder.\" under the folder name. Scroll to Linked and referenced files. Missing links are listed first. Read the Where column for each link: In this folder: found at the path the model saved. Same name found: a file with the same name is elsewhere in the folder. Check it is the right one. Library file: a keynote or assembly code table. These come with the program on each computer, so they are not expected in the folder. Not in this folder: not found anywhere in the folder. Read Used by to see which models link each file. BimCraft looks for each link first by its relative path, then by the end of its saved path (so \\\\server\\jobs\\Tower\\Cad\\X-Refs\\site.dwg is found at X-Refs/site.dwg when you picked the Cad folder), then by file name anywhere in the folder. Steps: leftovers Scroll to Set aside at the end of the report. Read the three lines that may appear: Housekeeping folders (*_backup, *_log, Revit_temp): not read. Lock, log and backup leftovers: files such as .dwl, .dwl2, .bak, .err, .log, .tmp, .sv$. These are left by programs that crashed or were open when the folder was copied. They are usually safe to delete once nobody has the files open. Other files: everything else BimCraft does not read. Common mistakes Deleting the damaged main file before saving a good backup. Copy the newest complete backup first, then deal with the damaged file. Trusting \"Same name found\" blindly. Two different drawings can share a name. Open both if in doubt. Thinking a missing library file is a problem. Keynote and assembly code tables live with the program on each computer. The file count differs between browsers. In Firefox and Safari, Open a folder hands over every file, including those inside housekeeping folders, so the count (and the 20,000-file limit) includes them. Dragging the folder in skips them, as Chrome and Edge do. The report is otherwise the same."},{"slug":"view-dwg","scope":"bimcraft","title":"View a DWG or DXF drawing","summary":"Open a DWG or DXF, move around model space and sheets, read coordinates, and see what is not drawn yet.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["dwg","dxf","cad","drawing","2d","view","viewer","pan","zoom","model space","paper space","layout","coordinates","open dwg"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"moving-around","text":"Moving around","level":2},{"id":"not-drawn-yet","text":"Not drawn yet","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a CAD drawing in 2D, move around it, switch between model space and its sheets, and read coordinates. BimCraft shows DWG and DXF drawings; it does not edit or save them. To change one, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E and the drawing opens in the CadCraft drafting app in a new tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose a file\u003C/span\u003E, or drop the drawing onto the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status line says &quot;Reading the drawing…&quot;. Large drawings take a few seconds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the tabs above the drawing: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E first, then one tab per sheet, in the drawing&#039;s own order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a sheet tab to see that sheet: the paper, with the model shown through its viewports.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E to go back to model space.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer over the drawing and read the X and Y coordinates in the corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe drawing, fitted to the window. Colors follow the drawing; white lines (color 7) draw in the theme&#039;s ink, and pale colors are darkened on the light theme so they stay readable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E panel on the left: file, size, format (for example &quot;AutoCAD 2018 (AC1032)&quot;), units, entity and layer counts, the number of sheets, and linked drawings and equipment when there are any.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E list on the left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the right: how to move around, linked drawings and images, and \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-around\"\u003EMoving around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with any mouse button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel; the point under the pointer stays put\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac;input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac, Mouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad, scrolling with two fingers \u003Cstrong\u003Ezooms\u003C/strong\u003E, it does not pan. Pinch zooms too. To pan, click and drag.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger to pan. Pinch zoom is not built yet for drawings: tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E to see the whole sheet, and use a mouse or trackpad to zoom in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-drawn-yet\"\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome kinds of objects are not drawn in this build. They are counted under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E on the right (&quot;These parts of the drawing are skipped for now, not guessed at.&quot;), so you know something is missing. Lines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses, splines, hatches and solid fills, text and multiline text, blocks with attributes, dimensions, leaders and multileaders, tables, images and viewports are drawn. 3D faces, polyface meshes and polygon meshes are drawn as their edges seen from above; switch to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E to see them as surfaces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA binary DXF does not open: BimCraft says so. Save it again as an ASCII DXF, or as a DWG, from the program that made it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing looks empty.\u003C/strong\u003E Some layers may be off or frozen in the drawing itself; they start unticked in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll on\u003C/span\u003E. If the status says &quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E is an empty drawing: it holds no lines, text or blocks.&quot;, there is nothing in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA sheet shows an empty frame.\u003C/strong\u003E The sheet may rely on linked drawings. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\"\u003EFind linked drawings and pictures\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText looks a little different.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft draws text in a narrow sans-serif font, not the drawing&#039;s own fonts, so text can be a little wider or narrower than in the program that made it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a format the reader does not handle yet.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The bracket at the end names the file&#039;s format code. Save it from the program that made it as an older DWG or as DXF, and try again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting to measure, draw or edit.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft only views drawings. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E to open the same file in the CadCraft drafting app, which edits it and saves a copy as DWG or DXF. Your original file is not changed, and linked drawings stay in BimCraft. The button shows only where CadCraft is open to you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\"\u003ESee a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-layers-sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-layers-sheets\"\u003EWork with layers and sheets\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/print-view\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/print-view\"\u003ECheck a drawing in print view\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Open a CAD drawing in 2D, move around it, switch between model space and its sheets, and read coordinates. BimCraft shows DWG and DXF drawings; it does not edit or save them. To change one, click Edit in CadCraft and the drawing opens in the CadCraft drafting app in a new tab. Steps Click Choose a file, or drop the drawing onto the page. Choose a .dwg or .dxf file. Wait while the status line says \"Reading the drawing…\". Large drawings take a few seconds. Look at the tabs above the drawing: Model first, then one tab per sheet, in the drawing's own order. Click a sheet tab to see that sheet: the paper, with the model shown through its viewports. Click Model to go back to model space. Move the pointer over the drawing and read the X and Y coordinates in the corner. What you should see The drawing, fitted to the window. Colors follow the drawing; white lines (color 7) draw in the theme's ink, and pale colors are darkened on the light theme so they stay readable. The Drawing panel on the left: file, size, format (for example \"AutoCAD 2018 (AC1032)\"), units, entity and layer counts, the number of sheets, and linked drawings and equipment when there are any. The Layers list on the left. On the right: how to move around, linked drawings and images, and Not drawn yet. Moving around To Do this Pan Drag with any mouse button Zoom Scroll the wheel; the point under the pointer stays put Fit Double-click, press F, or click Fit On a trackpad, scrolling with two fingers zooms, it does not pan. Pinch zooms too. To pan, click and drag. Drag with one finger to pan. Pinch zoom is not built yet for drawings: tap Fit to see the whole sheet, and use a mouse or trackpad to zoom in. Not drawn yet Some kinds of objects are not drawn in this build. They are counted under Not drawn yet on the right (\"These parts of the drawing are skipped for now, not guessed at.\"), so you know something is missing. Lines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses, splines, hatches and solid fills, text and multiline text, blocks with attributes, dimensions, leaders and multileaders, tables, images and viewports are drawn. 3D faces, polyface meshes and polygon meshes are drawn as their edges seen from above; switch to 3D to see them as surfaces. A binary DXF does not open: BimCraft says so. Save it again as an ASCII DXF, or as a DWG, from the program that made it. Common mistakes The drawing looks empty. Some layers may be off or frozen in the drawing itself; they start unticked in Layers. Click All on. If the status says \"name is an empty drawing: it holds no lines, text or blocks.\", there is nothing in it. A sheet shows an empty frame. The sheet may rely on linked drawings. See Find linked drawings and pictures. Text looks a little different. BimCraft draws text in a narrow sans-serif font, not the drawing's own fonts, so text can be a little wider or narrower than in the program that made it. \"name could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a format the reader does not handle yet.\" The bracket at the end names the file's format code. Save it from the program that made it as an older DWG or as DXF, and try again. Expecting to measure, draw or edit. BimCraft only views drawings. Click Edit in CadCraft to open the same file in the CadCraft drafting app, which edits it and saves a copy as DWG or DXF. Your original file is not changed, and linked drawings stay in BimCraft. The button shows only where CadCraft is open to you. Next See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D Work with layers and sheets Check a drawing in print view"},{"slug":"dwg-in-3d","scope":"bimcraft","title":"See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D","summary":"Switch a drawing's model space to 3D to see elevations, thickness, 3D faces, meshes and blocks, orbit round it, click an object to see its properties, and see what is not drawn yet.","section":"tasks","order":105,"keywords":["3d","dwg","dxf","drawing","model space","orbit","section","cut","clipping","pick","select","properties","attributes","handle","elevation","thickness","extrusion","3dface","polyface","mesh","3d polyline","blocks","views","perspective","parallel","extents","acis","solids"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"cut-it-with-a-section-plane","text":"Cut it with a section plane","level":2},{"id":"see-an-object-s-properties","text":"See an object's properties","level":2},{"id":"moving-around","text":"Moving around","level":2},{"id":"what-is-drawn-in-3d","text":"What is drawn in 3D","level":2},{"id":"in-the-2d-view-only","text":"In the 2D view only","level":2},{"id":"not-drawn-yet","text":"Not drawn yet","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELook at a drawing&#039;s model space in 3D. Lines and polylines sit at their real heights, objects with a thickness stand up as walls, and 3D faces, meshes and 3D blocks show as surfaces you can orbit round. The sheets stay 2D.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/view-dwg\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/view-dwg\"\u003EView a DWG or DXF drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E in the bar above the drawing, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait a moment the first time: the status line says &quot;Building the drawing in 3D…&quot;, then how many lines and faces it shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOrbit, pan and zoom (see the table below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a view in the second bar: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIso\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeft\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERight\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerspective\u003C/span\u003E to switch between a perspective and a parallel view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to see the whole drawing again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E again, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E or a sheet tab to go back to 2D.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EModel space in 3D, Z up, in the drawing&#039;s own colors. Faces are a shade lighter than their edges.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers you turn off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E disappear from the 3D view too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the right: the number of lines and faces, the size of the drawing in X, Y and Z, \u003Cstrong\u003EIn the 2D view only\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-it-with-a-section-plane\"\u003ECut it with a section plane\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E on the 3D bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The bar under it works as it does for IFC models (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E): cut from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E, drag or type the offset — in the drawing&#039;s own units here — and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip\u003C/span\u003E to keep the other side. What the plane cuts away cannot be picked. Going back to 2D takes the cut away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-an-object-s-properties\"\u003ESee an object&#039;s properties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an object in the 3D view. It turns the pick color and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows what the file says about it: its type, layer, color, linetype and handle, then its own data, such as a line&#039;s start, end and length, a circle&#039;s center and radius, a 3D face&#039;s corners, or a block reference&#039;s block, insertion point, scale, rotation and attribute values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA block reference picks as a whole, the blocks nested in it too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA click a few pixels from a line picks the line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click to pick more objects, or to take one out; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E then counts them by type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or a click on empty space, clears the pick.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObjects on layers you turned off cannot be picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-around\"\u003EMoving around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the middle button, or with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button, or hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel; it zooms toward what is under the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee it all\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELook from a side\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a face, edge or corner of the view cube in the bottom-right corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view cube can be swapped for an axis icon, or hidden, in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac;input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac, Mouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad, swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and swipe to pan, and pinch to zoom.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger to orbit. Pinch with two fingers to zoom, and move them together to pan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-drawn-in-3d\"\u003EWhat is drawn in 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the drawing\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn 3D\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines, 3D polylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAt their real heights\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines, circles, arcs, ellipses\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn their own plane, at their elevation, tilted when the drawing tilts them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThickness on lines, polylines, circles and arcs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWalls of that height; circles are closed top and bottom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D faces, polyface meshes, polygon meshes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShaded surfaces with their visible edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlocks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlaced, turned and scaled in 3D, nested blocks too\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHatches\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETheir outlines\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlain 2D linework\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlat, where it was drawn\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-2d-view-only\"\u003EIn the 2D view only\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText, multiline text and the visible attributes of blocks are drawn in 3D, in their own plane at their own height, and a click on them picks them. Wide polylines show their width as a flat band. Multileaders, pictures and hatch patterns are counted under \u003Cstrong\u003EIn the 2D view only\u003C/strong\u003E: see them on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab or the sheets.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-drawn-yet\"\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESolids and surfaces stored as ACIS bodies (3D solids, bodies, regions and surfaces) and subdivision meshes are listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E with how many there are. They are skipped, not guessed at.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything looks flat.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing is 2D: its lines have no height. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E to check; a flat drawing shows as a line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA solid model shows nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E Solids made with a solid modeler are ACIS bodies, listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E. Export the model as IFC or as 3D faces from the program that made it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing is a tiny cluster in the middle.\u003C/strong\u003E Something far away in model space, such as an old copy of a plan, makes the extents large. Zoom in with the wheel where you want to look.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-layers-sheets\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-layers-sheets\"\u003EWork with layers and sheets\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Look at a drawing's model space in 3D. Lines and polylines sit at their real heights, objects with a thickness stand up as walls, and 3D faces, meshes and 3D blocks show as surfaces you can orbit round. The sheets stay 2D. Steps Open a DWG or DXF. See View a DWG or DXF drawing. Click 3D in the bar above the drawing, or press 3. Wait a moment the first time: the status line says \"Building the drawing in 3D…\", then how many lines and faces it shows. Orbit, pan and zoom (see the table below). Click a view in the second bar: Iso, Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Left or Right. Click Perspective to switch between a perspective and a parallel view. Click Extents or press F to see the whole drawing again. Click 3D again, press 3, or click Model or a sheet tab to go back to 2D. What you should see Model space in 3D, Z up, in the drawing's own colors. Faces are a shade lighter than their edges. Layers you turn off in Layers disappear from the 3D view too. On the right: the number of lines and faces, the size of the drawing in X, Y and Z, In the 2D view only and Not drawn yet. Cut it with a section plane Click Section on the 3D bar, or press X. The bar under it works as it does for IFC models (see Cut an IFC model with a section plane): cut from the Top, Front or Side, drag or type the offset — in the drawing's own units here — and Flip to keep the other side. What the plane cuts away cannot be picked. Going back to 2D takes the cut away. See an object's properties Click an object in the 3D view. It turns the pick color and Properties shows what the file says about it: its type, layer, color, linetype and handle, then its own data, such as a line's start, end and length, a circle's center and radius, a 3D face's corners, or a block reference's block, insertion point, scale, rotation and attribute values. A block reference picks as a whole, the blocks nested in it too. A click a few pixels from a line picks the line. Hold Ctrl and click to pick more objects, or to take one out; Properties then counts them by type. Esc, or a click on empty space, clears the pick. Objects on layers you turned off cannot be picked. Moving around To Do this Orbit Drag with the middle button, or with the left button Pan Drag with the right button, or hold Shift and drag Zoom Scroll the wheel; it zooms toward what is under the pointer See it all Extents or F Look from a side Click a face, edge or corner of the view cube in the bottom-right corner The view cube can be swapped for an axis icon, or hidden, in Settings. See BimCraft settings. On a trackpad, swipe with two fingers to orbit, hold Shift and swipe to pan, and pinch to zoom. Drag with one finger to orbit. Pinch with two fingers to zoom, and move them together to pan. What is drawn in 3D In the drawing In 3D Lines, 3D polylines At their real heights Polylines, circles, arcs, ellipses In their own plane, at their elevation, tilted when the drawing tilts them Thickness on lines, polylines, circles and arcs Walls of that height; circles are closed top and bottom 3D faces, polyface meshes, polygon meshes Shaded surfaces with their visible edges Blocks Placed, turned and scaled in 3D, nested blocks too Hatches Their outlines Plain 2D linework Flat, where it was drawn In the 2D view only Text, multiline text and the visible attributes of blocks are drawn in 3D, in their own plane at their own height, and a click on them picks them. Wide polylines show their width as a flat band. Multileaders, pictures and hatch patterns are counted under In the 2D view only: see them on the Model tab or the sheets. Not drawn yet Solids and surfaces stored as ACIS bodies (3D solids, bodies, regions and surfaces) and subdivision meshes are listed under Not drawn yet with how many there are. They are skipped, not guessed at. Common mistakes Everything looks flat. The drawing is 2D: its lines have no height. Click Front to check; a flat drawing shows as a line. A solid model shows nothing. Solids made with a solid modeler are ACIS bodies, listed under Not drawn yet. Export the model as IFC or as 3D faces from the program that made it. The drawing is a tiny cluster in the middle. Something far away in model space, such as an old copy of a plan, makes the extents large. Zoom in with the wheel where you want to look. Next Work with layers and sheets Cut an IFC model with a section plane"},{"slug":"dwg-layers-sheets","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Work with layers and sheets","summary":"Turn drawing layers on and off, switch between model space and sheets, and understand how sheets show the model through their viewports.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["layers","layer on off","freeze","thaw","sheets","layouts","paper space","tabs","viewports","layer manager","la"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps-layers","text":"Steps: layers","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"steps-sheets","text":"Steps: sheets","level":2},{"id":"how-sheets-work-here","text":"How sheets work here","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClean up the view of a busy drawing by turning layers off, then look at the drawing&#039;s sheets one by one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-layers\"\u003ESteps: layers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E list on the left. Each layer has a checkbox, a color swatch and its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick a layer to hide everything on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll off\u003C/span\u003E to hide every layer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick only the layers you want to see.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll on\u003C/span\u003E to show everything again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELayers disappear and reappear at once, on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E and on every sheet. Layers that are off or frozen in the drawing itself start unticked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-sheets\"\u003ESteps: sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the row of tabs above the drawing: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E, then each sheet by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a sheet tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the sheet: the paper sits on a darker desk, and each viewport shows its part of the model at its own scale and angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZoom into a viewport to read detail. You are zooming the sheet, not the viewport, so the whole sheet moves together.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E or double-click to see the whole sheet again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-sheets-work-here\"\u003EHow sheets work here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sheet (a layout, or paper space) holds a title block and viewports that look into model space. BimCraft draws each viewport clipped to its edge, at its own scale and twist, and hides the layers frozen in that viewport. Each sheet&#039;s first viewport is the sheet itself and is not drawn twice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting layer changes to be saved.\u003C/strong\u003E Layer settings only affect the view, until you open another file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting per-viewport layer control.\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E list switches a layer everywhere. Layers frozen in one viewport by the drawing stay frozen there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELooking for a layer search or layer states.\u003C/strong\u003E Not built yet. The list is in the drawing&#039;s order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting to work inside a viewport.\u003C/strong\u003E There is nothing to activate: BimCraft only shows the sheet. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E to look at model space directly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Clean up the view of a busy drawing by turning layers off, then look at the drawing's sheets one by one. Steps: layers Open a DWG or DXF drawing. Find the Layers list on the left. Each layer has a checkbox, a color swatch and its name. Untick a layer to hide everything on it. Click All off to hide every layer. Tick only the layers you want to see. Click All on to show everything again. What you should see Layers disappear and reappear at once, on Model and on every sheet. Layers that are off or frozen in the drawing itself start unticked. Steps: sheets Look at the row of tabs above the drawing: Model, then each sheet by name. Click a sheet tab. Look at the sheet: the paper sits on a darker desk, and each viewport shows its part of the model at its own scale and angle. Zoom into a viewport to read detail. You are zooming the sheet, not the viewport, so the whole sheet moves together. Click Fit or double-click to see the whole sheet again. How sheets work here A sheet (a layout, or paper space) holds a title block and viewports that look into model space. BimCraft draws each viewport clipped to its edge, at its own scale and twist, and hides the layers frozen in that viewport. Each sheet's first viewport is the sheet itself and is not drawn twice. Common mistakes Expecting layer changes to be saved. Layer settings only affect the view, until you open another file. Expecting per-viewport layer control. The Layers list switches a layer everywhere. Layers frozen in one viewport by the drawing stay frozen there. Looking for a layer search or layer states. Not built yet. The list is in the drawing's order. Expecting to work inside a viewport. There is nothing to activate: BimCraft only shows the sheet. Use Model to look at model space directly."},{"slug":"dwg-linked-files","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Find linked drawings and pictures","summary":"Show a drawing's linked drawings (xrefs) and title-block pictures, by opening its folder or adding the files by hand.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["xref","external reference","linked drawing","attach","overlay","image","picture","logo","title block","missing xref","etransmit","reference manager"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way-open-the-folder","text":"The easy way: open the folder","level":2},{"id":"the-other-way-add-files-by-hand","text":"The other way: add files by hand","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMake a drawing complete: show the drawings it links to (external references, or xrefs) and the pictures it uses, such as a logo in the title block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way-open-the-folder\"\u003EThe easy way: open the folder\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E and choose the folder that holds the drawing and its links, for example the whole \u003Ccode\u003ECad\u003C/code\u003E folder of a project.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the folder report, scroll to \u003Cstrong\u003ECAD drawings\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right, under \u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EImages\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft looks for each linked drawing by its name (&quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E.dwg&quot;), in the same folder as the drawing first, then anywhere in the folder you opened. Pictures are found the same way, and also by a similar name: an eTransmit or Revit export often adds the project&#039;s name in front, so \u003Ccode\u003Elogo.png\u003C/code\u003E is found as \u003Ccode\u003ETower-logo-5425051.png\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-other-way-add-files-by-hand\"\u003EThe other way: add files by hand\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the drawing on its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the right, under \u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/strong\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd linked drawings…\u003C/span\u003E. Under \u003Cstrong\u003EImages\u003C/strong\u003E, the button is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd pictures…\u003C/span\u003E; both open the same chooser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the linked \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E files and the picture files. You can choose several at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while BimCraft opens the drawing again with them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach linked drawing and picture is listed with a label:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELabel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShown\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFound and drawn. The path under it says where it came from, or &quot;added by you&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShown, similar name\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picture found by a similar name. Check it is the right one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot found\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot in the folder or the files you added. A missing picture shows as its empty frame.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETIFF cannot be shown in a browser\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe picture is a TIFF file, which browsers do not draw. Save it as PNG with the same name and add it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECould not be read\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file was found but could not be opened as a picture.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E panel on the left shows &quot;\u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E of \u003Cem\u003Em\u003C/em\u003E loaded&quot; for linked drawings. Linked drawings&#039; layers appear in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E as &quot;\u003Cem\u003Elink name\u003C/em\u003E|\u003Cem\u003Elayer\u003C/em\u003E&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening only the drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;This drawing shows its own content only until its linked drawings are added. Opening the whole project folder finds them automatically.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a folder too small.\u003C/strong\u003E If the links live in a sibling folder, open the parent folder instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERenamed links.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft matches linked drawings by name. A link renamed on disk is not found; add it by hand.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinks of links.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft loads the drawings the open drawing links to. Drawings linked inside those are not looked for, so they usually do not show in this build.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Make a drawing complete: show the drawings it links to (external references, or xrefs) and the pictures it uses, such as a logo in the title block. The easy way: open the folder Click Open a folder and choose the folder that holds the drawing and its links, for example the whole Cad folder of a project. In the folder report, scroll to CAD drawings. Click the drawing. Look at the Properties panel on the right, under Linked drawings and Images. BimCraft looks for each linked drawing by its name (\"name.dwg\"), in the same folder as the drawing first, then anywhere in the folder you opened. Pictures are found the same way, and also by a similar name: an eTransmit or Revit export often adds the project's name in front, so logo.png is found as Tower-logo-5425051.png. The other way: add files by hand Open the drawing on its own. On the right, under Linked drawings, click Add linked drawings…. Under Images, the button is Add pictures…; both open the same chooser. Choose the linked .dwg files and the picture files. You can choose several at once. Wait while BimCraft opens the drawing again with them. What you should see Each linked drawing and picture is listed with a label: Label Means Shown Found and drawn. The path under it says where it came from, or \"added by you\". Shown, similar name A picture found by a similar name. Check it is the right one. Not found Not in the folder or the files you added. A missing picture shows as its empty frame. TIFF cannot be shown in a browser The picture is a TIFF file, which browsers do not draw. Save it as PNG with the same name and add it. Could not be read The file was found but could not be opened as a picture. The Drawing panel on the left shows \"n of m loaded\" for linked drawings. Linked drawings' layers appear in Layers as \"link name|layer\". Common mistakes Opening only the drawing. \"This drawing shows its own content only until its linked drawings are added. Opening the whole project folder finds them automatically.\" Opening a folder too small. If the links live in a sibling folder, open the parent folder instead. Renamed links. BimCraft matches linked drawings by name. A link renamed on disk is not found; add it by hand. Links of links. BimCraft loads the drawings the open drawing links to. Drawings linked inside those are not looked for, so they usually do not show in this build."},{"slug":"print-view","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Check a drawing in print view","summary":"Switch a DWG or DXF to print view to see it black on white with its line weights and without the layers that never print.","section":"tasks","order":130,"keywords":["print","plot","preview","plot preview","line weights","lineweight","monochrome","black and white","defpoints","no plot"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee how a sheet will look on paper before you print it from the program that made it: black on white, line weights at their true width, and without layers that are set not to print.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a sheet tab above the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E at the end of the tabs, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZoom in on a detail to compare heavy and light lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E again, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, to go back to the normal colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhite paper on a gray desk, every line and text in black, whatever the theme.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELine weights: on a sheet each weight is drawn at its real width on paper, so a 0.5 mm line is twice as thick as a 0.25 mm line at any zoom. A line is never drawn thinner than its screen weight.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers set not to print, and the DEFPOINTS layer, are hidden.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E button stays pressed. Print view stays on when you switch sheets or open another drawing, until you turn it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E, print view uses screen weights only (thin lines one pixel, heavier ones a little thicker), because model space has no paper size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting it to print.\u003C/strong\u003E Print view is a preview. Printing and PDF export are not built in BimCraft yet; print from the program that made the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting plot styles.\u003C/strong\u003E Pen tables and plot style colors are not read. Everything is plain black.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPressing P while typing.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E do nothing while you are typing in a field, or while the equipment schedule is showing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do See how a sheet will look on paper before you print it from the program that made it: black on white, line weights at their true width, and without layers that are set not to print. Steps Open a DWG or DXF drawing. Click a sheet tab above the drawing. Click Print view at the end of the tabs, or press P. Zoom in on a detail to compare heavy and light lines. Click Print view again, or press P, to go back to the normal colors. What you should see White paper on a gray desk, every line and text in black, whatever the theme. Line weights: on a sheet each weight is drawn at its real width on paper, so a 0.5 mm line is twice as thick as a 0.25 mm line at any zoom. A line is never drawn thinner than its screen weight. Layers set not to print, and the DEFPOINTS layer, are hidden. The Print view button stays pressed. Print view stays on when you switch sheets or open another drawing, until you turn it off. In Model, print view uses screen weights only (thin lines one pixel, heavier ones a little thicker), because model space has no paper size. Common mistakes Expecting it to print. Print view is a preview. Printing and PDF export are not built in BimCraft yet; print from the program that made the drawing. Expecting plot styles. Pen tables and plot style colors are not read. Everything is plain black. Pressing P while typing. F and P do nothing while you are typing in a field, or while the equipment schedule is showing."},{"slug":"equipment-schedule","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Build an equipment schedule and export CSV","summary":"Turn the item tags in a foodservice drawing into an equipment schedule, filter it, jump to each item on its sheet, and download it as CSV.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["equipment","schedule","foodservice","kitchen","item tags","kcl","attributes","bom","bill of materials","csv","spreadsheet","export","data extraction"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"how-the-schedule-is-built","text":"How the schedule is built","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERead the equipment list out of a kitchen or foodservice drawing, check it, find each item on the plan, and download it as a spreadsheet file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works on drawings whose equipment tags carry their data as block attributes, as kitchen-design libraries such as KCL do: each tag holds the item number (\u003Ccode\u003EKCL-ITEM\u003C/code\u003E), quantity, category, manufacturer, model, electrical and plumbing data, and remarks. Other drawings have no \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the drawing, ideally from its project folder so its linked drawings load too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E tab above the drawing. Its label shows how many items are on sheets, for example &quot;Equipment (88)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the schedule: \u003Cstrong\u003EItem\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EQty\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEquipment\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EManufacturer\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EModel\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EElectrical\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPlumbing\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EWhere\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick what to list in the menu above the table: \u003Cstrong\u003EEvery sheet\u003C/strong\u003E, one sheet, \u003Cstrong\u003ENot on any sheet\u003C/strong\u003E, or \u003Cstrong\u003EEverything tagged in the drawing\u003C/strong\u003E. Each choice shows its count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in the filter box (&quot;Filter: category, maker, model…&quot;) to narrow the list, for example \u003Ccode\u003Erefrigerator\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E208\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick an item&#039;s sheet name in the \u003Cstrong\u003EWhere\u003C/strong\u003E column.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the drawing: BimCraft switches to that sheet, zooms in on the item and draws a ring round it. The status line says &quot;Item \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E, \u003Cem\u003Ecategory\u003C/em\u003E is ringed on \u003Cem\u003Esheet\u003C/em\u003E.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E to go back to the schedule.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA file named &quot;\u003Cem\u003Edrawing name\u003C/em\u003E equipment.csv&quot; (with the sheet&#039;s name in brackets when you listed one sheet) in your downloads folder. It opens in Excel, Numbers, LibreOffice or Google Sheets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe status line: &quot;Downloaded \u003Cem\u003Efile name\u003C/em\u003E (\u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E items).&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe CSV has these columns: Item, Qty, Category, Manufacturer, Model, Electrical, Plumbing, Remarks, Electrical remarks, Plumbing remarks, Sheets, Placed (how many tags). It downloads exactly the rows the table shows, after the menu and the filter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-schedule-is-built\"\u003EHow the schedule is built\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt is read from the \u003Cstrong\u003Etags themselves\u003C/strong\u003E, not from the schedule table drawn on the sheet. The note above the table says how many tags were read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne row is one item number with one set of data. Its quantity is the \u003Cstrong\u003Esum\u003C/strong\u003E of its tags, the way a printed schedule counts it: shelving tagged &quot;5&quot; in one place and &quot;3&quot; in another is 8, shown as &quot;5 + 3&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere\u003C/strong\u003E lists the sheets that show each item, worked out from what each sheet&#039;s viewports show, which is what prints. &quot;Model only&quot; means no sheet shows it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENumber used twice\u003C/strong\u003E marks one item number on two different pieces of equipment. The row has an amber mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETags no sheet shows are kept apart under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot on any sheet\u003C/strong\u003E. They are often left over from earlier plans or other jobs, and are never mixed into \u003Cstrong\u003EEvery sheet\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElectrical\u003C/strong\u003E shows amps, kW, HP, volts, phase, connection and plug as tagged. \u003Cstrong\u003EPlumbing\u003C/strong\u003E shows connection sizes: CW cold water, HW hot water, DD direct drain, ID indirect drain, and gas. Other tag fields are not shown yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing has no \u003Ccode\u003EKCL-ITEM\u003C/code\u003E tags, or its equipment is drawn without data. There is nothing to read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECounts differ from the printed schedule.\u003C/strong\u003E A printed schedule may list spare numbers or use shorter names than the tags. Trust the tags for what is placed; check differences with the designer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening the CSV shows odd characters.\u003C/strong\u003E The file is UTF-8. Open it with your spreadsheet&#039;s import command and choose UTF-8 if the program asks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA cell starts with an apostrophe.\u003C/strong\u003E Values that begin with \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E get a leading \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E so spreadsheets do not run them as formulas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E do nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E They work on the drawing, not on the schedule. Click a sheet tab first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Read the equipment list out of a kitchen or foodservice drawing, check it, find each item on the plan, and download it as a spreadsheet file. This works on drawings whose equipment tags carry their data as block attributes, as kitchen-design libraries such as KCL do: each tag holds the item number (KCL-ITEM), quantity, category, manufacturer, model, electrical and plumbing data, and remarks. Other drawings have no Equipment tab. Steps Open the drawing, ideally from its project folder so its linked drawings load too. Click the Equipment tab above the drawing. Its label shows how many items are on sheets, for example \"Equipment (88)\". Read the schedule: Item, Qty, Equipment, Manufacturer, Model, Electrical, Plumbing and Where. Pick what to list in the menu above the table: Every sheet, one sheet, Not on any sheet, or Everything tagged in the drawing. Each choice shows its count. Type in the filter box (\"Filter: category, maker, model…\") to narrow the list, for example refrigerator or 208. Click an item's sheet name in the Where column. Look at the drawing: BimCraft switches to that sheet, zooms in on the item and draws a ring round it. The status line says \"Item n, category is ringed on sheet.\" Click Equipment to go back to the schedule. Click Download CSV. What you should see A file named \"drawing name equipment.csv\" (with the sheet's name in brackets when you listed one sheet) in your downloads folder. It opens in Excel, Numbers, LibreOffice or Google Sheets. The status line: \"Downloaded file name (n items).\" The CSV has these columns: Item, Qty, Category, Manufacturer, Model, Electrical, Plumbing, Remarks, Electrical remarks, Plumbing remarks, Sheets, Placed (how many tags). It downloads exactly the rows the table shows, after the menu and the filter. How the schedule is built It is read from the tags themselves, not from the schedule table drawn on the sheet. The note above the table says how many tags were read. One row is one item number with one set of data. Its quantity is the sum of its tags, the way a printed schedule counts it: shelving tagged \"5\" in one place and \"3\" in another is 8, shown as \"5 + 3\". Where lists the sheets that show each item, worked out from what each sheet's viewports show, which is what prints. \"Model only\" means no sheet shows it. Number used twice marks one item number on two different pieces of equipment. The row has an amber mark. Tags no sheet shows are kept apart under Not on any sheet. They are often left over from earlier plans or other jobs, and are never mixed into Every sheet. Electrical shows amps, kW, HP, volts, phase, connection and plug as tagged. Plumbing shows connection sizes: CW cold water, HW hot water, DD direct drain, ID indirect drain, and gas. Other tag fields are not shown yet. Common mistakes No Equipment tab. The drawing has no KCL-ITEM tags, or its equipment is drawn without data. There is nothing to read. Counts differ from the printed schedule. A printed schedule may list spare numbers or use shorter names than the tags. Trust the tags for what is placed; check differences with the designer. Opening the CSV shows odd characters. The file is UTF-8. Open it with your spreadsheet's import command and choose UTF-8 if the program asks. A cell starts with an apostrophe. Values that begin with =, +, - or @ get a leading ' so spreadsheets do not run them as formulas. F and P do nothing. They work on the drawing, not on the schedule. Click a sheet tab first."},{"slug":"place-from-library","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Place equipment and furniture from the library","summary":"Find an item in LibraryCraft, a file or the open drawing, place it on a storey of an IFC model with a ghost preview, turn it, snap it to a wall, and save.","section":"tasks","order":150,"keywords":["library","librarycraft","place","insert","equipment","furniture","block","family","item","catalog","content","kcl","csv","gltf","obj","rfa","dwg","ifc type"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPut a library item into an IFC model: a sink, a work table, a chair. BimCraft writes it as a proper IFC element with a type, so every copy of the same item shares one set of geometry, and its data, item number and license travel with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E tab beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E on the right, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar above the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a source:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELibraryCraft\u003C/strong\u003E: the CadCraft library on this site. Public items need no sign-in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThis file\u003C/strong\u003E: the blocks of an open DWG or DXF drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFiles…\u003C/strong\u003E: a DWG, DXF, IFC, glTF/GLB, OBJ, RFA or CSV file on your computer. It is read in this tab; nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther feeds\u003C/strong\u003E: another library that speaks the same feed format. Add its address once; it is kept in this browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESearch or pick a category, then click a card. The card shows a plan preview, the name, maker and model, the license and the version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace in the model\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the storey in the placing bar. Storeys above it are hidden while you place, and the view turns to the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer over the model: a see-through box shows where the item goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it 90°. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall snap\u003C/span\u003E) to put its back against the nearest wall, facing the room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to place it. Or type the position in \u003Cstrong\u003EX, Y (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1200, 3400\u003C/code\u003E, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnswer the one question: the \u003Cstrong\u003Eitem number\u003C/strong\u003E (the next free number is filled in), the quantity and a note. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlace more, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E to download the edited copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe item in the model, picked, with its properties on the right: a \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary item\u003C/strong\u003E box with its license and attribution, and the sets \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Placement\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_LibraryItem\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Equipment\u003C/code\u003E and, for items from tagged drawings, \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_SourceAttributes\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Placed: \u003Cem\u003Eitem\u003C/em\u003E (item \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E)&quot; in the change list. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe status line: &quot;Placed \u003Cem\u003Eitem\u003C/em\u003E as item \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn item with no 3D body (a 2D symbol) is placed with a stand-in box; you give its height when asked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMore of the same: pick a placed item and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate library items\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo start every placement with wall snap on, turn on \u003Cstrong\u003EStart with wall snap on\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELengths are converted to the model&#039;s own units, whatever they are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn an IFC2X3 file the body is written as a faceted solid; in IFC4 as a triangulated face set. Both open in other IFC tools.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA drawing (DWG or DXF) cannot be saved here, so items are placed into IFC models only. Use \u003Cstrong\u003EThis file\u003C/strong\u003E on a drawing to see what its blocks would become.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace in the model\u003C/span\u003E is missing.\u003C/strong\u003E No IFC model is open. Open one first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe ghost does not follow the pointer.\u003C/strong\u003E The pointer is off the model&#039;s area, or the storey plane is out of view. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall snap\u003C/span\u003E says there are no walls.\u003C/strong\u003E The storey has no walls in the IFC (some exports put walls on another storey). Pick that storey, or type X and Y.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA file&#039;s items are the wrong size.\u003C/strong\u003E The file has no units, or says the wrong ones. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFiles…\u003C/span\u003E choose the units and read it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlacing the same item twice shares one IFC type, so a model with forty chairs stores the chair once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you'll do Put a library item into an IFC model: a sink, a work table, a chair. BimCraft writes it as a proper IFC element with a type, so every copy of the same item shares one set of geometry, and its data, item number and license travel with it. Steps Open an IFC model. Click the Library tab beside Properties on the right, or Library in the toolbar above the model. Pick a source: LibraryCraft: the CadCraft library on this site. Public items need no sign-in. This file: the blocks of an open DWG or DXF drawing. Files…: a DWG, DXF, IFC, glTF/GLB, OBJ, RFA or CSV file on your computer. It is read in this tab; nothing is uploaded. Other feeds: another library that speaks the same feed format. Add its address once; it is kept in this browser. Search or pick a category, then click a card. The card shows a plan preview, the name, maker and model, the license and the version. Click Place in the model. Pick the storey in the placing bar. Storeys above it are hidden while you place, and the view turns to the top. Move the pointer over the model: a see-through box shows where the item goes. Press R to turn it 90°. Press Tab (or Wall snap) to put its back against the nearest wall, facing the room. Click to place it. Or type the position in X, Y (mm), for example 1200, 3400, and press Enter. Answer the one question: the item number (the next free number is filled in), the quantity and a note. Press Enter. Place more, or press Esc to stop. Click Save as IFC to download the edited copy. What you should see The item in the model, picked, with its properties on the right: a Library item box with its license and attribution, and the sets CadCraft_Placement, CadCraft_LibraryItem, CadCraft_Equipment and, for items from tagged drawings, CadCraft_SourceAttributes. \"Placed: item (item n)\" in the change list. Ctrl+Z undoes it. The status line: \"Placed item as item n.\" Good to know An item with no 3D body (a 2D symbol) is placed with a stand-in box; you give its height when asked. More of the same: pick a placed item and use Copy and Paste, or Ctrl+D. See Copy, paste and duplicate library items. To start every placement with wall snap on, turn on Start with wall snap on in BimCraft settings. Lengths are converted to the model's own units, whatever they are. In an IFC2X3 file the body is written as a faceted solid; in IFC4 as a triangulated face set. Both open in other IFC tools. A drawing (DWG or DXF) cannot be saved here, so items are placed into IFC models only. Use This file on a drawing to see what its blocks would become. Common mistakes Place in the model is missing. No IFC model is open. Open one first. The ghost does not follow the pointer. The pointer is off the model's area, or the storey plane is out of view. Click Top. Wall snap says there are no walls. The storey has no walls in the IFC (some exports put walls on another storey). Pick that storey, or type X and Y. A file's items are the wrong size. The file has no units, or says the wrong ones. In Files… choose the units and read it again. Placing the same item twice shares one IFC type, so a model with forty chairs stores the chair once."},{"slug":"swap-and-update-library-items","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Swap a placed item, and update items from the library","summary":"Replace one placed library item with another while keeping its place and item number, and check LibraryCraft for newer versions of everything in the model.","section":"tasks","order":151,"keywords":["swap","replace","substitute","update","newer version","check for updates","library","withdrawn","license change","item number"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"swap-one-item","text":"Swap one item","level":2},{"id":"check-for-updates","text":"Check for updates","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange your mind about an item without moving it again, and keep the library items in a model current.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"swap-one-item\"\u003ESwap one item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a placed library item in the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESwap item…\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary item\u003C/strong\u003E box. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E tab opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the new item and click its card.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESwap … to this\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe element keeps its place, turn, storey, item number, quantity and notes. Its type, shape and item data change to the new item. Items placed the same way share one type, as when you place them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the new item is a different kind of thing, for example a sink in place of a table, the element&#039;s IFC class changes to match (here from furnishing element to sanitary terminal), so other software schedules it correctly. It stays the same element, with the same GlobalId, so links to it still work. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings the old class back with the old item.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-for-updates\"\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdates\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/span\u003E in a library item&#039;s box).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the list: each LibraryCraft item in the model with its version in the model, the latest version and a status: \u003Cstrong\u003EUp to date\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ENewer version\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EWithdrawn\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENot found\u003C/strong\u003E. It also says what changed (geometry, data, license or a fix).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate\u003C/span\u003E on a row, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate all\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Updated \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E placements. Places, item numbers, quantities and notes are kept.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Updated: \u003Cem\u003Eitem\u003C/em\u003E&quot; lines in the change list. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes each one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOnly items that came from LibraryCraft can be checked. Items from files carry no library id.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen a license changed, the copy you already placed keeps the license it came with until you update it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003EWithdrawn\u003C/strong\u003E item is not offered for new placements. One withdrawn for copyright reasons cannot be downloaded at all; replace it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESwap item…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChecking sends only the item ids and versions, never your model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Change your mind about an item without moving it again, and keep the library items in a model current. Swap one item Click a placed library item in the model. In Properties, click Swap item… in the Library item box. The Library tab opens. Find the new item and click its card. Click Swap … to this. The element keeps its place, turn, storey, item number, quantity and notes. Its type, shape and item data change to the new item. Items placed the same way share one type, as when you place them. When the new item is a different kind of thing, for example a sink in place of a table, the element's IFC class changes to match (here from furnishing element to sanitary terminal), so other software schedules it correctly. It stays the same element, with the same GlobalId, so links to it still work. Ctrl+Z brings the old class back with the old item. Check for updates Click Updates in the toolbar (or Check for updates in a library item's box). Read the list: each LibraryCraft item in the model with its version in the model, the latest version and a status: Up to date, Newer version, Withdrawn or Not found. It also says what changed (geometry, data, license or a fix). Click Update on a row, or Update all. What you should see \"Updated n placements. Places, item numbers, quantities and notes are kept.\" \"Updated: item\" lines in the change list. Ctrl+Z undoes each one. Good to know Only items that came from LibraryCraft can be checked. Items from files carry no library id. When a license changed, the copy you already placed keeps the license it came with until you update it. A Withdrawn item is not offered for new placements. One withdrawn for copyright reasons cannot be downloaded at all; replace it with Swap item…. Checking sends only the item ids and versions, never your model."},{"slug":"library-credits","scope":"bimcraft","title":"List library credits and sign in to the library","summary":"See every library item used in a model with its license and attribution, copy the credits or download them with the schedule, and sign in to see your team's items.","section":"tasks","order":152,"keywords":["credits","attribution","license","cc by","cc0","copyright","team library","sign in","connect","token","equipment schedule","csv"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"library-credits","text":"Library credits","level":2},{"id":"the-equipment-schedule-of-a-model","text":"The equipment schedule of a model","level":2},{"id":"sign-in-to-the-library","text":"Sign in to the library","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGive credit where a license asks for it, and reach your team&#039;s own library items.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"library-credits\"\u003ELibrary credits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECredits\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the list: each item used, its version, license, attribution and the item numbers it is placed as.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy credits\u003C/span\u003E to copy the attribution lines, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV with the schedule\u003C/span\u003E for a spreadsheet of every placement with its credit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe license and attribution also show in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E when you pick a placed item, and they are saved in the IFC (\u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_LibraryItem\u003C/code\u003E), so they travel with the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-equipment-schedule-of-a-model\"\u003EThe equipment schedule of a model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. The schedule is built from the placed library items: item number, quantity, name, maker, model, electrical and plumbing data, grouped by storey. It downloads as the same CSV a drawing&#039;s schedule does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sign-in-to-the-library\"\u003ESign in to the library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E tab, pick \u003Cstrong\u003ELibraryCraft\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESign in to the library\u003C/span\u003E. A small window opens on this site.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESign in if asked, then click \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect\u003C/strong\u003E. The window hands BimCraft a library key and you can close it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Signed in to the library&quot; at the top of the LibraryCraft list. Your team&#039;s items appear beside the public ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EItems you place are recorded as used, so the library can tell you when they change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe key is kept in this tab only and ends when you close it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForget sign-in\u003C/span\u003E drops it sooner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour account&#039;s security page lists the key as &quot;BimCraft library (browser)&quot;; revoke it there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENothing about your model is sent: only which library items you placed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Give credit where a license asks for it, and reach your team's own library items. Library credits Open the model. Click Credits in the toolbar. Read the list: each item used, its version, license, attribution and the item numbers it is placed as. Click Copy credits to copy the attribution lines, or CSV with the schedule for a spreadsheet of every placement with its credit. The license and attribution also show in Properties when you pick a placed item, and they are saved in the IFC (CadCraft_LibraryItem), so they travel with the file. The equipment schedule of a model Click Equipment in the toolbar. The schedule is built from the placed library items: item number, quantity, name, maker, model, electrical and plumbing data, grouped by storey. It downloads as the same CSV a drawing's schedule does. Sign in to the library In the Library tab, pick LibraryCraft. Click Sign in to the library. A small window opens on this site. Sign in if asked, then click Connect. The window hands BimCraft a library key and you can close it. What you should see \"Signed in to the library\" at the top of the LibraryCraft list. Your team's items appear beside the public ones. Items you place are recorded as used, so the library can tell you when they change. Good to know The key is kept in this tab only and ends when you close it. Forget sign-in drops it sooner. Your account's security page lists the key as \"BimCraft library (browser)\"; revoke it there. Nothing about your model is sent: only which library items you placed."},{"slug":"copy-paste","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Copy, paste and duplicate library items","summary":"Copy a placed library item and paste it where you click, in place beside the original, or into another model; copy any other element's properties as text; undo and redo.","section":"tasks","order":155,"keywords":["copy","paste","duplicate","clipboard","paste in place","cut","delete","select all","undo","redo","edit menu","more","library item","placement","repeat","array"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"paste-in-place","text":"Paste in place","level":2},{"id":"duplicate","text":"Duplicate","level":2},{"id":"into-another-model-or-another-tab","text":"Into another model or another tab","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPut more copies of an item you already placed: a second work table, a row of sinks. This topic is about \u003Cstrong\u003Eplaced library items\u003C/strong\u003E, the elements BimCraft wrote into the model: one of them can be pasted into any model, even in another tab. Walls, doors and every other element are copied, cut, pasted and deleted the same way within their own model: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-elements\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-elements\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste building elements\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe commands are in the toolbar above the model: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, a menu with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E. A command that cannot act right now is greyed out; point at it to read why, or click it and the status line says why.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model with a library item in it, or place one first. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/place-from-library\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/place-from-library\"\u003EPlace equipment and furniture from the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the item in the 3D view. Its properties show a \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary item\u003C/strong\u003E box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The placing bar opens and a see-through box follows the pointer, turned like the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it 90°, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to snap its back to a wall, or type X and Y in the placing bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the copy goes. Each click places another copy; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E to download the edited copy of the model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-in-place\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes with no clicking, at the same X and Y and turned the same way:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInto the same model, on the same storey\u003C/strong\u003E: two items in exactly one spot would be no use, so the copy goes right beside the original, one item width plus 100 mm along the item&#039;s own width. Paste in place again from the new copy to make a row.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnto another storey or into another model\u003C/strong\u003E: exactly the same X, Y and turn. BimCraft uses the storey with the same name; if there is none, the one at the nearest height. The status line says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"duplicate\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies the picked item and starts pasting it straight away: one step instead of two.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"into-another-model-or-another-tab\"\u003EInto another model or another tab\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat you copy is kept in this browser, so you can open another IFC model, or BimCraft in another tab, and paste there. The other model needs the library item itself: BimCraft has it when the item was placed, swapped in or updated in this tab. An item placed in an earlier session can be pasted again in its own model only; in another model, place it from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe copy in the model, picked, with the same library item, license, equipment data, quantity and note as the original, and the \u003Cstrong\u003Enext free item number\u003C/strong\u003E. Change the number in Properties, under \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Placement\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Placed: \u003Cem\u003Eitem\u003C/em\u003E (item \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E)&quot; in the change list. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopies share the original&#039;s type, so all of them use one set of geometry, as placing the same item twice does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E cover every change: values you edited, placements, pastes, swaps and updates. Redo puts a placement back at the same place; a new change clears what could be redone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the 2D view of a drawing there is nothing to select, so there is nothing to copy. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E to change the drawing, when it shows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E says the elements came from another model.\u003C/strong\u003E The last thing you copied was a building element, or several elements: those paste into their own model only. Pick one placed library item and copy it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E says the copy came from another model without its library item.\u003C/strong\u003E Place the item from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E tab once in this tab, then copy and paste it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies page text instead.\u003C/strong\u003E Text was selected on the page. Click the model first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/keyboard#edit\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"edit\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Put more copies of an item you already placed: a second work table, a row of sinks. This topic is about placed library items, the elements BimCraft wrote into the model: one of them can be pasted into any model, even in another tab. Walls, doors and every other element are copied, cut, pasted and deleted the same way within their own model: see Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements. The commands are in the toolbar above the model: Undo, Redo, Copy, Paste and More, a menu with Cut, Copy, Paste, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Select all and Select none. A command that cannot act right now is greyed out; point at it to read why, or click it and the status line says why. Steps Open an IFC model with a library item in it, or place one first. See Place equipment and furniture from the library. Click the item in the 3D view. Its properties show a Library item box. Click Copy or press Ctrl+C. Click Paste or press Ctrl+V. The placing bar opens and a see-through box follows the pointer, turned like the original. Press R to turn it 90°, Tab to snap its back to a wall, or type X and Y in the placing bar. Click where the copy goes. Each click places another copy; press Esc to stop. Click Save as IFC to download the edited copy of the model. Paste in place More, Paste in place or Ctrl+Shift+V pastes with no clicking, at the same X and Y and turned the same way: Into the same model, on the same storey: two items in exactly one spot would be no use, so the copy goes right beside the original, one item width plus 100 mm along the item's own width. Paste in place again from the new copy to make a row. Onto another storey or into another model: exactly the same X, Y and turn. BimCraft uses the storey with the same name; if there is none, the one at the nearest height. The status line says which. Duplicate More, Duplicate or Ctrl+D copies the picked item and starts pasting it straight away: one step instead of two. Into another model or another tab What you copy is kept in this browser, so you can open another IFC model, or BimCraft in another tab, and paste there. The other model needs the library item itself: BimCraft has it when the item was placed, swapped in or updated in this tab. An item placed in an earlier session can be pasted again in its own model only; in another model, place it from the Library tab. What you should see The copy in the model, picked, with the same library item, license, equipment data, quantity and note as the original, and the next free item number. Change the number in Properties, under CadCraft_Placement. \"Placed: item (item n)\" in the change list. Ctrl+Z takes it back; Ctrl+Y puts it back again. Good to know Copies share the original's type, so all of them use one set of geometry, as placing the same item twice does. Undo and Redo cover every change: values you edited, placements, pastes, swaps and updates. Redo puts a placement back at the same place; a new change clears what could be redone. In the 2D view of a drawing there is nothing to select, so there is nothing to copy. Use Edit in CadCraft to change the drawing, when it shows. Common mistakes Paste says the elements came from another model. The last thing you copied was a building element, or several elements: those paste into their own model only. Pick one placed library item and copy it. Paste says the copy came from another model without its library item. Place the item from the Library tab once in this tab, then copy and paste it. Ctrl+C copies page text instead. Text was selected on the page. Click the model first. Related Keys and mouse BimCraft settings"},{"slug":"edit-elements","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements","summary":"Pick walls, doors, furniture or anything else in an IFC model; delete them, cut them, or copy and paste them where you click, in place, or on another storey.","section":"tasks","order":156,"keywords":["delete","remove","erase","cut","copy","paste","duplicate","move","select all","pick all","building element","wall","door","window","stair","opening","undo"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"what-goes-with-an-element","text":"What goes with an element","level":2},{"id":"what-you-should-see","text":"What you should see","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange what is in an IFC model: take out elements you do not want, copy a wall or a set of furniture to another place, or move elements by cutting and pasting them. Every change is one step you can undo, and saving writes a new file, so your original stays as it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen an IFC model. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-ifc-model\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-ifc-model\"\u003EOpen an IFC model and look around in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick what you want to change: click an element, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click to pick more, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E) to pick every element shown.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThen:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopy:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECut:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E. The elements go, and you can paste them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo paste, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E. A see-through box the size of what you copied follows the pointer on the storey chosen in the placing bar. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to turn it 90°, then click where it goes. Each click places another copy; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E): after a cut the elements go back exactly where they were; after a copy the copy goes right beside the originals.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E to download the edited model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-goes-with-an-element\"\u003EWhat goes with an element\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParts:\u003C/strong\u003E a stair takes its flights, a curtain wall its panels, anything made of parts takes them all.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpenings:\u003C/strong\u003E a wall takes the openings cut in it. Deleting a door or window also takes the opening it filled, so the wall closes, as it does in an authoring program. Deleting a wall leaves its doors and windows where they were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EData:\u003C/strong\u003E a copy has the same type, materials, property sets and quantities as the original, and a new GlobalId. Changing a value on the copy later leaves the original alone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary items\u003C/strong\u003E keep their library data; a copy gets the next free item number, and a cut item pasted back keeps its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-should-see\"\u003EWhat you should see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Deleted: …&quot; or &quot;Pasted: …&quot; in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the last change back; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter a delete, the status line says how many parts and openings went too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPasted elements are picked, so you can paste in place again to make a row, or delete them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopies use the same geometry as the original (IFC lets elements share it), so the file grows very little.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBuilding elements paste into the model they were copied from, in the same tab. To use placed library items in another model, copy one item at a time: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate library items\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStoreys, spaces, buildings and the site cannot be deleted or copied. Pick the elements in them instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy also puts the names and properties of what you copied on your clipboard as text, to paste into a spreadsheet or an e-mail.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E is greyed out.\u003C/strong\u003E Nothing is picked, or only storeys or spaces are. Point at it to read why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPaste says the elements came from another model.\u003C/strong\u003E You opened another model since you copied them. Open the first model again and copy them there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe pasted copy is on another floor.\u003C/strong\u003E The placing bar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStorey\u003C/span\u003E decides where it goes; pick the storey before you click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/keyboard#edit\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"edit\"\u003EKeys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you'll do Change what is in an IFC model: take out elements you do not want, copy a wall or a set of furniture to another place, or move elements by cutting and pasting them. Every change is one step you can undo, and saving writes a new file, so your original stays as it was. Steps Open an IFC model. See Open an IFC model and look around in 3D. Pick what you want to change: click an element, hold Ctrl or Shift and click to pick more, or press Ctrl+A (More, Select all) to pick every element shown. Then: Delete: press Delete, or More, Delete. Copy: press Ctrl+C or click Copy. Cut: press Ctrl+X, or More, Cut. The elements go, and you can paste them. To paste, press Ctrl+V or click Paste. A see-through box the size of what you copied follows the pointer on the storey chosen in the placing bar. Press R to turn it 90°, then click where it goes. Each click places another copy; press Esc to stop. Or press Ctrl+Shift+V (More, Paste in place): after a cut the elements go back exactly where they were; after a copy the copy goes right beside the originals. Click Save as IFC to download the edited model. What goes with an element Parts: a stair takes its flights, a curtain wall its panels, anything made of parts takes them all. Openings: a wall takes the openings cut in it. Deleting a door or window also takes the opening it filled, so the wall closes, as it does in an authoring program. Deleting a wall leaves its doors and windows where they were. Data: a copy has the same type, materials, property sets and quantities as the original, and a new GlobalId. Changing a value on the copy later leaves the original alone. Library items keep their library data; a copy gets the next free item number, and a cut item pasted back keeps its own. What you should see \"Deleted: …\" or \"Pasted: …\" in the Changes list. Ctrl+Z takes the last change back; Ctrl+Y puts it back again. After a delete, the status line says how many parts and openings went too. Pasted elements are picked, so you can paste in place again to make a row, or delete them. Good to know Copies use the same geometry as the original (IFC lets elements share it), so the file grows very little. Building elements paste into the model they were copied from, in the same tab. To use placed library items in another model, copy one item at a time: see Copy, paste and duplicate library items. Storeys, spaces, buildings and the site cannot be deleted or copied. Pick the elements in them instead. Copy also puts the names and properties of what you copied on your clipboard as text, to paste into a spreadsheet or an e-mail. Common mistakes Delete is greyed out. Nothing is picked, or only storeys or spaces are. Point at it to read why. Paste says the elements came from another model. You opened another model since you copied them. Open the first model again and copy them there. The pasted copy is on another floor. The placing bar's Storey decides where it goes; pick the storey before you click. Related Hide, isolate and see through elements Keys and mouse"},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Coming from other BIM and drawing programs","summary":"How habits from desktop BIM authoring and DWG viewing map to BimCraft, and the differences that trip people up.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","migrate","familiar","desktop bim","family","parameters","project browser","properties palette","view cube","xref","layer manager","differences","compare"],"headings":[{"id":"habits-mapped","text":"Habits mapped","level":2},{"id":"from-viewing-dwg-files","text":"From viewing DWG files","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-trip-people-up","text":"Differences that trip people up","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you know Revit, most of BimCraft will feel familiar in words and unfamiliar in scope. The single most important difference: \u003Cstrong\u003Ethis build reads and inspects models and edits IFC data. It does not author like Revit yet.\u003C/strong\u003E You cannot draw walls, place families, make views or sheets, or save a project file. What you can do is open almost any building file on any computer with a browser, see what is in it, check a whole project folder, and correct the data in an IFC model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"habits-mapped\"\u003EHabits mapped\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIf you are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn BimCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening an \u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E in Revit to see the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft opens the \u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E as a file card: release, project information, links, preview. For the 3D model, export IFC and open that. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/read-rvt-file\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/read-rvt-file\"\u003ERead a project or family file\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChecking which release a file was saved in before opening it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe card says &quot;saved in Revit …&quot; at the top, without upgrading or touching the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe Project Browser\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo browser of views and sheets. An IFC model has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E lists; a drawing has sheet tabs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe Properties palette\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel on the right, for the element you clicked. IFC property sets instead of parameter groups\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInstance and type parameters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInstance property sets, and sets marked &quot;(type)&quot;. A type change applies to every element of the type, as in Revit. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/type-vs-instance\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/type-vs-instance\"\u003EChange a type property for every element of a type\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit Type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo dialog: click a value in a &quot;(type)&quot; set and type\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelecting many elements, filters, Select All Instances\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne element at a time. Not built yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe view cube and the navigation wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA view cube in the bottom-right corner, four buttons (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E) and free orbit. No navigation wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShift and middle-drag to orbit, middle-drag to pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft-drag orbits, right-drag (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag) pans, wheel zooms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFloor plans, sections, section boxes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E plane at a time (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E); no section box or 2D plans. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHide in view, isolate, visibility/graphics\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E on and off, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges\u003C/span\u003E. No per-element hiding yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERooms\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC spaces, hidden at first. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave, Save As, synchronize with central\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E downloads a new IFC file. The original never changes. No central, no worksharing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on IFC edits until you save. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-ifc-properties\"\u003EChange IFC properties and save an edited copy\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EManage Links\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the project folder: every link is checked and marked \u003Cstrong\u003EIn this folder\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESame name found\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary file\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENot in this folder\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/open-project-folder\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/open-project-folder\"\u003EOpen a whole project folder\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAudit, or opening a file to see if it is corrupt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe folder report checks every copy and backup for being cut off, in seconds. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/check-folder-health\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/check-folder-health\"\u003ECheck a folder for damaged copies\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicking the right file from a messy folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart here\u003C/span\u003E box suggests one, with reasons\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"from-viewing-dwg-files\"\u003EFrom viewing DWG files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you open DWGs in AutoCAD or a DWG viewer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIf you are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn BimCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModel and layout tabs at the bottom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETabs above the drawing: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E and one per sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZOOM Extents, double-click the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with any button\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe layer manager\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E list: tick to show, untick to hide, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll on\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll off\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe xref manager\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/strong\u003E on the right, with \u003Cstrong\u003EShown\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENot found\u003C/strong\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd linked drawings…\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot preview\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: black on white with line weights. It does not print\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EData extraction of block attributes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/span\u003E tab for tagged foodservice drawings, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure, draw, edit, save as\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot in BimCraft. The CadCraft drafting app draws and edits\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-trip-people-up\"\u003EDifferences that trip people up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt runs in the browser, and the browser is careful with your files.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft can only read files and folders you hand it, by choosing them or dragging them in. It cannot open a file by itself, and it cannot save over one. Every save is a download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E The words &quot;upload&quot; in some browsers&#039; folder dialogs are the browser&#039;s own wording. The files stay on your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIFC is the model format.\u003C/strong\u003E A project file opens as a card because its 3D content is undocumented. Treat IFC as the way models travel into BimCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdits are copy-on-write.\u003C/strong\u003E Changing one element&#039;s value never changes its neighbors, even when the exporter shared that value between them. Type properties are the deliberate exception.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo project, no views.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft shows one file at a time and remembers nothing about it after you close the tab, except the names of recent folders in Chrome and Edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMac keys.\u003C/strong\u003E Where Revit&#039;s Windows shortcuts use Ctrl, BimCraft&#039;s shortcuts use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: Command on a Mac.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYour mouse habits.\u003C/strong\u003E Orbit is the left button, not Shift and the middle button. You will try the old way a few times.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E for what to use instead today.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you know Revit, most of BimCraft will feel familiar in words and unfamiliar in scope. The single most important difference: this build reads and inspects models and edits IFC data. It does not author like Revit yet. You cannot draw walls, place families, make views or sheets, or save a project file. What you can do is open almost any building file on any computer with a browser, see what is in it, check a whole project folder, and correct the data in an IFC model. Habits mapped If you are used to In BimCraft Opening an .rvt in Revit to see the model BimCraft opens the .rvt as a file card: release, project information, links, preview. For the 3D model, export IFC and open that. Read a project or family file Checking which release a file was saved in before opening it The card says \"saved in Revit …\" at the top, without upgrading or touching the file The Project Browser No browser of views and sheets. An IFC model has Storeys and Elements lists; a drawing has sheet tabs The Properties palette The Properties panel on the right, for the element you clicked. IFC property sets instead of parameter groups Instance and type parameters Instance property sets, and sets marked \"(type)\". A type change applies to every element of the type, as in Revit. Change a type property for every element of a type Edit Type No dialog: click a value in a \"(type)\" set and type Selecting many elements, filters, Select All Instances One element at a time. Not built yet The view cube and the navigation wheel A view cube in the bottom-right corner, four buttons (Fit, Top, Front, Side) and free orbit. No navigation wheel Shift and middle-drag to orbit, middle-drag to pan Left-drag orbits, right-drag (or Shift and drag) pans, wheel zooms Floor plans, sections, section boxes One Section plane at a time (X); no section box or 2D plans. Cut an IFC model with a section plane Hide in view, isolate, visibility/graphics Storeys on and off, Spaces and Edges. No per-element hiding yet Rooms IFC spaces, hidden at first. Click Spaces Save, Save As, synchronize with central Save as IFC downloads a new IFC file. The original never changes. No central, no worksharing Undo with Ctrl+Z Works on IFC edits until you save. Change IFC properties and save an edited copy Manage Links Open the project folder: every link is checked and marked In this folder, Same name found, Library file or Not in this folder. Open a whole project folder Audit, or opening a file to see if it is corrupt The folder report checks every copy and backup for being cut off, in seconds. Check a folder for damaged copies Picking the right file from a messy folder The Start here box suggests one, with reasons From viewing DWG files If you open DWGs in AutoCAD or a DWG viewer: If you are used to In BimCraft Model and layout tabs at the bottom Tabs above the drawing: Model and one per sheet ZOOM Extents, double-click the wheel Double-click, F or Fit Pan with the middle button Drag with any button The layer manager The Layers list: tick to show, untick to hide, All on and All off The xref manager Linked drawings on the right, with Shown or Not found, and Add linked drawings… Plot preview Print view or P: black on white with line weights. It does not print Data extraction of block attributes The Equipment tab for tagged foodservice drawings, with Download CSV Measure, draw, edit, save as Not in BimCraft. The CadCraft drafting app draws and edits Differences that trip people up It runs in the browser, and the browser is careful with your files. BimCraft can only read files and folders you hand it, by choosing them or dragging them in. It cannot open a file by itself, and it cannot save over one. Every save is a download. Nothing is uploaded. The words \"upload\" in some browsers' folder dialogs are the browser's own wording. The files stay on your computer. IFC is the model format. A project file opens as a card because its 3D content is undocumented. Treat IFC as the way models travel into BimCraft. Edits are copy-on-write. Changing one element's value never changes its neighbors, even when the exporter shared that value between them. Type properties are the deliberate exception. No project, no views. BimCraft shows one file at a time and remembers nothing about it after you close the tab, except the names of recent folders in Chrome and Edge. Mac keys. Where Revit's Windows shortcuts use Ctrl, BimCraft's shortcuts use Ctrl: Command on a Mac. Your mouse habits. Orbit is the left button, not Shift and the middle button. You will try the old way a few times. See What is not built yet for what to use instead today."},{"slug":"settings","scope":"bimcraft","title":"BimCraft settings","summary":"Choose whether rooms and spaces show, whether edges are drawn and which view a model opens in, and start placing with wall snap on.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["settings","preferences","options","spaces","rooms","ifcspace","edges","lines","performance","large model","opening view","camera","top view","wall snap","snapping"],"headings":[{"id":"when-a-model-opens","text":"When a model opens","level":2},{"id":"opening-view","text":"Opening view","level":2},{"id":"placing-library-items","text":"Placing library items","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2},{"id":"assistants","text":"Assistants","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBimCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). BimCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset BimCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of the dialog puts them, and the shared ones, back to the start.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-model-opens\"\u003EWhen a model opens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EShow rooms and spaces\u003C/strong\u003E (off at the start): rooms and spaces are see-through boxes that can hide the walls behind them, so they start hidden. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E button above the model still turns them on and off for the open model.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEdges\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges on\u003C/span\u003E (the start) draws the edge lines that give the drafting look. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESmall models only\u003C/span\u003E draws them only for models under 300,000 triangles, which keeps big models quicker to turn. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges off\u003C/span\u003E starts every model without them. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges\u003C/span\u003E button still switches them for the open model. Above 1.5 million triangles edges are always off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-view\"\u003EOpening view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe camera a model opens with: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E (the start, the whole model from a corner), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E. The buttons above the model change the view any time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"placing-library-items\"\u003EPlacing library items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStart with wall snap on\u003C/strong\u003E (off at the start): when placing or pasting a library item starts, its back snaps to the nearest wall, facing the room. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall snap\u003C/span\u003E in the placing bar turns it off for the moment. A storey with no walls starts without it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat sits in the bottom-right corner of the 3D views (an IFC model, and a drawing switched to 3D): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView cube\u003C/span\u003E (the start), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis icon\u003C/span\u003E (a small X, Y and Z) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E. It changes at once. The 2D drawing view never shows one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the view cube, click a face, an edge or a corner to look from that side, drag the cube to orbit, drag the ring to turn round, and click the house for the starting view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft\u003C/strong\u003E (off at the start) lets an assistant on your computer reach the open model through CadCraft Studio: \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E finds elements and reads their properties, \u003Cstrong\u003EChange the view\u003C/strong\u003E isolates, hides and shows them. \u003Cstrong\u003EActivity\u003C/strong\u003E lists its calls. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/assistants\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/assistants\"\u003ELet an AI assistant work with your model\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe other settings apply when a model opens or placing starts. A model already open keeps how it looks until you use the toolbar buttons or open it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate library items\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/place-from-library\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/place-from-library\"\u003EPlace equipment and furniture from the library\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe BimCraft screen\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/assistants\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/assistants\"\u003ELet an AI assistant work with your model\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, BimCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). BimCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. Reset BimCraft settings at the bottom of the dialog puts them, and the shared ones, back to the start. When a model opens Show rooms and spaces (off at the start): rooms and spaces are see-through boxes that can hide the walls behind them, so they start hidden. The Spaces button above the model still turns them on and off for the open model. Edges: Edges on (the start) draws the edge lines that give the drafting look. Small models only draws them only for models under 300,000 triangles, which keeps big models quicker to turn. Edges off starts every model without them. The Edges button still switches them for the open model. Above 1.5 million triangles edges are always off. Opening view The camera a model opens with: Fit (the start, the whole model from a corner), Top, Front or Side. The buttons above the model change the view any time. Placing library items Start with wall snap on (off at the start): when placing or pasting a library item starts, its back snaps to the nearest wall, facing the room. Tab or Wall snap in the placing bar turns it off for the moment. A storey with no walls starts without it. Corner indicator What sits in the bottom-right corner of the 3D views (an IFC model, and a drawing switched to 3D): View cube (the start), Axis icon (a small X, Y and Z) or None. It changes at once. The 2D drawing view never shows one. On the view cube, click a face, an edge or a corner to look from that side, drag the cube to orbit, drag the ring to turn round, and click the house for the starting view. Assistants Let AI assistants and scripts use BimCraft (off at the start) lets an assistant on your computer reach the open model through CadCraft Studio: Read finds elements and reads their properties, Change the view isolates, hides and shows them. Activity lists its calls. See Let an AI assistant work with your model. The other settings apply when a model opens or placing starts. A model already open keeps how it looks until you use the toolbar buttons or open it again. Related Copy, paste and duplicate library items Place equipment and furniture from the library The BimCraft screen Let an AI assistant work with your model"},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Keys and mouse","summary":"Every key, mouse, trackpad and touch action in BimCraft, grouped by what you are looking at.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","mouse","trackpad","touch","orbit","pan","zoom","hide","isolate","see-through","undo","redo","copy","paste","duplicate","save","fit","print view","escape","settings"],"headings":[{"id":"everywhere","text":"Everywhere","level":2},{"id":"3d-view-ifc-models","text":"3D view (IFC models)","level":2},{"id":"edit","text":"Edit","level":2},{"id":"placing-and-pasting-library-items","text":"Placing and pasting library items","level":2},{"id":"editing-ifc-values","text":"Editing IFC values","level":2},{"id":"2d-drawings-dwg-and-dxf","text":"2D drawings (DWG and DXF)","level":2},{"id":"drawings-in-3d","text":"Drawings in 3D","level":2},{"id":"files","text":"Files","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft has few shortcuts. Most work is done with the mouse and the panels. Shortcuts written with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E use Command on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E for how keys are written in this help.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everywhere\"\u003EEverywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECycle the screen modes: normal, full screen, full screen with the bars hidden. Ctrl on every platform, including a Mac\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeave full screen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/settings\"\u003EBimCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove between buttons, checkboxes and values. Every control can be reached from the keyboard\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-view-ifc-models\"\u003E3D view (IFC models)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit the whole model, three-quarter view (same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClear the selection, or stop placing or pasting\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick the element under the pointer; click empty background to clear\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd an element to the pick, or take it out\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHide the picked elements. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/hide-and-isolate\"\u003EHide, isolate and see through elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow only the picked elements (isolate)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow the picked elements, everything else see-through\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift H\"\u003E⇧H\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+H\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow every element again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWalk\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThen \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"W\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"D\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or arrows walk, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E runs, drag looks round, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/walk\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/walk\"\u003EWalk through a model\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure distances and face areas. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances and face areas in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with the left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan (handy on a trackpad)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel, or drag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger scroll or pinch on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit; a tap picks\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch to zoom, move together to pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E| \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E | Section plane on and off. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E |\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are no keys for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETop\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFront\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide\u003C/span\u003E; use the buttons.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit\"\u003EEdit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same commands are in the toolbar: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, which shows each key. They work in the 3D view of an IFC model, not while you type in a field. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate library items\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo the last change\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo what Undo took back\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the picked elements (their names and properties also go to the clipboard as text)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut the picked elements: copy them, then delete them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste what you copied: it follows the pointer, click where it goes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place: where a cut came from, or beside the originals after a copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate: copy the picked elements and start pasting them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the picked elements, with their parts and openings. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-elements\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-elements\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste building elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick every element shown\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect none\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen text is selected on the page, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies that text as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"placing-and-pasting-library-items\"\u003EPlacing and pasting library items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurn the item 90°\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWall snap on and off: the item&#039;s back goes against the nearest wall\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in \u003Cstrong\u003EX, Y (mm)\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlace at the typed position\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStop placing or pasting\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing-ifc-values\"\u003EEditing IFC values\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep the value you typed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancel and put the old value back\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick elsewhere\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep the value you typed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo the last change. Only while you have unsaved changes and are not typing in a field\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo what Undo took back\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave as IFC. Only while you have unsaved changes; otherwise the browser&#039;s own save command opens\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo edit with the keyboard alone: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E until a value is highlighted, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start typing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"2d-drawings-dwg-and-dxf\"\u003E2D drawings (DWG and DXF)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit the drawing or the sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrint view on and off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with any button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom at the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger scroll or pinch on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom at the pointer. Two-finger scroll does not pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan. Pinch zoom is not built yet for drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings-in-3d\"\u003EDrawings in 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D on and off (also on the 2D view)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtents: the whole drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the middle or left button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScroll the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom toward what is under the pointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo-finger swipe on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit; with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers (touch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch to zoom, move together to pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"3\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E do nothing while you type in a field, while the equipment schedule is showing, or when you hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a file onto the page\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a folder onto the page\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen it as a project folder, with no permission prompt\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"BimCraft has few shortcuts. Most work is done with the mouse and the panels. Shortcuts written with Ctrl use Command on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere. See Keys, mice and trackpads for how keys are written in this help. Everywhere Key What it does Ctrl+Shift+F Cycle the screen modes: normal, full screen, full screen with the bars hidden. Ctrl on every platform, including a Mac Esc Leave full screen Ctrl+, Open Settings. See BimCraft settings Tab Move between buttons, checkboxes and values. Every control can be reached from the keyboard 3D view (IFC models) Key or action What it does F Fit the whole model, three-quarter view (same as Fit) Esc Clear the selection, or stop placing or pasting Click Pick the element under the pointer; click empty background to clear Ctrl+click or Shift+click Add an element to the pick, or take it out H Hide the picked elements. See Hide, isolate and see through elements I Show only the picked elements (isolate) T Show the picked elements, everything else see-through Shift+H Show every element again Walk Then W A S D or arrows walk, Shift runs, drag looks round, Esc stops. See Walk through a model M Measure distances and face areas. See Measure distances and face areas in 3D Drag with the left button Orbit Drag with the right button Pan Shift or Ctrl and drag with the left button Pan (handy on a trackpad) Scroll the wheel, or drag with the middle button Zoom Two-finger scroll or pinch on a trackpad Zoom One finger (touch) Orbit; a tap picks Two fingers (touch) Pinch to zoom, move together to pan | X | Section plane on and off. See Cut an IFC model with a section plane | There are no keys for Top, Front and Side; use the buttons. Edit The same commands are in the toolbar: Undo, Redo, Copy, Paste and the More menu, which shows each key. They work in the 3D view of an IFC model, not while you type in a field. See Copy, paste and duplicate library items. Key What it does Ctrl+Z Undo the last change Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo what Undo took back Ctrl+C Copy the picked elements (their names and properties also go to the clipboard as text) Ctrl+X Cut the picked elements: copy them, then delete them Ctrl+V Paste what you copied: it follows the pointer, click where it goes Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place: where a cut came from, or beside the originals after a copy Ctrl+D Duplicate: copy the picked elements and start pasting them Delete Delete the picked elements, with their parts and openings. See Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements Ctrl+A Pick every element shown Esc Select none When text is selected on the page, Ctrl+C copies that text as usual. Placing and pasting library items Key What it does R Turn the item 90° Tab Wall snap on and off: the item's back goes against the nearest wall Enter in X, Y (mm) Place at the typed position Esc Stop placing or pasting Editing IFC values Key What it does Enter Keep the value you typed Esc Cancel and put the old value back Click elsewhere Keep the value you typed Ctrl+Z Undo the last change. Only while you have unsaved changes and are not typing in a field Ctrl+Y Redo what Undo took back Ctrl+S Save as IFC. Only while you have unsaved changes; otherwise the browser's own save command opens To edit with the keyboard alone: press Tab until a value is highlighted, then press Enter to start typing. 2D drawings (DWG and DXF) Key or action What it does F Fit the drawing or the sheet P Print view on and off Double-click Fit Drag with any button Pan Scroll the wheel Zoom at the pointer Two-finger scroll or pinch on a trackpad Zoom at the pointer. Two-finger scroll does not pan One finger (touch) Pan. Pinch zoom is not built yet for drawings Drawings in 3D Key or action What it does 3 3D on and off (also on the 2D view) F Extents: the whole drawing Drag with the middle or left button Orbit Drag with the right button, or Shift and drag Pan Scroll the wheel Zoom toward what is under the pointer Two-finger swipe on a trackpad Orbit; with Shift, pan Pinch on a trackpad Zoom One finger (touch) Orbit Two fingers (touch) Pinch to zoom, move together to pan F, P and 3 do nothing while you type in a field, while the equipment schedule is showing, or when you hold Ctrl or Alt. Files Action What it does Drag a file onto the page Open it Drag a folder onto the page Open it as a project folder, with no permission prompt"},{"slug":"file-formats","scope":"bimcraft","title":"What BimCraft reads from each format","summary":"Exactly what BimCraft reads from IFC, project and family files, DWG and DXF, and what it leaves alone.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["formats","ifc","ifc2x3","ifc4","rvt","rfa","rte","rft","dwg","dxf","dgn","ifczip","versions","compatibility","what is read"],"headings":[{"id":"ifc","text":"IFC","level":2},{"id":"project-and-family-files","text":"Project and family files","level":2},{"id":"pln-pla-tpl-and-bpn-project-files","text":".pln, .pla, .tpl and .bpn project files","level":2},{"id":"dwg-and-dxf","text":"DWG and DXF","level":2},{"id":"in-a-folder","text":"In a folder","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"ifc\"\u003EIFC\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERead\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDetails\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGeometry\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery element with a shape, colored as the file says. Rooms and spaces are drawn see-through and hidden at first\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStoreys\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuilding storeys in elevation order, and which elements sit on each. Elements on no storey are grouped as &quot;Not on a storey&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElement counts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many elements of each kind (walls, doors, slabs…), shown in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject facts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject name, long name and phase; schema; the exporting program, author and export date from the file header\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElement data\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName, type, object type, tag, description, GlobalId; every property set and quantity set on the element and on its type\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEditable: name, object type, tag, description, and single property values. Saved as a new IFC file. Schemas IFC2X3, IFC4 and IFC4X3 open, read by BimCraft&#039;s own IFC engine; anything it cannot draw yet is listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E panel. The model is moved to the origin when it opens, so a model placed far from it still shows in the middle of the screen. \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E do not open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"project-and-family-files\"\u003EProject and family files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E projects, \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E project templates, \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E families and \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E family templates.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERead\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDetails\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile information\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERelease and build, worksharing, central model path, last saved path, user name, locale, document GUID\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject information\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s own fields (name, number, client, address, status…), without new-project defaults\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamily information\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamily name, category, release, last update, and its parameters by group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe list of linked models, CAD links, images and keynote tables the file points to\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPreview picture\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe thumbnail saved in the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot read: the 3D model, elements, views, sheets and schedules. They are stored in an undocumented format. BimCraft reads only a few tens of kilobytes from each file, so file size barely matters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pln-pla-tpl-and-bpn-project-files\"\u003E.pln, .pla, .tpl and .bpn project files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot read yet: BimCraft says so when you open one. Export IFC for the model and DWG for the drawings; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pln-project-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pln-project-files\"\u003EOpen .pln and .pla project files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dwg-and-dxf\"\u003EDWG and DXF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERead\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDetails\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpaces\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModel space and every sheet (layout), in tab order\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EViewports\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach sheet&#039;s viewports, with their scale, twist and frozen layers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName, color, on/off, frozen, plot flag and line weight\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses, splines, hatches and solids, text and multiline text, blocks and attributes, dimensions, leaders and multileaders, tables, points, images\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawn where they are inserted, when their files are found\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImages\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawn from the picture files when found (PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP, WebP; not TIFF)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment tags\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFoodservice item tags (\u003Ccode\u003EKCL-ITEM\u003C/code\u003E attributes), for the equipment schedule\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnything else is counted under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E, never guessed. DWG formats from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 are the ones checked (R13 and R14 files are accepted too, but none were in the test set; older DWGs do not open); the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E panel shows the format code (such as AC1032). DXF files of every version open, R12 included. Both are read by BimCraft&#039;s own readers, in this tab. Nothing is saved back to DWG or DXF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-a-folder\"\u003EIn a folder\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKind\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExtensions\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the report\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrouped with numbered backups, health-checked, card read, links checked\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EListed; opens in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECAD\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.dgn\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EListed with the models that link them; DWG and DXF open in 2D, DGN does not\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeftovers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dwl\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.dwl2\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.bak\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.err\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.log\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.tmp\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.sv$\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.ac$\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.lck\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.slog\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EListed under \u003Cstrong\u003ESet aside\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHousekeeping folders\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*_backup\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*_log\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERevit_temp\u003C/code\u003E, hidden folders\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EListed under \u003Cstrong\u003ESet aside\u003C/strong\u003E, not read\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/folder-roles\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/folder-roles\"\u003EFolder report: roles, statuses and links\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"IFC Read Details Geometry Every element with a shape, colored as the file says. Rooms and spaces are drawn see-through and hidden at first Storeys Building storeys in elevation order, and which elements sit on each. Elements on no storey are grouped as \"Not on a storey\" Element counts How many elements of each kind (walls, doors, slabs…), shown in Elements Project facts Project name, long name and phase; schema; the exporting program, author and export date from the file header Element data Name, type, object type, tag, description, GlobalId; every property set and quantity set on the element and on its type Editable: name, object type, tag, description, and single property values. Saved as a new IFC file. Schemas IFC2X3, IFC4 and IFC4X3 open, read by BimCraft's own IFC engine; anything it cannot draw yet is listed under Not drawn yet in the File panel. The model is moved to the origin when it opens, so a model placed far from it still shows in the middle of the screen. .ifczip and .ifcxml do not open. Project and family files .rvt projects, .rte project templates, .rfa families and .rft family templates. Read Details File information Release and build, worksharing, central model path, last saved path, user name, locale, document GUID Project information The project's own fields (name, number, client, address, status…), without new-project defaults Family information Family name, category, release, last update, and its parameters by group Links The list of linked models, CAD links, images and keynote tables the file points to Preview picture The thumbnail saved in the file Not read: the 3D model, elements, views, sheets and schedules. They are stored in an undocumented format. BimCraft reads only a few tens of kilobytes from each file, so file size barely matters. .pln, .pla, .tpl and .bpn project files Not read yet: BimCraft says so when you open one. Export IFC for the model and DWG for the drawings; see Open .pln and .pla project files. DWG and DXF Read Details Spaces Model space and every sheet (layout), in tab order Viewports Each sheet's viewports, with their scale, twist and frozen layers Layers Name, color, on/off, frozen, plot flag and line weight Objects Lines, polylines, arcs, circles, ellipses, splines, hatches and solids, text and multiline text, blocks and attributes, dimensions, leaders and multileaders, tables, points, images Linked drawings Drawn where they are inserted, when their files are found Images Drawn from the picture files when found (PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP, WebP; not TIFF) Equipment tags Foodservice item tags (KCL-ITEM attributes), for the equipment schedule Anything else is counted under Not drawn yet, never guessed. DWG formats from AutoCAD 2000 to 2018 are the ones checked (R13 and R14 files are accepted too, but none were in the test set; older DWGs do not open); the Drawing panel shows the format code (such as AC1032). DXF files of every version open, R12 included. Both are read by BimCraft's own readers, in this tab. Nothing is saved back to DWG or DXF. In a folder Kind Extensions In the report Models .rvt .rfa .rte .rft Grouped with numbered backups, health-checked, card read, links checked IFC .ifc Listed; opens in 3D CAD .dwg .dxf .dgn Listed with the models that link them; DWG and DXF open in 2D, DGN does not Leftovers .dwl .dwl2 .bak .err .log .tmp .sv$ .ac$ .lck .slog Listed under Set aside Housekeeping folders *_backup, *_log, Revit_temp, hidden folders Listed under Set aside, not read See Folder report: roles, statuses and links."},{"slug":"properties-panel","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Properties panel fields","summary":"What every field in the Properties panel and the File, Drawing and Folder panels means, and which ones you can change.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["properties","fields","attributes","globalid","guid","tag","object type","line","express id","property set","pset","quantities","schema","file panel"],"headings":[{"id":"an-ifc-element","text":"An IFC element","level":2},{"id":"the-file-panel-ifc","text":"The File panel (IFC)","level":2},{"id":"the-file-panel-project-and-family-files","text":"The File panel (project and family files)","level":2},{"id":"the-drawing-panel-dwg-and-dxf","text":"The Drawing panel (DWG and DXF)","level":2},{"id":"the-folder-panel","text":"The Folder panel","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"an-ifc-element\"\u003EAn IFC element\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E panel shows the element&#039;s kind without the &quot;Ifc&quot; prefix (for example &quot;Door&quot;, &quot;WallStandardCase&quot;, &quot;FurnishingElement&quot;) and its name, or &quot;Unnamed&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEditable\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EName\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe element&#039;s name, often the family and type name from the exporter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name of the element&#039;s type, when it has one\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObject type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA free-text type description some exporters fill\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe element&#039;s tag or mark; many exporters write the element ID here\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDescription\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFree text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGlobalId\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe element&#039;s permanent 22-character IFC identifier, the same in every export of the same element\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe element&#039;s line number (\u003Ccode\u003E#1234\u003C/code\u003E) in this IFC file. It changes between exports\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelow come the \u003Cstrong\u003Eproperty sets\u003C/strong\u003E, sorted by name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach set opens and closes when you click its name. The first three are open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA set marked &quot;(type)&quot; belongs to the element&#039;s type: &quot;Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EQuantity sets (lengths, areas, volumes, counts, weights) are read-only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELists and enumerated values are shown, read-only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYes/No values show as \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003ENo\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENumbers are rounded to four decimals for display, in the file&#039;s own units, without a unit symbol.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn empty value shows as \u003Cem\u003Eempty\u003C/em\u003E. An edited value is bold with a blue bar beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-file-panel-ifc\"\u003EThe File panel (IFC)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile, Size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file you opened\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESchema\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe IFC version, such as IFC2X3 or IFC4\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s name and long name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPhase\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project phase, if the exporter wrote one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExported from\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe exporting program, from the file header\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAuthor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe author in the file header\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExported\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe date and time in the file header\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElements\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many elements with a shape were drawn\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETriangles\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many triangles their shapes make; a measure of how heavy the model is\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-file-panel-project-and-family-files\"\u003EThe File panel (project and family files)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKind\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject, Project template, Family or Family template\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaved in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe release that last saved the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuild\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe exact build of that release\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow much of the file BimCraft read, out of its size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-drawing-panel-dwg-and-dxf\"\u003EThe Drawing panel (DWG and DXF)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFormat\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe release the drawing was saved as, with its format code, such as AC1032\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnits\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing&#039;s insertion units, when set\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEntities\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many objects were read\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many layers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many sheets (layouts), or None\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many of its linked drawings were found and loaded\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many tagged equipment items are on sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-folder-panel\"\u003EThe Folder panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFolder, Files, Size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe folder you opened, how many files it holds and their total size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject and family files, each with its backups counted once\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECAD drawings, IFC models\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many of each\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProblems\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow many model copies are incomplete or unreadable, or None\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"An IFC element The top of the Properties panel shows the element's kind without the \"Ifc\" prefix (for example \"Door\", \"WallStandardCase\", \"FurnishingElement\") and its name, or \"Unnamed\". Field Meaning Editable Name The element's name, often the family and type name from the exporter Yes Type The name of the element's type, when it has one No Object type A free-text type description some exporters fill Yes Tag The element's tag or mark; many exporters write the element ID here Yes Description Free text Yes GlobalId The element's permanent 22-character IFC identifier, the same in every export of the same element No Line The element's line number (#1234) in this IFC file. It changes between exports No Below come the property sets, sorted by name: Each set opens and closes when you click its name. The first three are open. A set marked \"(type)\" belongs to the element's type: \"Type property: a change here applies to every element of this type.\" Quantity sets (lengths, areas, volumes, counts, weights) are read-only. Lists and enumerated values are shown, read-only. Yes/No values show as Yes or No. Numbers are rounded to four decimals for display, in the file's own units, without a unit symbol. An empty value shows as empty. An edited value is bold with a blue bar beside it. The File panel (IFC) Field Meaning File, Size The file you opened Schema The IFC version, such as IFC2X3 or IFC4 Project The project's name and long name Phase The project phase, if the exporter wrote one Exported from The exporting program, from the file header Author The author in the file header Exported The date and time in the file header Elements How many elements with a shape were drawn Triangles How many triangles their shapes make; a measure of how heavy the model is The File panel (project and family files) Field Meaning Kind Project, Project template, Family or Family template Saved in The release that last saved the file Build The exact build of that release Read How much of the file BimCraft read, out of its size The Drawing panel (DWG and DXF) Field Meaning Format The release the drawing was saved as, with its format code, such as AC1032 Units The drawing's insertion units, when set Entities How many objects were read Layers How many layers Sheets How many sheets (layouts), or None Linked drawings How many of its linked drawings were found and loaded Equipment How many tagged equipment items are on sheets The Folder panel Field Meaning Folder, Files, Size The folder you opened, how many files it holds and their total size Models Project and family files, each with its backups counted once CAD drawings, IFC models How many of each Problems How many model copies are incomplete or unreadable, or None"},{"slug":"folder-roles","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Folder report: roles, statuses and links","summary":"Every label in the folder report, what it means, and how the Start here suggestion is chosen.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["folder report","roles","status","complete","incomplete","unreadable","linked","background","starter","central","family","backups only","start here","link status"],"headings":[{"id":"copy-status","text":"Copy status","level":2},{"id":"backups","text":"Backups","level":2},{"id":"roles","text":"Roles","level":2},{"id":"how-start-here-is-chosen","text":"How Start here is chosen","level":2},{"id":"link-status","text":"Link status","level":2},{"id":"set-aside","text":"Set aside","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-status\"\u003ECopy status\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery copy of a model, main file and numbered backups alike, gets a header check.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELabel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EComplete\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file&#039;s header and index fit inside the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIncomplete\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is shorter than its own index says: it was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced, or it is a cloud file that is still &quot;online only&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnreadable\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is not a valid compound file, or could not be read\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cstrong\u003EStatus\u003C/strong\u003E column a model shows \u003Cstrong\u003EComplete\u003C/strong\u003E, an amber &quot;\u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E incomplete&quot; when at least one copy is bad but another is sound, or red \u003Cstrong\u003EIncomplete\u003C/strong\u003E when no copy is sound.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"backups\"\u003EBackups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFiles named \u003Ccode\u003EName.0001.rvt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.0002.rvt\u003C/code\u003E and so on are numbered backups of \u003Ccode\u003EName.rvt\u003C/code\u003E. They are grouped under their model, newest first, in \u003Cstrong\u003EAll … copies\u003C/strong\u003E. The card is read from the main file when it is complete, else from the newest complete backup (&quot;newest complete&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"roles\"\u003ERoles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShown in italics under a model&#039;s name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERole\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinked by \u003Cem\u003Eother model\u003C/em\u003E: background\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnother model in the folder links this one, so it is most likely someone else&#039;s background, such as a consultant&#039;s model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStarter or untitled file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA name like Blank, Template, Default, Untitled or Project1: a starting point, or a file never properly named\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECentral model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA workshared central model. Work happens in a local copy, not here\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamily (a part)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E file: a component, not the project\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackups only\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe main file is gone and only numbered backups remain\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-start-here-is-chosen\"\u003EHow Start here is chosen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft applies the reasoning an experienced user would:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECandidates are readable project files (\u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E) that are not linked by another model and not starters.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECentrals and backup-only models count only when nothing better exists.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOf the candidates, the one saved most recently wins.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe box lists its reasons, and warns when a file that looks like the obvious choice is damaged, when other models are background, and when the pick is a central model. It is a best guess; check with whoever last worked on the project.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"link-status\"\u003ELink status\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELabel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn this folder\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFound at the saved relative path, or at the end of the saved full path inside the folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESame name found\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot at its path, but a file with the same name is in the folder. Check it is the right one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary file\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA keynote or assembly code table. These come with the program on each computer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot in this folder\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot found anywhere in the folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003ELinks found\u003C/strong\u003E column in \u003Cstrong\u003EModels\u003C/strong\u003E shows how many of a model&#039;s links were found, out of those expected in the folder (library files are not counted).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-aside\"\u003ESet aside\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERevit housekeeping folders (not read)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*_backup\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E*_log\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ERevit_temp\u003C/code\u003E folders, plus hidden folders and \u003Ccode\u003E__MACOSX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELock, log and backup leftovers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dwl\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dwl2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.bak\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.err\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.log\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tmp\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.sv$\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.ac$\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.lck\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.slog\u003C/code\u003E files\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOther files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEverything else (the first 40 are named)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"Copy status Every copy of a model, main file and numbered backups alike, gets a header check. Label Meaning Complete The file's header and index fit inside the file Incomplete The file is shorter than its own index says: it was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced, or it is a cloud file that is still \"online only\" Unreadable The file is not a valid compound file, or could not be read In the Status column a model shows Complete, an amber \"n incomplete\" when at least one copy is bad but another is sound, or red Incomplete when no copy is sound. Backups Files named Name.0001.rvt, Name.0002.rvt and so on are numbered backups of Name.rvt. They are grouped under their model, newest first, in All … copies. The card is read from the main file when it is complete, else from the newest complete backup (\"newest complete\"). Roles Shown in italics under a model's name. Role Meaning Linked by other model: background Another model in the folder links this one, so it is most likely someone else's background, such as a consultant's model Starter or untitled file A name like Blank, Template, Default, Untitled or Project1: a starting point, or a file never properly named Central model A workshared central model. Work happens in a local copy, not here Family (a part) An .rfa or .rft file: a component, not the project Backups only The main file is gone and only numbered backups remain How Start here is chosen BimCraft applies the reasoning an experienced user would: Candidates are readable project files (.rvt, .rte) that are not linked by another model and not starters. Centrals and backup-only models count only when nothing better exists. Of the candidates, the one saved most recently wins. The box lists its reasons, and warns when a file that looks like the obvious choice is damaged, when other models are background, and when the pick is a central model. It is a best guess; check with whoever last worked on the project. Link status Label Meaning In this folder Found at the saved relative path, or at the end of the saved full path inside the folder Same name found Not at its path, but a file with the same name is in the folder. Check it is the right one Library file A keynote or assembly code table. These come with the program on each computer Not in this folder Not found anywhere in the folder The Links found column in Models shows how many of a model's links were found, out of those expected in the folder (library files are not counted). Set aside Line Meaning Revit housekeeping folders (not read) *_backup, *_log and Revit_temp folders, plus hidden folders and __MACOSX Lock, log and backup leftovers .dwl, .dwl2, .bak, .err, .log, .tmp, .sv$, .ac$, .lck and .slog files Other files Everything else (the first 40 are named)"},{"slug":"platforms","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Browsers, devices and memory","summary":"What differs in BimCraft between Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on Mac, Windows and Linux, with a mouse, a trackpad or touch, and how big a file it can take.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["browser","chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","phone","touch","trackpad","memory","large files","webgl","performance"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-a-folder","text":"Opening a folder","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-and-linux","text":"Mac, Windows and Linux","level":2},{"id":"mouse-trackpad-and-touch","text":"Mouse, trackpad and touch","level":2},{"id":"large-files-and-memory","text":"Large files and memory","level":2},{"id":"related","text":"Related","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft runs in current versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on Mac, Windows and Linux. The 3D view needs WebGL, which every current browser has. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E for what the apps need in general.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-a-folder\"\u003EOpening a folder\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a folder\u003C/span\u003E does this\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDragging a folder in\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecent folders\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser&#039;s folder picker, then &quot;Let site view files?&quot;: click \u003Cstrong\u003EView files\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens with no prompt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, for folders picked or dragged\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe system&#039;s folder chooser\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens with no prompt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe system&#039;s folder chooser, with an \u003Cstrong\u003EUpload\u003C/strong\u003E button (nothing is uploaded)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens with no prompt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBehind the scenes: Chrome and Edge use the File System Access picker and can remember the folder. Firefox and Safari use the older folder chooser, which hands over every file in the folder at once and cannot be remembered. A dragged folder is read entry by entry in every browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBrave and some other Chromium browsers turn the folder picker off. BimCraft then uses the system&#039;s folder chooser instead, and the browser may ask &quot;Upload \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E files to this site?&quot;. Nothing is uploaded; the files only go to the tab. Dragging the folder in avoids the question.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-and-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows and Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShortcuts written \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E are Command on a Mac and Ctrl on Windows and Linux.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (screen modes) is Control on every platform, including a Mac.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFile dialogs and the downloads folder are your system&#039;s own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-trackpad-and-touch\"\u003EMouse, trackpad and touch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInput\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E3D view\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E2D drawing\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMouse\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft-drag orbits, right-drag pans, wheel zooms, click picks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAny drag pans, wheel zooms, double-click fits\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick-drag orbits; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag pans; two-finger scroll or pinch zooms\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick-drag pans; two-finger scroll or pinch zooms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETouch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger orbits, two fingers pinch and pan, tap picks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger pans; no pinch zoom yet; tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac trackpad, right-click is a two-finger click (or Control-click). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETablets and phones can open single files and look at them, but the screen is small, many mobile browsers cannot choose folders, and large models may not fit in their memory. A computer works best.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"large-files-and-memory\"\u003ELarge files and memory\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything BimCraft reads lives in the browser tab&#039;s memory. How much a tab can use depends on the browser and your computer, more than on BimCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow much is read\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to expect\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIFC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole file, plus the 3D geometry built from it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow large depends on your computer and browser; a model of several hundred megabytes can be too much for a laptop. Over 1.5 million triangles, edges are turned off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProject and family files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbout 40 KB to 100 KB, whatever the size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEven very large central models open in a moment, also from a network drive\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe whole file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVery large drawings show &quot;This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFolders\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file list, plus a few kilobytes per model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUp to 20,000 files\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a tab runs out of memory, the browser shows its own error page (&quot;Aw, Snap!&quot; in Chrome, for example). Close other tabs, reload BimCraft and try again, or use a computer with more memory.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"related\"\u003ERelated\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/online-offline\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/online-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/troubleshooting\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/troubleshooting\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"BimCraft runs in current versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on Mac, Windows and Linux. The 3D view needs WebGL, which every current browser has. See Browsers and computers for what the apps need in general. Opening a folder Browser Open a folder does this Dragging a folder in Recent folders Chrome, Edge The browser's folder picker, then \"Let site view files?\": click View files Opens with no prompt Yes, for folders picked or dragged Firefox The system's folder chooser Opens with no prompt No Safari The system's folder chooser, with an Upload button (nothing is uploaded) Opens with no prompt No Behind the scenes: Chrome and Edge use the File System Access picker and can remember the folder. Firefox and Safari use the older folder chooser, which hands over every file in the folder at once and cannot be remembered. A dragged folder is read entry by entry in every browser. Brave and some other Chromium browsers turn the folder picker off. BimCraft then uses the system's folder chooser instead, and the browser may ask \"Upload n files to this site?\". Nothing is uploaded; the files only go to the tab. Dragging the folder in avoids the question. Mac, Windows and Linux Shortcuts written Ctrl+S and Ctrl+Z are Command on a Mac and Ctrl on Windows and Linux. Ctrl+Shift+F (screen modes) is Control on every platform, including a Mac. File dialogs and the downloads folder are your system's own. Mouse, trackpad and touch Input 3D view 2D drawing Mouse Left-drag orbits, right-drag pans, wheel zooms, click picks Any drag pans, wheel zooms, double-click fits Trackpad Click-drag orbits; Shift or Ctrl and drag pans; two-finger scroll or pinch zooms Click-drag pans; two-finger scroll or pinch zooms Touch One finger orbits, two fingers pinch and pan, tap picks One finger pans; no pinch zoom yet; tap Fit On a Mac trackpad, right-click is a two-finger click (or Control-click). See Keys, mice and trackpads. Tablets and phones can open single files and look at them, but the screen is small, many mobile browsers cannot choose folders, and large models may not fit in their memory. A computer works best. Large files and memory Everything BimCraft reads lives in the browser tab's memory. How much a tab can use depends on the browser and your computer, more than on BimCraft. File How much is read What to expect IFC The whole file, plus the 3D geometry built from it How large depends on your computer and browser; a model of several hundred megabytes can be too much for a laptop. Over 1.5 million triangles, edges are turned off Project and family files About 40 KB to 100 KB, whatever the size Even very large central models open in a moment, also from a network drive DWG and DXF The whole file Very large drawings show \"This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.\" Folders The file list, plus a few kilobytes per model Up to 20,000 files If a tab runs out of memory, the browser shows its own error page (\"Aw, Snap!\" in Chrome, for example). Close other tabs, reload BimCraft and try again, or use a computer with more memory. Related Online and offline Troubleshooting"},{"slug":"touch-screens","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Use BimCraft on a touch screen or tablet","summary":"Orbit, zoom and pick in the 3D model, pan and pinch a drawing, pick several elements, walk, and place items with your fingers.","section":"reference","order":52,"keywords":["touch","tablet","ipad","android","finger","pinch","long press","tap","orbit","walk","gestures","stylus","pen"],"headings":[{"id":"the-3d-model","text":"The 3D model","level":2},{"id":"drawings-dwg-and-dxf","text":"Drawings (DWG and DXF)","level":2},{"id":"walk","text":"Walk","level":2},{"id":"placing-library-items","text":"Placing library items","level":2},{"id":"measure","text":"Measure","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft works with fingers as well as with a mouse. The first time you touch the screen, buttons, tabs and list rows grow to finger size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-3d-model\"\u003EThe 3D model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrbit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag one finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch two fingers apart or together\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag two fingers together\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick an element\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd an element to the pick, or take it out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress and hold on it for half a second (the same as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap empty space\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pinch or a two-finger drag never picks anything, even when a finger lifts over an element.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings-dwg-and-dxf\"\u003EDrawings (DWG and DXF)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag one finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch; the point between your fingers stays put\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShow the whole sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-tap, or tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a drawing&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E view, the model&#039;s gestures apply: one finger orbits, two pinch and pan, a tap picks, a long press adds to the pick.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"walk\"\u003EWalk\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWalk\u003C/span\u003E, then tap the floor where you want to stand. A row of arrow buttons appears in the walk bar:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold an arrow to walk forward, back, or step to the side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGravity\u003C/span\u003E off, the double arrows rise and sink.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag one finger on the model to look round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone\u003C/span\u003E to stop.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"placing-library-items\"\u003EPlacing library items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap where the item goes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn 90°\u003C/span\u003E on the placing bar turns it (the \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E key on a keyboard), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWall snap\u003C/span\u003E snaps its back to the nearest wall, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop placing\u003C/span\u003E ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the first point, then the second. Points snap to corners and edges near your tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a keyboard attached to a tablet, every shortcut works as on a computer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard shortcuts\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"BimCraft works with fingers as well as with a mouse. The first time you touch the screen, buttons, tabs and list rows grow to finger size. The 3D model To Do this Orbit Drag one finger Zoom Pinch two fingers apart or together Pan Drag two fingers together Pick an element Tap it Add an element to the pick, or take it out Press and hold on it for half a second (the same as Ctrl+click) Pick nothing Tap empty space A pinch or a two-finger drag never picks anything, even when a finger lifts over an element. Drawings (DWG and DXF) To Do this Pan Drag one finger Zoom Pinch; the point between your fingers stays put Show the whole sheet Double-tap, or tap Fit In a drawing's 3D view, the model's gestures apply: one finger orbits, two pinch and pan, a tap picks, a long press adds to the pick. Walk Tap Walk, then tap the floor where you want to stand. A row of arrow buttons appears in the walk bar: Hold an arrow to walk forward, back, or step to the side. With Gravity off, the double arrows rise and sink. Drag one finger on the model to look round. Tap Done to stop. Placing library items Tap where the item goes. Turn 90° on the placing bar turns it (the R key on a keyboard), Wall snap snaps its back to the nearest wall, and Stop placing ends. Measure Tap the first point, then the second. Points snap to corners and edges near your tap. With a keyboard attached to a tablet, every shortcut works as on a computer. See Keyboard shortcuts."},{"slug":"online-offline","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Online and offline","summary":"What BimCraft loads from the site, how Save for offline makes IFC models and DWG drawings open with no connection, and why nothing you open is ever uploaded.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["offline","online","internet","connection","no network","airplane","site visit","save for offline","offline ready","upload","privacy","wasm","webassembly","ifc engine","dwg","cloud"],"headings":[{"id":"what-bimcraft-loads-from-the-site","text":"What BimCraft loads from the site","level":2},{"id":"save-bimcraft-for-offline-use","text":"Save BimCraft for offline use","level":2},{"id":"what-the-button-says","text":"What the button says","level":2},{"id":"what-never-needs-a-connection","text":"What never needs a connection","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-available-yet","text":"What is not available yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft reads your files inside the browser tab. Opening, inspecting, editing and saving never send your files anywhere, online or offline. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E explains how offline copies work in every app; this page covers what is particular to BimCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-bimcraft-loads-from-the-site\"\u003EWhat BimCraft loads from the site\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPart\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhen it loads without an offline copy\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESize\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe app itself, with the 3D viewer and the IFC engine&#039;s code\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhen you open BimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeveral megabytes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe IFC engine&#039;s WebAssembly part\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe first IFC file you open in the tab: &quot;Starting the IFC engine…&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbout 1.6 MB\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDWG and DXF drawings, project and family files and folders need nothing extra: their readers are part of the app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-bimcraft-for-offline-use\"\u003ESave BimCraft for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen BimCraft while you are online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the app bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWatch the button count up: &quot;Saving 40 of 212&quot;, for example. BimCraft&#039;s copy is about 11 MB, because it includes the IFC engine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnce it says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, IFC models, DWG and DXF drawings, project files, folders and this help all open with no connection. You do not need to open an IFC or a DWG first to &quot;warm up&quot; the readers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo update or remove the copy, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the offline copy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-button-says\"\u003EWhat the button says\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo offline copy in this browser yet. Click to save one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Saving \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E of \u003Cem\u003Em\u003C/em\u003E&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving; keep the tab open until it finishes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaved. Hover to see when; click for the menu\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome files did not download. Check your connection and click to try again. The last good copy, if any, is kept\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThis window cannot keep an offline copy (a private window or a managed computer). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the button says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, every kind of file opens as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it does not, only what this tab has already loaded works: a reader that never loaded in this tab cannot start, and opening that kind of file shows an error from the browser in the status line. Do not reload the tab until you are online again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-never-needs-a-connection\"\u003EWhat never needs a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnce BimCraft is open in the tab, these work offline even without a saved copy:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EReading project and family files and checking folders.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPicking elements, reading properties and editing IFC values in a model that is already open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E: both are downloads made in the tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-available-yet\"\u003EWhat is not available yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECloud saving.\u003C/strong\u003E Files are saved to your computer only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDesktop builds.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft runs in the browser only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"BimCraft reads your files inside the browser tab. Opening, inspecting, editing and saving never send your files anywhere, online or offline. Working offline explains how offline copies work in every app; this page covers what is particular to BimCraft. What BimCraft loads from the site Part When it loads without an offline copy Size The app itself, with the 3D viewer and the IFC engine's code When you open BimCraft Several megabytes The IFC engine's WebAssembly part The first IFC file you open in the tab: \"Starting the IFC engine…\" About 1.6 MB DWG and DXF drawings, project and family files and folders need nothing extra: their readers are part of the app. Save BimCraft for offline use Open BimCraft while you are online. Click Save for offline in the app bar at the top. Watch the button count up: \"Saving 40 of 212\", for example. BimCraft's copy is about 11 MB, because it includes the IFC engine. Wait for Offline ready. Once it says Offline ready, IFC models, DWG and DXF drawings, project files, folders and this help all open with no connection. You do not need to open an IFC or a DWG first to \"warm up\" the readers. To update or remove the copy, click Offline ready and choose Save again (get the newest version) or Remove the offline copy. What the button says Button Means Save for offline No offline copy in this browser yet. Click to save one \"Saving n of m\" Saving; keep the tab open until it finishes Offline ready Saved. Hover to see when; click for the menu Could not save Some files did not download. Check your connection and click to try again. The last good copy, if any, is kept Offline not available This window cannot keep an offline copy (a private window or a managed computer). See Working offline You are offline. If the button says Offline ready, every kind of file opens as usual. If it does not, only what this tab has already loaded works: a reader that never loaded in this tab cannot start, and opening that kind of file shows an error from the browser in the status line. Do not reload the tab until you are online again. What never needs a connection Once BimCraft is open in the tab, these work offline even without a saved copy: Reading project and family files and checking folders. Picking elements, reading properties and editing IFC values in a model that is already open. Save as IFC and Download CSV: both are downloads made in the tab. What is not available yet Cloud saving. Files are saved to your computer only. Desktop builds. BimCraft runs in the browser only. See Where your files go."},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"bimcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Messages BimCraft shows, what causes them, and what to do, for opening files, folders, 3D models, drawings and edits.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problem","error","not working","cannot open","failed","incomplete","corrupt","slow","crash","blank","missing","message","help"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-files","text":"Opening files","level":2},{"id":"opening-folders","text":"Opening folders","level":2},{"id":"the-ifc-engine-loads-the-first-time","text":"The IFC engine loads the first time","level":2},{"id":"saving-for-offline-use","text":"Saving for offline use","level":2},{"id":"3d-view","text":"3D view","level":2},{"id":"editing-ifc","text":"Editing IFC","level":2},{"id":"drawings","text":"Drawings","level":2},{"id":"still-stuck","text":"Still stuck","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMessages appear in the status line at the bottom of the stage, in red when something failed. Find the message below.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-files\"\u003EOpening files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E is not an IFC, RVT, RFA, DWG or DXF file.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft did not recognize the file. Check the extension and that the file is not empty. PDFs, images and office files do not open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;.ifczip is not supported yet. Save or export the model as a plain .ifc file.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E (also for \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E) Unzip the \u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E (it is an ordinary zip holding an \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E) or export plain IFC.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… is a project file BimCraft does not read yet.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E A \u003Ccode\u003E.pln\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pla\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tpl\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.bpn\u003C/code\u003E file. Export IFC or DWG from it and open that: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pln-project-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pln-project-files\"\u003EOpen .pln and .pla project files\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This IFC file could not be read.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The IFC file is damaged or not really IFC. Open it in a text editor: a real IFC file starts with \u003Ccode\u003EISO-10303-21;\u003C/code\u003E. Export it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This file is incomplete: it is … MB, but its own index points to data at least … MB in. …&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The project or family file was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is &quot;online only&quot; looks the same. A file opened on its own still opens, with this message at the top of the card and &quot;Showing what was recovered; copy the original again for the full model.&quot;: the card shows only the parts whose every byte is in the file and lists the rest under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot in the file\u003C/strong\u003E. Make the file available offline in your cloud drive, or copy the original again, and reopen it. In a folder, BimCraft shows the newest complete backup instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;… The file&#039;s own list of contents is in the missing part too, so nothing in it can be opened.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file was cut off so early that not even its list of contents is there. Nothing can be recovered from this copy. Copy the original again and reopen it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This compound file has no BasicFileInfo stream, so it is not a Revit file.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file is a compound file of another kind (old office documents use the same container). Check you picked the right file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Still working on the last one…&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft is still busy with the previous file or folder. Wait for the status line to finish, then try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe file chooser greys out my file.\u003C/strong\u003E The chooser only offers \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rvt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rfa\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rte\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.rft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E. Drag other files onto the page instead, and BimCraft will tell you whether it can read them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-folders\"\u003EOpening folders\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This folder has more than 20,000 files. Pick a smaller folder, such as one project.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You picked a folder too high up, such as a whole drive. Pick one project folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The browser did not give access to \u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E. Choose it again with Open a folder.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You clicked \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReopen\u003C/span\u003E on a recent folder and the browser&#039;s permission was refused or has lapsed. Choose the folder again, or drag it in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe browser says it will &quot;upload&quot; files.\u003C/strong\u003E That is the browser&#039;s own wording for handing files to a page. BimCraft does not send them anywhere. Drag the folder in to avoid the question.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing happens on a phone or tablet.\u003C/strong\u003E Many mobile browsers cannot choose folders. Open single files, or use a computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-ifc-engine-loads-the-first-time\"\u003EThe IFC engine loads the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe status line stays on &quot;Starting the IFC engine…&quot;, or shows an error such as &quot;Failed to fetch&quot;.\u003C/strong\u003E The IFC engine could not be downloaded. You may be offline, or a firewall or content blocker is stopping it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline. If the app bar&#039;s button does not say \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E, the IFC engine was never saved in this browser. Reconnect, open the file again, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E so it works offline next time. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/online-offline\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/online-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:online\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOnline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReload the page and try again. On a work network, ask your IT team to allow the site&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003E.wasm\u003C/code\u003E files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving-for-offline-use\"\u003ESaving for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe button says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECould not save\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Some files did not download, usually because the connection dropped. Check your connection and click the button again. Any earlier offline copy is kept until a new one is complete.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe button says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline not available\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E This window cannot keep an offline copy, for example a private window or a managed work computer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe button stays on &quot;Saving \u003Cem\u003En\u003C/em\u003E of \u003Cem\u003Em\u003C/em\u003E&quot; for a while.\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft&#039;s copy is about 11 MB, most of it the IFC engine. Keep the tab open until it says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBimCraft opened offline but looks out of date.\u003C/strong\u003E Your saved copy is from an earlier visit. When you are online, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffline ready\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-view\"\u003E3D view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe model is not where I expected, or it is tiny.\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit\u003C/span\u003E. Fit always frames everything in the model, including storeys you have unticked and stray elements far from the building, so the building can look small. Zoom in from there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClicking does not pick anything.\u003C/strong\u003E Hold the mouse still while you click: a small drag orbits. Elements on unticked storeys cannot be picked. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E on, the see-through spaces are picked before what is inside them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdges\u003C/span\u003E button is greyed out: &quot;Edges are off for very large models&quot;.\u003C/strong\u003E The model has more than 1.5 million triangles. Edges are left off to save memory.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe tab turns slow, white, or shows the browser&#039;s crash page.\u003C/strong\u003E The model is too large for the memory the browser gives the tab. Close other tabs and reload. Export a smaller IFC: one building, one discipline or fewer storeys. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/platforms\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/platforms\"\u003EBrowsers, devices and memory\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe 3D view stays blank.\u003C/strong\u003E WebGL may be turned off or blocked. In the browser&#039;s settings, turn hardware acceleration (or graphics acceleration) on and restart the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing-ifc\"\u003EEditing IFC\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Use a number.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The value is numeric. Type a plain number without units, such as \u003Ccode\u003E2.4\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Use a whole number.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The value is a count or integer. Remove the decimals.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Only single-value properties can be edited.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Lists, enumerations and quantities are read-only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens the browser&#039;s &quot;Save page&quot; window.\u003C/strong\u003E There are no unsaved changes, so BimCraft left the key to the browser. Close the window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E It works only while you have unsaved changes and are not typing in a field. After a save, there is nothing left to undo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanges\u003C/span\u003E list still shows edits after I discarded them and opened a drawing or a folder.\u003C/strong\u003E When you answer the &quot;Discard them?&quot; question with OK and then open something other than an IFC model, the old edits are kept in memory and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC\u003C/span\u003E would still save them. To really start fresh, open the original IFC again, or reload the page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMy edit changed more elements than I picked.\u003C/strong\u003E You changed a property in a set marked &quot;(type)&quot;. Type properties apply to every element of the type. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/type-vs-instance\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/type-vs-instance\"\u003EChange a type property for every element of a type\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a format the reader does not handle yet (…).&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Save the drawing from the program that made it as an older DWG (2018 or earlier) or as DXF, and try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This drawing has nothing to show.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;\u003Cem\u003Ename\u003C/em\u003E is an empty drawing: it holds no lines, text or blocks.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing is empty, or holds only things BimCraft does not draw yet (see \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E on the right).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E BimCraft stopped drawing to stay responsive. Turn off layers you do not need, or open the sheets you need from a smaller drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParts of a sheet are missing.\u003C/strong\u003E Linked drawings may be missing: look under \u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/strong\u003E on the right. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-linked-files\"\u003EFind linked drawings and pictures\u003C/a\u003E. Layers may also be off: click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll on\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA picture shows as an empty frame.\u003C/strong\u003E Its file was not found, or it is a TIFF (&quot;TIFF cannot be shown in a browser&quot;). Add a PNG or JPEG copy with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd pictures…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScrolling on my trackpad zooms instead of moving the drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E That is how drawings work in BimCraft. Click and drag to pan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-stuck\"\u003EStill stuck\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERead \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E: the thing you are looking for may not exist in this build.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Messages appear in the status line at the bottom of the stage, in red when something failed. Find the message below. Opening files \"name is not an IFC, RVT, RFA, DWG or DXF file.\" BimCraft did not recognize the file. Check the extension and that the file is not empty. PDFs, images and office files do not open. \".ifczip is not supported yet. Save or export the model as a plain .ifc file.\" (also for .ifcxml) Unzip the .ifczip (it is an ordinary zip holding an .ifc) or export plain IFC. \"… is a project file BimCraft does not read yet.\" A .pln, .pla, .tpl or .bpn file. Export IFC or DWG from it and open that: see Open .pln and .pla project files. \"This IFC file could not be read.\" The IFC file is damaged or not really IFC. Open it in a text editor: a real IFC file starts with ISO-10303-21;. Export it again. \"This file is incomplete: it is … MB, but its own index points to data at least … MB in. …\" The project or family file was cut off while being copied, uploaded or synced. A cloud-drive file that is \"online only\" looks the same. A file opened on its own still opens, with this message at the top of the card and \"Showing what was recovered; copy the original again for the full model.\": the card shows only the parts whose every byte is in the file and lists the rest under Not in the file. Make the file available offline in your cloud drive, or copy the original again, and reopen it. In a folder, BimCraft shows the newest complete backup instead. \"… The file's own list of contents is in the missing part too, so nothing in it can be opened.\" The file was cut off so early that not even its list of contents is there. Nothing can be recovered from this copy. Copy the original again and reopen it. \"This compound file has no BasicFileInfo stream, so it is not a Revit file.\" The file is a compound file of another kind (old office documents use the same container). Check you picked the right file. \"Still working on the last one…\" BimCraft is still busy with the previous file or folder. Wait for the status line to finish, then try again. The file chooser greys out my file. The chooser only offers .ifc, .rvt, .rfa, .rte, .rft, .dwg and .dxf. Drag other files onto the page instead, and BimCraft will tell you whether it can read them. Opening folders \"This folder has more than 20,000 files. Pick a smaller folder, such as one project.\" You picked a folder too high up, such as a whole drive. Pick one project folder. \"The browser did not give access to name. Choose it again with Open a folder.\" You clicked Reopen on a recent folder and the browser's permission was refused or has lapsed. Choose the folder again, or drag it in. The browser says it will \"upload\" files. That is the browser's own wording for handing files to a page. BimCraft does not send them anywhere. Drag the folder in to avoid the question. Nothing happens on a phone or tablet. Many mobile browsers cannot choose folders. Open single files, or use a computer. The IFC engine loads the first time The status line stays on \"Starting the IFC engine…\", or shows an error such as \"Failed to fetch\". The IFC engine could not be downloaded. You may be offline, or a firewall or content blocker is stopping it. You are offline. If the app bar's button does not say Offline ready, the IFC engine was never saved in this browser. Reconnect, open the file again, then click Save for offline so it works offline next time. See Online and offline. Reload the page and try again. On a work network, ask your IT team to allow the site's .wasm files. Saving for offline use The button says Could not save. Some files did not download, usually because the connection dropped. Check your connection and click the button again. Any earlier offline copy is kept until a new one is complete. The button says Offline not available. This window cannot keep an offline copy, for example a private window or a managed work computer. See Working offline. The button stays on \"Saving n of m\" for a while. BimCraft's copy is about 11 MB, most of it the IFC engine. Keep the tab open until it says Offline ready. BimCraft opened offline but looks out of date. Your saved copy is from an earlier visit. When you are online, click Offline ready and choose Save again (get the newest version). 3D view The model is not where I expected, or it is tiny. Press F or click Fit. Fit always frames everything in the model, including storeys you have unticked and stray elements far from the building, so the building can look small. Zoom in from there. Clicking does not pick anything. Hold the mouse still while you click: a small drag orbits. Elements on unticked storeys cannot be picked. With Spaces on, the see-through spaces are picked before what is inside them. The Edges button is greyed out: \"Edges are off for very large models\". The model has more than 1.5 million triangles. Edges are left off to save memory. The tab turns slow, white, or shows the browser's crash page. The model is too large for the memory the browser gives the tab. Close other tabs and reload. Export a smaller IFC: one building, one discipline or fewer storeys. See Browsers, devices and memory. The 3D view stays blank. WebGL may be turned off or blocked. In the browser's settings, turn hardware acceleration (or graphics acceleration) on and restart the browser. Editing IFC \"Use a number.\" The value is numeric. Type a plain number without units, such as 2.4. \"Use a whole number.\" The value is a count or integer. Remove the decimals. \"Only single-value properties can be edited.\" Lists, enumerations and quantities are read-only. Ctrl+S opens the browser's \"Save page\" window. There are no unsaved changes, so BimCraft left the key to the browser. Close the window. Ctrl+Z does nothing. It works only while you have unsaved changes and are not typing in a field. After a save, there is nothing left to undo. The Changes list still shows edits after I discarded them and opened a drawing or a folder. When you answer the \"Discard them?\" question with OK and then open something other than an IFC model, the old edits are kept in memory and Save as IFC would still save them. To really start fresh, open the original IFC again, or reload the page. My edit changed more elements than I picked. You changed a property in a set marked \"(type)\". Type properties apply to every element of the type. Click Undo. See Change a type property for every element of a type. Drawings \"name could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a format the reader does not handle yet (…).\" Save the drawing from the program that made it as an older DWG (2018 or earlier) or as DXF, and try again. \"This drawing has nothing to show.\" or \"name is an empty drawing: it holds no lines, text or blocks.\" The drawing is empty, or holds only things BimCraft does not draw yet (see Not drawn yet on the right). \"This drawing is very large; only part of it is shown.\" BimCraft stopped drawing to stay responsive. Turn off layers you do not need, or open the sheets you need from a smaller drawing. Parts of a sheet are missing. Linked drawings may be missing: look under Linked drawings on the right. See Find linked drawings and pictures. Layers may also be off: click All on. A picture shows as an empty frame. Its file was not found, or it is a TIFF (\"TIFF cannot be shown in a browser\"). Add a PNG or JPEG copy with Add pictures…. Scrolling on my trackpad zooms instead of moving the drawing. That is how drawings work in BimCraft. Click and drag to pan. Still stuck Read What is not built yet: the thing you are looking for may not exist in this build."},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"bimcraft","title":"What is not built yet","summary":"What this BimCraft build does not do yet, and what to use instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["missing","limits","limitations","not supported","roadmap","authoring","modeling","sections","measure","clash","bcf","cloud","desktop","export"],"headings":[{"id":"modeling-and-authoring","text":"Modeling and authoring","level":2},{"id":"viewing-models","text":"Viewing models","level":2},{"id":"drawings","text":"Drawings","level":2},{"id":"files-and-platforms","text":"Files and platforms","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft today is a reader, checker and IFC data editor. This is the honest list of what it does not do yet, with what to do instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modeling-and-authoring\"\u003EModeling and authoring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing or placing walls, doors, floors or any element\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse your authoring program, then open its IFC export here\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging an element&#039;s shape or size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour authoring program. Delete, copy, cut and paste work here: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/edit-elements\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/edit-elements\"\u003EDelete, cut, copy and paste building elements\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDeleting storeys, spaces, buildings or the site\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the elements in them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPasting building elements into another model\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy and paste within one model; placed library items paste into any model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdding new properties or property sets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFill in existing values here, or add the parameter in your authoring program and export again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing quantities, lists, enumerations\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour authoring program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving project or family files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft saves IFC only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFamilies: editing or creating\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour authoring program\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"viewing-models\"\u003EViewing models\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3D from project files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport IFC and open it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/rvt-to-ifc\"\u003EGet a project file into 3D through IFC\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFloor plans, elevations, several section planes at once\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESection\u003C/span\u003E plane or a section box at a time; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/section-plane\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/section-plane\"\u003ECut an IFC model with a section plane\u003C/a\u003E. Untick storeys in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStoreys\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESharing search sets with others, sets inside the IFC file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESets stay in this browser; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/find\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/find\"\u003EFind elements by class, name or property\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasuring angles, or along a path of several points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeasure each part with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/measure\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/measure\"\u003EMeasure distances and face areas in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaved views, shadows\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave a view as an issue: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/issues-bcf\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/issues-bcf\"\u003EIssues and BCF files\u003C/a\u003E. Walk mode: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/walk\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/walk\"\u003EWalk through a model\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClearance (soft) clashes, clash groups, saving clash results\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClash check\u003C/span\u003E and make issues of the ones that matter: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/clash-check\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/clash-check\"\u003ECheck for clashes\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving several models as one set, or editing two models at once\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit one model at a time; each saves its own file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssigning issues, due dates, labels, several viewpoints per issue, editing an issue\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne view per issue; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/issues-bcf\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/issues-bcf\"\u003EIssues and BCF files\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.ifczip\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.ifcxml\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnzip or export plain \u003Ccode\u003E.ifc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening \u003Ccode\u003E.pln\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pla\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tpl\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.bpn\u003C/code\u003E project files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport IFC and DWG from them: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/pln-project-files\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/pln-project-files\"\u003EOpen .pln and .pla project files\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElements\u003C/span\u003E list to select by kind\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a kind&#039;s group in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel tree\u003C/span\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/model-tree\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/model-tree\"\u003EFind your way with the model tree\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawings\"\u003EDrawings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing, measuring or saving DWG and DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe CadCraft drafting app (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit in CadCraft\u003C/span\u003E on the 2D view)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrinting or exporting PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrint from the program that made the drawing; check it first with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot styles and pen tables\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint view\u003C/span\u003E shows plain black with line weights\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG files older than R13, and binary DXF (BimCraft says so when you open one)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave the drawing again as a newer DWG, or as ASCII DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawings linked inside linked drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen them directly\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETIFF pictures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave a PNG or JPEG copy with the same name and add it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDGN drawings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EListed in folder reports only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects listed under \u003Cstrong\u003ENot drawn yet\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EView the drawing in the program that made it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EACIS solids, bodies, regions and surfaces, and subdivision meshes in 3D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport IFC or 3D faces from the program that made the drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPictures, multileaders and sheets in the 3D view of a drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee them on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab and the sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicking objects in a drawing&#039;s 2D view\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESwitch to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E and click the object there: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\" data-topic=\"bimcraft/dwg-in-3d\"\u003ESee a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment schedules from drawings without \u003Ccode\u003EKCL-ITEM\u003C/code\u003E tags\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment schedule as XLSX or PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E and open it in a spreadsheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-and-platforms\"\u003EFiles and platforms\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave copies on your computer or office server. Cloud saving is not available yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDesktop builds\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse BimCraft in a browser. Desktop builds are not available yet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemembering folders in Firefox and Safari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose the folder again, or drag it in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo after saving\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the original file again to start over\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFilling in the missing part of a cut-off project file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the original again; in a folder, BimCraft reads the newest complete backup\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReopening the files you had open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnsaved changes are offered back as &quot;Recover your work?&quot; when you return; open the files again from the recent list\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"BimCraft today is a reader, checker and IFC data editor. This is the honest list of what it does not do yet, with what to do instead. Modeling and authoring Not yet Instead, today Drawing or placing walls, doors, floors or any element Use your authoring program, then open its IFC export here Changing an element's shape or size Your authoring program. Delete, copy, cut and paste work here: see Delete, cut, copy and paste building elements Deleting storeys, spaces, buildings or the site Delete the elements in them Pasting building elements into another model Copy and paste within one model; placed library items paste into any model Adding new properties or property sets Fill in existing values here, or add the parameter in your authoring program and export again Editing quantities, lists, enumerations Your authoring program Saving project or family files BimCraft saves IFC only Families: editing or creating Your authoring program Viewing models Not yet Instead, today 3D from project files Export IFC and open it. See Get a project file into 3D through IFC Floor plans, elevations, several section planes at once One Section plane or a section box at a time; see Cut an IFC model with a section plane. Untick storeys in Storeys Sharing search sets with others, sets inside the IFC file Sets stay in this browser; see Find elements by class, name or property Measuring angles, or along a path of several points Measure each part with Measure: see Measure distances and face areas in 3D Saved views, shadows Save a view as an issue: see Issues and BCF files. Walk mode: see Walk through a model Clearance (soft) clashes, clash groups, saving clash results Run Clash check and make issues of the ones that matter: see Check for clashes Saving several models as one set, or editing two models at once Edit one model at a time; each saves its own file Assigning issues, due dates, labels, several viewpoints per issue, editing an issue One view per issue; see Issues and BCF files .ifczip and .ifcxml Unzip or export plain .ifc Opening .pln, .pla, .tpl and .bpn project files Export IFC and DWG from them: see Open .pln and .pla project files Opening the Elements list to select by kind Click a kind's group in the Model tree: see Find your way with the model tree Drawings Not yet Instead, today Editing, measuring or saving DWG and DXF The CadCraft drafting app (Edit in CadCraft on the 2D view) Printing or exporting PDF Print from the program that made the drawing; check it first with Print view Plot styles and pen tables Print view shows plain black with line weights DWG files older than R13, and binary DXF (BimCraft says so when you open one) Save the drawing again as a newer DWG, or as ASCII DXF Drawings linked inside linked drawings Open them directly TIFF pictures Save a PNG or JPEG copy with the same name and add it DGN drawings Listed in folder reports only Objects listed under Not drawn yet View the drawing in the program that made it ACIS solids, bodies, regions and surfaces, and subdivision meshes in 3D Export IFC or 3D faces from the program that made the drawing Pictures, multileaders and sheets in the 3D view of a drawing See them on the Model tab and the sheets Picking objects in a drawing's 2D view Switch to 3D and click the object there: see See a DWG or DXF drawing in 3D Equipment schedules from drawings without KCL-ITEM tags Not available Equipment schedule as XLSX or PDF Download CSV and open it in a spreadsheet Files and platforms Not yet Instead, today Cloud saving Save copies on your computer or office server. Cloud saving is not available yet Desktop builds Use BimCraft in a browser. Desktop builds are not available yet Remembering folders in Firefox and Safari Choose the folder again, or drag it in Undo after saving Open the original file again to start over Filling in the missing part of a cut-off project file Copy the original again; in a folder, BimCraft reads the newest complete backup Reopening the files you had open Unsaved changes are offered back as \"Recover your work?\" when you return; open the files again from the recent list"},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Using this help","summary":"Open the help, search it, and see the steps for your computer.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["help","f1","search","tutorials","manual"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-help","text":"Open the help","level":2},{"id":"find-a-topic","text":"Find a topic","level":2},{"id":"steps-for-your-computer","text":"Steps for your computer","level":2},{"id":"the-same-help-on-the-website","text":"The same help on the website","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-help\"\u003EOpen the help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top of the app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E together with the \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E key (or the globe key), or use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EKeys on a Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-a-topic\"\u003EFind a topic\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs&#039; command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETopics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E returns to where you were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-for-your-computer\"\u003ESteps for your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example &quot;Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChange\u003C/span\u003E on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E to see every version of every step, each labeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-same-help-on-the-website\"\u003EThe same help on the website\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform&#039;s steps. In the app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running. Open the help Press F1, or choose Help in the bar at the top of the app. Press Esc or choose Close to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was. On most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press F1 together with the fn key (or the globe key), or use the Help button. See Keys on a Mac. Find a topic Type in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs' command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page. Topics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting. Links inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. Back returns to where you were. Steps for your computer Some steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example \"Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online\". Choose Change on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague. Choose Show all to see every version of every step, each labeled. Keyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: Ctrl+S reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux. The same help on the website The website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform's steps. In the app, Open on the website at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online."},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Browsers and computers","summary":"Which browsers the apps run in, what differs between them, and what your computer needs.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","browser","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","webgl","requirements","memory"],"headings":[{"id":"which-browser","text":"Which browser","level":2},{"id":"what-differs-between-browsers","text":"What differs between browsers","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-and-linux","text":"Mac, Windows and Linux","level":2},{"id":"tablets-and-touch-screens","text":"Tablets and touch screens","level":2},{"id":"what-your-computer-needs","text":"What your computer needs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-browser\"\u003EWhich browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an up-to-date version of one of these:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEngine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EWhat differs between browsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a folder.\u003C/strong\u003E Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving.\u003C/strong\u003E The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Chrome or Edge, turn on &quot;Ask where to save each file before downloading&quot; (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose &quot;Always ask you where to save files&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari, open Settings, then General, and set &quot;File download location&quot; to &quot;Ask for each download&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-and-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows and Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tablets-and-touch-screens\"\u003ETablets and touch screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app&#039;s help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-your-computer-needs\"\u003EWhat your computer needs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVery large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see Where your files go). Which browser Use an up-to-date version of one of these: Browser Engine Notes Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium The most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling. Firefox Gecko Works. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows. Safari WebKit Works on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks. All three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser. What differs between browsers Opening a folder. Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three. Saving. The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first. In Chrome or Edge, turn on \"Ask where to save each file before downloading\" (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time. In Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose \"Always ask you where to save files\" to pick a folder every time. In Safari, open Settings, then General, and set \"File download location\" to \"Ask for each download\" to pick a folder every time. Mac, Windows and Linux The apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Tablets and touch screens Some apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app's help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands. What your computer needs These are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements: 8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models). A graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser's settings. A screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them. Very large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down."},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Keys, mice and trackpads","summary":"How shortcuts are written, function keys on a Mac, right-clicking and panning without a mouse.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcut","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","function keys","trackpad","right click","middle button","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"how-shortcuts-are-written","text":"How shortcuts are written","level":2},{"id":"function-keys-on-a-mac","text":"Function keys on a Mac","level":2},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps","text":"Shortcuts the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad","text":"Mouse buttons on a trackpad","level":2},{"id":"typing-in-a-text-box","text":"Typing in a text box","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"how-shortcuts-are-written\"\u003EHow shortcuts are written\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWritten\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn a Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn Windows and Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe Delete key\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn and Delete (forward delete)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plus sign means hold the keys together. &quot;Then&quot; means press one after the other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works both ways. A few, such as full screen (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EFunction keys on a Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (or the globe key) while you press the key, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eopen \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard Shortcuts\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EFunction Keys\u003C/strong\u003E, and turn on &quot;Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EShortcuts the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a Mac and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;); choose to stay, then save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser&#039;s own full screen (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, or the green button on the window\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, which can hide the app&#039;s bars too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad\"\u003EMouse buttons on a trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click opens the browser&#039;s menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-in-a-text-box\"\u003ETyping in a text box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app&#039;s one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing &quot;L&quot; types an L. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"How shortcuts are written The help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it. Written On a Mac On Windows and Linux Ctrl+S Command (⌘) and S Ctrl and S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F Ctrl, Shift and F Alt+← Option (⌥) and ← Alt and ← Backspace the Delete key Backspace Delete fn and Delete (forward delete) Delete Enter Return Enter A plus sign means hold the keys together. \"Then\" means press one after the other. Most app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so Ctrl+Z works both ways. A few, such as full screen (Ctrl+Shift+F), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies. Function keys on a Mac On most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12: hold fn (or the globe key) while you press the key, or open System Settings, then Keyboard, then Keyboard Shortcuts, then Function Keys, and turn on \"Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys\". Some F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead. Shortcuts the browser keeps A web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T and Ctrl+W always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead. Ctrl+Q on a Mac and Alt+F4 on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"); choose to stay, then save. The browser's own full screen (Control-Command-F, or the green button on the window / F11) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on Ctrl+Shift+F, which can hide the app's bars too. Mouse buttons on a trackpad The apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse: Right-click: click with two fingers. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding Ctrl while you click opens the browser's menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it. Right-click: click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps. Typing in a text box While you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app's one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing \"L\" types an L. Press Esc or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app."},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Find a tool or setting","summary":"Press Ctrl+K and type a few letters to run any tool, change a setting or open a help topic.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["find","search","command palette","quick open","run command","ctrl k","cmd k","tool","setting","shortcut"],"headings":[{"id":"find-and-run","text":"Find and run","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don&#039;t need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-and-run\"\u003EFind and run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a few letters of what you want: &quot;fillet&quot;, &quot;panel text&quot;, &quot;units&quot;. Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETools that can&#039;t run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd-ons&#039; tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Every app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don't need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar. Find and run Press Ctrl+K, or choose Find in the bar at the top. Type a few letters of what you want: \"fillet\", \"panel text\", \"units\". Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list. Press Enter to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. Esc closes the box. A tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens Settings at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel. Things to know What you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away. Tools that can't run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out. Add-ons' tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on."},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Arrange the panels and use a second monitor","summary":"Dock the side panels left, right or at the bottom, float them over the drawing, or move them to their own window on another screen.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["panels","dock","float","move panel","drag","layout","second monitor","two screens","dual monitor","multiple monitors","window","pop-up","reset layout"],"headings":[{"id":"move-a-panel-in-the-window","text":"Move a panel in the window","level":2},{"id":"use-the-keyboard","text":"Use the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor","text":"Put the panels on a second monitor","level":2},{"id":"more-than-one-panels-window","text":"More than one panels window","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-back","text":"Put the panels back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel&#039;s name and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-panel-in-the-window\"\u003EMove a panel in the window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the panel by its name at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger on the panel&#039;s name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-the-keyboard\"\u003EUse the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button opens a menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the left\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock at the bottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloat over the drawing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it back\u003C/span\u003E. Tab to the button, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, move with the arrow keys and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor\"\u003EPut the panels on a second monitor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E on a panel, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove all panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E. You can also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-than-one-panels-window\"\u003EMore than one panels window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E opens another window for that panel, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to panels window 2\u003C/span\u003E (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called &quot;panels 2&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA panels window that has no panels left closes by itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the next one\u003C/span\u003E once for each window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-back\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClosing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/span\u003E to bring the window back, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep them here\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E puts every panel back where it started.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one. This works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel's name and a ⋯ button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next. Move a panel in the window Drag the panel by its name at the top. While you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens. Drop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float. Both panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back. A floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was. Drag with one finger on the panel's name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the ⋯ menu. Use the keyboard Every panel's ⋯ button opens a menu: Dock on the left, Dock on the right, Dock at the bottom, Float over the drawing, Move to a new window and Put it back. Tab to the button, press Enter, move with the arrow keys and press Enter again. Esc closes the menu. Put the panels on a second monitor Choose ⋯ on a panel, then Move all panels to a new window. You can also choose Move panels to a new window in Settings. A small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like. Keep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window. Shortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window. Chrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose Put it on my other screen in the panels window (or Put the panels on my other screen in a panel's ⋯ menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time. Drag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time. More than one panels window Each panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor. In a panel's ⋯ menu, Move to a new window opens another window for that panel, and Move to panels window 2 (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called \"panels 2\". Drag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was. A panels window that has no panels left closes by itself. After a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose Open the next one once for each window. Put the panels back Choose Put the panels back in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were. Closing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose Open it to bring the window back, or Keep them here. Settings › General › Reset layout puts every panel back where it started. If nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again. The layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings."},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Working offline","summary":"Save an app for offline use, what works with no connection, and what needs one.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["offline","no internet","airplane","site visit","cache","service worker","install","download","connection"],"headings":[{"id":"save-an-app-for-offline-use","text":"Save an app for offline use","level":2},{"id":"what-works-offline","text":"What works offline","level":2},{"id":"what-needs-a-connection","text":"What needs a connection","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app&#039;s files saved first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-an-app-for-offline-use\"\u003ESave an app for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the app while you are online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for &quot;Offline ready&quot;. The app&#039;s files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDo this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don&#039;t reload it until you are online again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-works-offline\"\u003EWhat works offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis help, including search.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-needs-a-connection\"\u003EWhat needs a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGetting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E refreshes the saved copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks to the website, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E and links to other pages.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser&#039;s site data removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDesktop builds, which will not need a browser, are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app's files saved first. Save an app for offline use Open the app while you are online. Choose Save for offline in the bar at the top. Wait for \"Offline ready\". The app's files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time. Do this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it. You are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don't reload it until you are online again. What works offline Opening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking. Saving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer. This help, including search. What needs a connection Opening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use. Getting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and Save for offline refreshes the saved copy. Links to the website, such as Open on the website and links to other pages. Cloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet. Things to know A saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser's site data removes it. Private windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save. Safari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip. Desktop builds, which will not need a browser, are not available yet."},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Where your files go","summary":"Files stay on your computer, how saving works, and what the browser remembers.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["privacy","upload","cloud","save","download","storage","local","security","confidential"],"headings":[{"id":"your-files-stay-on-your-computer","text":"Your files stay on your computer","level":2},{"id":"how-saving-works","text":"How saving works","level":2},{"id":"what-the-browser-remembers","text":"What the browser remembers","level":2},{"id":"cloud-saving","text":"Cloud saving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-stay-on-your-computer\"\u003EYour files stay on your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. \u003Cstrong\u003EThe file is not uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E We never see it, and it works the same with no connection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-saving-works\"\u003EHow saving works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by \u003Cstrong\u003Edownloading\u003C/strong\u003E a new file:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as &quot;(edited)&quot; or &quot;(marked up)&quot;, so your original stays untouched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-browser-remembers\"\u003EWhat the browser remembers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app&#039;s help says what it keeps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cloud-saving\"\u003ECloud saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Your files stay on your computer When you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. The file is not uploaded. We never see it, and it works the same with no connection. How saving works A browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by downloading a new file: The download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see Browsers and computers). Apps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as \"(edited)\" or \"(marked up)\", so your original stays untouched. If you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"). What the browser remembers Some apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app's help says what it keeps. Cloud saving Cloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way."},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does."}]};
